{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1793742362568,"sessionID":"ses_e5ca055c6ffeOZO66b0AKuGGW6","part":{"partID":"prt_1a3605770001H6oz0p7AuFXBtt","sessionID":"ses_e5ca055c6ffeOZO66b0AKuGGW6","messageID":"msg_1a3604dff0013p31ZrmzqFjrcK","type":"tool","id":"call_00_mua7enx6j7e4gh66o8113cxq","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":".skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-skill/assets/skill/skill-reference-template.md","offset":54,"limit":180},"output":"Read file .skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-skill/assets/skill/skill-reference-template.md, lines 54-233\n54: ## 2. DOCUMENT STRUCTURE\n55: \n56: ### Document Structure: Title and Intro\n57: \n58: **CRITICAL: The intro after the H1 title must be 1-2 SHORT sentences only.**\n59: \n60: ```markdown\n61: ---\n62: title: [Title]\n63: description: [One-line description - same as intro]\n64: trigger_phrases:\n65:   - \"[distinctive phrase one]\"\n66:   - \"[distinctive phrase two]\"\n67:   - \"[distinctive phrase three]\"\n68: importance_tier: normal\n69: contextType: general\n70: version: 1.0.0.0\n71: ---\n72: \n73: # [Title]\n74: \n75: [1-2 SHORT sentences only. Brief hook/summary. NO subsections, NO headers here.]\n76: \n77: ---\n78: \n79: \\## 1. OVERVIEW\n80: [All detailed content goes here, NOT in the intro]\n81: \n82: ```\n83: \n84: Every reference file carries this full 5-field frontmatter block: the Skill Advisor harvests it as routing signal (3-8 lowercase multi-word `trigger_phrases` drawn from the doc's content. `importance_tier` defaults to `normal`, with `important` only for formal contract docs. `contextType` is one of `planning|research|implementation|general`). See [frontmatter-templates.md](../../../sk-create-frontmatter/assets/frontmatter-templates.md) for field rules.\n85: \n86: > **WARNING: DO NOT duplicate content between intro and Section 1.**\n87: > The intro is a hook. Section 1 provides the details.\n88: \n89: #### GOOD Intro Structure\n90: \n91: ```markdown\n92: # Git Commit Workflows\n93: \n94: Step-by-step workflows for creating clean, atomic commits with proper filtering.\n95: \n96: ---\n97: \n98: \\## 1. OVERVIEW\n99: \n100: ### Purpose\n101: Provides systematic workflows for analyzing changes, filtering artifacts, and creating well-structured commits.\n102: \n103: ### When to Use\n104: - Creating commits with multiple file changes\n105: - Filtering generated files from commits\n106: - Writing clear commit messages\n107: \n108: ### Core Principle\n109: Atomic commits with clear intent + filtered artifacts = maintainable Git history.\n110: ```\n111: \n112: #### BAD Intro Structure (duplicates content)\n113: \n114: ```markdown\n115: # Git Commit Workflows\n116: \n117: Step-by-step workflows for creating clean, atomic commits with proper filtering.\n118: \n119: ### Core Principle   ← WRONG: No headers in intro!\n120: Atomic commits with clear intent + filtered artifacts = maintainable Git history.\n121: \n122: ### When to Use      ← WRONG: This belongs in Section 1!\n123: - Creating commits with multiple file changes\n124: \n125: ---\n126: \n127: \\## 1. OVERVIEW\n128: \n129: ### Purpose\n130: Provides systematic workflows...  ← Now this duplicates the intro!\n131: \n132: ### Core Principle\n133: Atomic commits...  ← DUPLICATED from intro!\n134: ```\n135: \n136: #### Intro vs Section 1 Content Placement\n137: \n138: | Content Type          | Intro (after H1) | Section 1 OVERVIEW |\n139: | --------------------- | ---------------- | ------------------ |\n140: | 1-2 sentence summary  | ✅ YES            | ❌ NO (don't repeat) |\n141: | Core Principle        | ❌ NO             | ✅ YES              |\n142: | When to Use           | ❌ NO             | ✅ YES              |\n143: | Primary Sources       | ❌ NO             | ✅ YES (as \"Key Sources\") |\n144: | Purpose (detailed)    | ❌ NO             | ✅ YES              |\n145: | Prerequisites         | ❌ NO             | ✅ YES              |\n146: | Any H3 subsections    | ❌ NEVER          | ✅ YES              |\n147: \n148: ---\n149: \n150: ### What Are AI Agent Skill Reference Files?\n151: \n152: Reference files are detailed technical documentation in the `references/` directory that provide deep-dive guidance with **explicit reasoning** - making thought processes visible, showing decision logic, and analyzing root causes.\n153: \n154: **Core Purpose**:\n155: - **Technical depth** - Step-by-step reasoning with rationale\n156: - **Phased workflows** - Structured processes with validation checkpoints\n157: - **Pattern libraries** - Before/after examples, comparison tables\n158: - **Tool integration** - MCP tools, scripts, asset references\n159: - **Root cause analysis** - Systematic debugging and troubleshooting\n160: \n161: **Key Difference from Generic Documentation**:\n162: - AI agent skills = Actionable workflows with explicit reasoning\n163: - Generic docs = Information reference without decision logic\n164: \n165: ### Progressive Disclosure in Skills\n166: \n167: ```\n168: Level 1: Metadata (name + description)\n169:          └─ Always in context (~100 words)\n170:             ↓\n171: Level 2: SKILL.md body\n172:          └─ When skill triggers (<5k words)\n173:             ↓\n174: Level 3: Reference files (this document)\n175:          └─ Loaded as needed (500-3000 lines per file)\n176: ```\n177: \n178: **Reference files = Level 3**: Deep technical guidance loaded only when the agent needs specific workflow details.\n179: \n180: ---\n181: \n182: ## 3. WHEN TO CREATE REFERENCE FILES\n183: \n184: ### Create Reference File When\n185: \n186: **Workflow Complexity**:\n187: - Multi-phase workflows with validation checkpoints\n188: - Decision trees with multiple branches\n189: - Pattern libraries with 5+ variations\n190: - Systematic debugging procedures\n191: \n192: **Technical Depth**:\n193: - Root cause analysis required\n194: - Tool integration details (MCP, scripts)\n195: - Before/after code transformations\n196: - Common error patterns and fixes\n197: \n198: **Size Threshold**:\n199: - Content exceeds 200 lines\n200: - Multiple related workflows\n201: - Extensive troubleshooting needed\n202: \n203: ### Keep in SKILL.md When\n204: \n205: **Core Content**:\n206: - Overview and \"When to Use\"\n207: - High-level workflow (3-5 steps)\n208: - Essential rules (ALWAYS/NEVER/ESCALATE IF)\n209:   > **Note**: RULES sections in SKILL.md use ALL-CAPS H3 headers: `### ALWAYS`, `### NEVER`, `### ESCALATE IF` (or `####` at phase depth). See [skill-md-template.md](./skill-md-template.md) for the complete pattern.\n210: - Quick reference\n211: \n212: **Size**: Content under 200 lines\n213: \n214: ### Reference File Types for AI Agent Skills\n215: \n216: | Type                | Purpose                                | Example (kebab-case)          | Size           |\n217: | ------------------- | -------------------------------------- | ----------------------------- | -------------- |\n218: | **Workflow**        | Multi-phase processes with checkpoints | `implementation-workflows.md` | 500-1500 lines |\n219: | **Pattern Library** | Code patterns with before/after        | `shared-patterns.md`          | 300-800 lines  |\n220: | **Standards**       | Rules and conventions                  | `code-quality-standards.md`   | 400-1000 lines |\n221: | **Debugging**       | Systematic troubleshooting             | `debugging-workflows.md`      | 600-1200 lines |\n222: | **Tool Guide**      | MCP/external tool integration          | `devtools-guide.md`           | 200-600 lines  |\n223: | **Quick Reference** | Commands, shortcuts, checklists        | `quick-reference.md`          | 200-400 lines  |\n224: \n225: **Note:** All authored reference file names use kebab-case (hyphens, lowercase).\n226: \n227: ---\n228: \n229: ## 4. AI AGENT SKILL REFERENCE CHARACTERISTICS\n230: \n231: ### Core Principle Statements\n232: \n233: **Purpose**: Distill philosophy into one-line wisdom\n[Output truncated. Continue reading with offset: 234]","title":"read","metadata":{