{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1793742415306,"sessionID":"ses_e5ca055c6ffeOZO66b0AKuGGW6","part":{"id":"prt_1a36125ca001om6sGJYjAafyat","sessionID":"ses_e5ca055c6ffeOZO66b0AKuGGW6","messageID":"msg_1a36120b5001R7cA9WN4yiWzko","type":"step-start","snapshot":"6504f9c1e81d9350a211efdb0fcace5ea0e4418b"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1793742420311,"sessionID":"ses_e5ca055c6ffeOZO66b0AKuGGW6","part":{"partID":"prt_1a361390e00176ni7lyZMR21r5","sessionID":"ses_e5ca055c6ffeOZO66b0AKuGGW6","messageID":"msg_1a36120b5001R7cA9WN4yiWzko","type":"tool","id":"call_01_jiebq3rj7334rzronoj38fo0","tool":"shell","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"grep -rn --include='*.md' -E '^\\*\\*[^*]{2,90}\\*\\*' .skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-*/references .skilled/skills/sk-code/shared/references | head -40"},"output":".skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-ci/references/trigger-and-filter-rules.md:52:**The rule.** A rebuild commit carries `[skip ci]` in its message body. GitHub then starts no push-triggered or pull-request-triggered workflow for that commit, and the downstream workflows stay quiet without a single filter edit. The commit also ends with a trailer the profile names as `rebuildTrailer`, so the report can skip it when it looks for the newest commit worth reading.\n.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-ci/references/trigger-and-filter-rules.md:60:**The alternative.** A push-only `paths-ignore` that lists the eight twin entries on each downstream workflow does the same job, at the price of 104 twin-matched entries across 13 files and an amended ordering rule. Use it only if the skip token proves unreliable.\n.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-ci/references/trigger-and-filter-rules.md:62:**What it saves.** Skipping the downstream workflows on the four generated paths saves about 2,073 runner-min/day, roughly 54% of the repository's daily total. The push-only filter form saves about 2,002 runner-min/day when `message-contract.yml` is left unfiltered.\n.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-ci/references/trigger-and-filter-rules.md:64:**Reading statuses.** A skipped rebuild commit leaves no runs on the branch tip. Any tool that reads statuses on the tip must resolve the newest commit that is not a rebuild commit.\n.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-ci/references/job-shape-rules.md:34:**Formula.** `max(<floor>, ceil(3 x job p50 minutes), ceil(2 x job p100 minutes))` with a floor of 10 minutes. Pull at least 40 recent runs of job-level timings (`gh run view --json jobs`), compute p50 and p100 per job and state the observed p100 next to the chosen value in the commit or the workflow comment. A successful job in the sample should never exceed half of its new timeout.\n.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-ci/references/job-shape-rules.md:36:**Today.** `44 of 44` jobs in the 32 workflows carry a timeout, on one job-level line each. `35` of them sit at the 10 minute floor. The other **9 jobs** sit above it.\n.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-frontend/references/web-markup-and-css.md:58:**Deeper:** [Elements and attributes](web-elements-and-attributes.md) and [accessibility patterns](web-accessibility-patterns.md).\n.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-frontend/references/web-markup-and-css.md:73:**The `autocomplete` disagreement.** `interaction-craft.md` Section 2 advises disabling `autocomplete` on most inputs. That holds for free-text fields where browser history adds noise, such as comments, in-app search filters and admin entry of someone else's data. It does not hold for fields about the user. Identity, contact, address, payment and credential fields need the right token so autofill and password managers work and the input purpose is machine-readable:\n.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-frontend/references/web-markup-and-css.md:85:**Deeper:** [Elements and attributes](web-elements-and-attributes.md).\n.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-frontend/references/web-markup-and-css.md:101:**Deeper:** [Layout and sizing](web-layout-and-sizing.md) and [internationalization](web-internationalization.md).\n.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-frontend/references/web-markup-and-css.md:117:**Deeper:** [Layout and sizing](web-layout-and-sizing.md) and [accessibility patterns](web-accessibility-patterns.md).\n.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-frontend/references/web-markup-and-css.md:130:**Deeper:** [Elements and attributes](web-elements-and-attributes.md) and [performance and loading](web-performance-and-loading.md).\n.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-frontend/references/web-markup-and-css.md:143:**Deeper:** [Cascade and selectors](web-cascade-and-selectors.md) and [accessibility patterns](web-accessibility-patterns.md).\n.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-frontend/references/web-markup-and-css.md:164:**Deeper:** [Elements and attributes](web-elements-and-attributes.md), [accessibility patterns](web-accessibility-patterns.md), [performance and loading](web-performance-and-loading.md) and [internationalization](web-internationalization.md).\n.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-frontend/references/frontend-practice.md:95:**Layers**\n.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-frontend/references/frontend-practice.md:102:**Web topic files**\n.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-quality/references/code-style-guide.md:116:**CHK-CMT-07 (P1):** each comment describes what the code does now, never how it got there, what it used to do or which change introduced it. A comment that narrates its own history is wrong the moment the next change lands. Before: `// this check used to live in the old loader`. After: `// skips blank lines so the count stays true`. This rule adds to the ephemeral-pointer rule below and never softens it.\n.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-review/references/pr-state-dedup.md:44:**Path:** `${XDG_CACHE_HOME:-$HOME/.cache}/sk-code-review/<repo-ref>.jsonl`\n.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-review/references/pr-state-dedup.md:53:**Schema per line:**\n.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-review/references/review-core.md:20:**Ownership.** This file owns the severity ids and their meanings (section 2), the evidence rules (section 3) and the finding schema (section 7). The deep-review contract, [`review-mode-contract.yaml`](../../../system-deep-loop/deep-review/assets/review-mode-contract.yaml), is an external consumer: it owns the deep-review loop (dimensions, verdicts, severity weights, convergence and quality gates) and reuses the severity ids defined here. When the two disagree on a severity id or its meaning, this file wins and the YAML is corrected by its owner, `deep-review`. The YAML may be stricter for its own loop, as when it requires `file:line` evidence on `P2`, but never looser.\n.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/references/python/quality-standards.md:55:**Evidence**: `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/skill_advisor.py:1-4`\n.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/references/python/quality-standards.md:69:**Evidence**: `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/skill_advisor.py:6-16`\n.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/references/python/quality-standards.md:88:**Evidence**: `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/skill_advisor.py`, `.skilled/skills/sk-doc/scripts/package_skill.py:87`\n.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/references/python/quality-standards.md:104:**Evidence**: `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/skill_advisor.py:31-49`\n.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/references/python/quality-standards.md:152:**Evidence**: `.skilled/skills/sk-doc/scripts/package_skill.py:87`\n.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/references/python/quality-standards.md:175:**Evidence**: `.skilled/skills/sk-doc/scripts/package_skill.py:87-93`\n.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/references/python/quality-standards.md:220:**Evidence**: `.skilled/skills/sk-doc/scripts/package_skill.py:98-104`\n.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/references/python/quality-standards.md:327:**Evidence**: `.skilled/skills/sk-doc/scripts/package_skill.py:87-145`\n.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/references/python/quality-standards.md:368:**Evidence**: `.skilled/skills/sk-doc/scripts/package_skill.py:117-121`\n.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/references/python/quality-standards.md:382:**Evidence**: `.skilled/skills/sk-doc/scripts/package_skill.py:134`\n.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/references/python/style-guide.md:50:**ALWAYS** start Python scripts with the portable shebang:\n.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/references/python/style-guide.md:56:**Evidence**: `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/skill_advisor.py:1`\n.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/references/python/style-guide.md:69:**Alternative** (full box for major scripts):\n.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/references/python/style-guide.md:78:**Evidence**: `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/skill_advisor.py:1-4`\n.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/references/python/style-guide.md:97:**Evidence**: `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/skill_advisor.py:6-16`\n.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/references/python/style-guide.md:119:**Evidence**: `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/skill_advisor.py:17-22`, `.skilled/skills/sk-doc/scripts/package_skill.py:25-30`\n.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/references/python/style-guide.md:140:**Evidence**: `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/skill_advisor.py:25-27`, `.skilled/skills/sk-doc/scripts/package_skill.py:32-34`\n.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/references/python/style-guide.md:148:**Use**: `snake_case` (lowercase with underscores)\n.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/references/python/style-guide.md:164:**Evidence**: `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/skill_advisor.py`, `.skilled/skills/sk-doc/scripts/package_skill.py:87`\n.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/references/python/style-guide.md:168:**Use**: `PascalCase`\n","title":"shell","metadata":{"metadata":{"status":"completed","truncated":false,"exit":0},"content":[{"type":"text","text":".skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-ci/references/trigger-and-filter-rules.md:52:**The rule.** A rebuild commit carries `[skip ci]` in its message body. GitHub then starts no push-triggered or pull-request-triggered workflow for that commit, and the downstream workflows stay quiet without a single filter edit. The commit also ends with a trailer the profile names as `rebuildTrailer`, so the report can skip it when it looks for the newest commit worth reading.\n.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-ci/references/trigger-and-filter-rules.md:60:**The alternative.** A push-only `paths-ignore` that lists the eight twin entries on each downstream workflow does the same job, at the price of 104 twin-matched entries across 13 files and an amended ordering rule. Use it only if the skip token proves unreliable.\n.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-ci/references/trigger-and-filter-rules.md:62:**What it saves.** Skipping the downstream workflows on the four generated paths saves about 2,073 runner-min/day, roughly 54% of the repository's daily total. The push-only filter form saves about 2,002 runner-min/day when `message-contract.yml` is left unfiltered.\n.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-ci/references/trigger-and-filter-rules.md:64:**Reading statuses.** A skipped rebuild commit leaves no runs on the branch tip. Any tool that reads statuses on the tip must resolve the newest commit that is not a rebuild commit.\n.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-ci/references/job-shape-rules.md:34:**Formula.** `max(<floor>, ceil(3 x job p50 minutes), ceil(2 x job p100 minutes))` with a floor of 10 minutes. Pull at least 40 recent runs of job-level timings (`gh run view --json jobs`), compute p50 and p100 per job and state the observed p100 next to the chosen value in the commit or the workflow comment. A successful job in the sample should never exceed half of its new timeout.\n.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-ci/references/job-shape-rules.md:36:**Today.** `44 of 44` jobs in the 32 workflows carry a timeout, on one job-level line each. `35` of them sit at the 10 minute floor. The other **9 jobs** sit above it.\n.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-frontend/references/web-markup-and-css.md:58:**Deeper:** [Elements and attributes](web-elements-and-attributes.md) and [accessibility patterns](web-accessibility-patterns.md).\n.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-frontend/references/web-markup-and-css.md:73:**The `autocomplete` disagreement.** `interaction-craft.md` Section 2 advises disabling `autocomplete` on most inputs. That holds for free-text fields where browser history adds noise, such as comments, in-app search filters and admin entry of someone else's data. It does not hold for fields about the user. Identity, contact, address, payment and credential fields need the right token so autofill and password managers work and the input purpose is machine-readable:\n.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-frontend/references/web-markup-and-css.md:85:**Deeper:** [Elements and attributes](web-elements-and-attributes.md).\n.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-frontend/references/web-markup-and-css.md:101:**Deeper:** [Layout and sizing](web-layout-and-sizing.md) and [internationalization](web-internationalization.md).\n.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-frontend/references/web-markup-and-css.md:117:**Deeper:** [Layout and sizing](web-layout-and-sizing.md) and [accessibility patterns](web-accessibility-patterns.md).\n.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-frontend/references/web-markup-and-css.md:130:**Deeper:** [Elements and attributes](web-elements-and-attributes.md) and [performance and loading](web-performance-and-loading.md).\n.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-frontend/references/web-markup-and-css.md:143:**Deeper:** [Cascade and selectors](web-cascade-and-selectors.md) and [accessibility patterns](web-accessibility-patterns.md).\n.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-frontend/references/web-markup-and-css.md:164:**Deeper:** [Elements and attributes](web-elements-and-attributes.md), [accessibility patterns](web-accessibility-patterns.md), [performance and loading](web-performance-and-loading.md) and [internationalization](web-internationalization.md).\n.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-frontend/references/frontend-practice.md:95:**Layers**\n.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-frontend/references/frontend-practice.md:102:**Web topic files**\n.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-quality/references/code-style-guide.md:116:**CHK-CMT-07 (P1):** each comment describes what the code does now, never how it got there, what it used to do or which change introduced it. A comment that narrates its own history is wrong the moment the next change lands. Before: `// this check used to live in the old loader`. After: `// skips blank lines so the count stays true`. This rule adds to the ephemeral-pointer rule below and never softens it.\n.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-review/references/pr-state-dedup.md:44:**Path:** `${XDG_CACHE_HOME:-$HOME/.cache}/sk-code-review/<repo-ref>.jsonl`\n.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-review/references/pr-state-dedup.md:53:**Schema per line:**\n.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-review/references/review-core.md:20:**Ownership.** This file owns the severity ids and their meanings (section 2), the evidence rules (section 3) and the finding schema (section 7). The deep-review contract, [`review-mode-contract.yaml`](../../../system-deep-loop/deep-review/assets/review-mode-contract.yaml), is an external consumer: it owns the deep-review loop (dimensions, verdicts, severity weights, convergence and quality gates) and reuses the severity ids defined here. When the two disagree on a severity id or its meaning, this file wins and the YAML is corrected by its owner, `deep-review`. The YAML may be stricter for its own loop, as when it requires `file:line` evidence on `P2`, but never looser.\n.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/references/python/quality-standards.md:55:**Evidence**: `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/skill_advisor.py:1-4`\n.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/references/python/quality-standards.md:69:**Evidence**: `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/skill_advisor.py:6-16`\n.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/references/python/quality-standards.md:88:**Evidence**: `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/skill_advisor.py`, `.skilled/skills/sk-doc/scripts/package_skill.py:87`\n.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/references/python/quality-standards.md:104:**Evidence**: `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/skill_advisor.py:31-49`\n.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/references/python/quality-standards.md:152:**Evidence**: `.skilled/skills/sk-doc/scripts/package_skill.py:87`\n.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/references/python/quality-standards.md:175:**Evidence**: `.skilled/skills/sk-doc/scripts/package_skill.py:87-93`\n.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/references/python/quality-standards.md:220:**Evidence**: `.skilled/skills/sk-doc/scripts/package_skill.py:98-104`\n.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/references/python/quality-standards.md:327:**Evidence**: `.skilled/skills/sk-doc/scripts/package_skill.py:87-145`\n.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/references/python/quality-standards.md:368:**Evidence**: `.skilled/skills/sk-doc/scripts/package_skill.py:117-121`\n.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/references/python/quality-standards.md:382:**Evidence**: `.skilled/skills/sk-doc/scripts/package_skill.py:134`\n.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/references/python/style-guide.md:50:**ALWAYS** start Python scripts with the portable shebang:\n.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/references/python/style-guide.md:56:**Evidence**: `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/skill_advisor.py:1`\n.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/references/python/style-guide.md:69:**Alternative** (full box for major scripts):\n.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/references/python/style-guide.md:78:**Evidence**: `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/skill_advisor.py:1-4`\n.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/references/python/style-guide.md:97:**Evidence**: `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/skill_advisor.py:6-16`\n.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/references/python/style-guide.md:119:**Evidence**: `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/skill_advisor.py:17-22`, `.skilled/skills/sk-doc/scripts/package_skill.py:25-30`\n.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/references/python/style-guide.md:140:**Evidence**: `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/skill_advisor.py:25-27`, `.skilled/skills/sk-doc/scripts/package_skill.py:32-34`\n.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/references/python/style-guide.md:148:**Use**: `snake_case` (lowercase with underscores)\n.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/references/python/style-guide.md:164:**Evidence**: `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/skill_advisor.py`, `.skilled/skills/sk-doc/scripts/package_skill.py:87`\n.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/references/python/style-guide.md:168:**Use**: `PascalCase`\n"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"command\": \"grep -rn --include='*.md' -E '^\\\\*\\\\*[^*]{2,90}\\\\*\\\\*' .skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-*/references .skilled/skills/sk-code/shared/references | head -40\"}"},"time":{"start":1793742420238,"end":1793742420311}}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1793742420315,"sessionID":"ses_e5ca055c6ffeOZO66b0AKuGGW6","part":{"partID":"prt_1a36138bf001YYfYyU2p00vPOX","sessionID":"ses_e5ca055c6ffeOZO66b0AKuGGW6","messageID":"msg_1a36120b5001R7cA9WN4yiWzko","type":"tool","id":"call_00_74f2my2g5xrja4pet439yreq","tool":"shell","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"git show cd00629166:.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-frontend/references/web-cascade-and-selectors.md 2>/dev/null | head -60; echo \"===EXIT:$?\""},"output":"---\ntitle: Web Cascade and Selectors\ndescription: CSS cascade order, selector specificity and modern selector behavior.\ntrigger_phrases:\n  - \"css cascade layer order\"\n  - \"css selector specificity\"\n  - \"native css nesting\"\nimportance_tier: important\ncontextType: implementation\nversion: 1.0.1.0\n---\n\n# Web Cascade and Selectors\n\nThis file keeps the deeper cascade and selector mechanics. The baseline CSS architecture rules remain in [web-markup-and-css.md](web-markup-and-css.md).\n\n---\n\n## 1. OVERVIEW AND CASCADE AND SPECIFICITY\n\n### CS1. Cascade decision order\n\n**Rule.** Resolve competing declarations by origin and importance, layer order, specificity, scope proximity when scoped rules apply, then source order.\n**Why.** The cascade compares these criteria in order, so a later rule cannot win by source order when an earlier criterion already decided the result.\n**When not to.** Skip the comparison when the declarations do not apply to the same element and property.\n**Pitfall.** Normal unlayered declarations outrank normal declarations in named layers, while important declarations reverse layer precedence.\n**Support.** Judgment, no version gate.\n**See also.** https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/Cascade\n\n### CS2. Global cascade layer order\n\n**Rule.** Declare the cascade layer order once in a global stylesheet before component styles load.\n**Why.** Layer order is established the first time each layer name appears. Component style injection can vary with load order, so per-component layer blocks cannot define a reliable shared order.\n**When not to.** Use unlayered styles only when the app deliberately relies on the unlayered precedence boundary.\n**Pitfall.** A deeply scoped rule inside a layer loses to an unlayered rule even when its selector looks stronger.\n**Support.** Baseline widely available since 2024-09-14 (web-features `cascade-layers`). Chrome 99 / Firefox 97 / Safari 15.4.\n**See also.** https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/@layer\n\n### CS3. Specificity arithmetic with Angular emulation\n\n**Rule.** Count IDs, class-like selectors and type selectors in separate specificity columns before changing a selector.\n**Why.** IDs add to the first column, classes, attributes and pseudo-classes add to the second, and type selectors and pseudo-elements add to the third. A component-scoping attribute such as `[data-scope]` adds one class-like unit.\n**When not to.** Do not raise specificity when the actual conflict comes from origin, importance or cascade layer order.\n**Pitfall.** A selector such as `.card[data-scope]` has specificity `0-2-0`, not `0-1-0`.\n**Support.** Judgment, no version gate.\n**See also.** https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/Specificity\n\n---\n\n## 2. SELECTOR LOGIC\n\n### CS4. Zero-specificity and maximum-argument selectors\n\n**Rule.** Wrap low-priority defaults in `:where()` and use `:is()` when the matching alternatives should share the strongest argument's specificity.\n**Why.** `:where()` contributes zero specificity, while `:is()` contributes the specificity of its most specific argument.\n**When not to.** Keep a direct class selector when the alternatives would obscure which component states match.\n**Pitfall.** Adding a high-specificity selector to one `:is()` alternative raises the specificity of the entire selector.\n**Support.** Baseline widely available since 2023-07-21 (web-features `is`). Chrome 88 / Firefox 82 / Safari 14.\n**Support.** Baseline widely available since 2023-07-21 (web-features `where`). Chrome 88 / Firefox 82 / Safari 14.\n**See also.** https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/:is\n===EXIT:0\n","title":"shell","metadata":{"metadata":{"status":"completed","truncated":false,"exit":0},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"---\ntitle: Web Cascade and Selectors\ndescription: CSS cascade order, selector specificity and modern selector behavior.\ntrigger_phrases:\n  - \"css cascade layer order\"\n  - \"css selector specificity\"\n  - \"native css nesting\"\nimportance_tier: important\ncontextType: implementation\nversion: 1.0.1.0\n---\n\n# Web Cascade and Selectors\n\nThis file keeps the deeper cascade and selector mechanics. The baseline CSS architecture rules remain in [web-markup-and-css.md](web-markup-and-css.md).\n\n---\n\n## 1. OVERVIEW AND CASCADE AND SPECIFICITY\n\n### CS1. Cascade decision order\n\n**Rule.** Resolve competing declarations by origin and importance, layer order, specificity, scope proximity when scoped rules apply, then source order.\n**Why.** The cascade compares these criteria in order, so a later rule cannot win by source order when an earlier criterion already decided the result.\n**When not to.** Skip the comparison when the declarations do not apply to the same element and property.\n**Pitfall.** Normal unlayered declarations outrank normal declarations in named layers, while important declarations reverse layer precedence.\n**Support.** Judgment, no version gate.\n**See also.** https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/Cascade\n\n### CS2. Global cascade layer order\n\n**Rule.** Declare the cascade layer order once in a global stylesheet before component styles load.\n**Why.** Layer order is established the first time each layer name appears. Component style injection can vary with load order, so per-component layer blocks cannot define a reliable shared order.\n**When not to.** Use unlayered styles only when the app deliberately relies on the unlayered precedence boundary.\n**Pitfall.** A deeply scoped rule inside a layer loses to an unlayered rule even when its selector looks stronger.\n**Support.** Baseline widely available since 2024-09-14 (web-features `cascade-layers`). Chrome 99 / Firefox 97 / Safari 15.4.\n**See also.** https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/@layer\n\n### CS3. Specificity arithmetic with Angular emulation\n\n**Rule.** Count IDs, class-like selectors and type selectors in separate specificity columns before changing a selector.\n**Why.** IDs add to the first column, classes, attributes and pseudo-classes add to the second, and type selectors and pseudo-elements add to the third. A component-scoping attribute such as `[data-scope]` adds one class-like unit.\n**When not to.** Do not raise specificity when the actual conflict comes from origin, importance or cascade layer order.\n**Pitfall.** A selector such as `.card[data-scope]` has specificity `0-2-0`, not `0-1-0`.\n**Support.** Judgment, no version gate.\n**See also.** https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/Specificity\n\n---\n\n## 2. SELECTOR LOGIC\n\n### CS4. Zero-specificity and maximum-argument selectors\n\n**Rule.** Wrap low-priority defaults in `:where()` and use `:is()` when the matching alternatives should share the strongest argument's specificity.\n**Why.** `:where()` contributes zero specificity, while `:is()` contributes the specificity of its most specific argument.\n**When not to.** Keep a direct class selector when the alternatives would obscure which component states match.\n**Pitfall.** Adding a high-specificity selector to one `:is()` alternative raises the specificity of the entire selector.\n**Support.** Baseline widely available since 2023-07-21 (web-features `is`). Chrome 88 / Firefox 82 / Safari 14.\n**Support.** Baseline widely available since 2023-07-21 (web-features `where`). Chrome 88 / Firefox 82 / Safari 14.\n**See also.** https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/:is\n===EXIT:0\n"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"command\": \"git show cd00629166:.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-frontend/references/web-cascade-and-selectors.md 2>/dev/null | head -60; echo \\\"===EXIT:$?\\\"\"}"},"time":{"start":1793742420159,"end":1793742420315}}}}
