{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1793742490266,"sessionID":"ses_e5ca055c6ffeOZO66b0AKuGGW6","part":{"partID":"prt_1a3624a440013BITXSuTSkwvLv","sessionID":"ses_e5ca055c6ffeOZO66b0AKuGGW6","messageID":"msg_1a3623dc2001YBGn2gwCERBI50","type":"tool","id":"call_01_ntnbold3xu0mcqe7ao0cze9o","tool":"shell","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"sed -n '18,34p' .skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/references/rust/style-guide/naming-conventions.md"},"output":"\n## 1. OVERVIEW\n\n### Purpose\n\nProvides focused Rust guidance for naming conventions in the OpenCode development environment.\n\n### When to Use\n\n- Implementing or reviewing Rust code covered by this topic\n- Preserving TypeScript interoperability and deterministic behavior\n- Applying the corresponding Rust standards at an implementation boundary\n\n---\n\n## 2. NAMING CONVENTIONS\n\n","title":"shell","metadata":{"metadata":{"status":"completed","truncated":false,"exit":0},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"\n## 1. OVERVIEW\n\n### Purpose\n\nProvides focused Rust guidance for naming conventions in the OpenCode development environment.\n\n### When to Use\n\n- Implementing or reviewing Rust code covered by this topic\n- Preserving TypeScript interoperability and deterministic behavior\n- Applying the corresponding Rust standards at an implementation boundary\n\n---\n\n## 2. NAMING CONVENTIONS\n\n"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"command\": \"sed -n '18,34p' .skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/references/rust/style-guide/naming-conventions.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1793742490181,"end":1793742490266}}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1793742490276,"sessionID":"ses_e5ca055c6ffeOZO66b0AKuGGW6","part":{"partID":"prt_1a36249ef001EdZSIjdP7EOKC8","sessionID":"ses_e5ca055c6ffeOZO66b0AKuGGW6","messageID":"msg_1a3623dc2001YBGn2gwCERBI50","type":"tool","id":"call_00_kfdmxbnklu73gfsb8lgfudct","tool":"shell","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"sed -n '18,40p' .skilled/skills/sk-code/shared/references/phase-detection.md; echo \"===ROUTER 20-40===\"; sed -n '16,42p' .skilled/skills/sk-code/ROUTER.md; echo \"===ALIGN 136-155===\"; sed -n '136,155p' .skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/references/shared/alignment-verification-automation.md"},"output":"---\n\n## 1. OVERVIEW\n\n### Purpose\n\nKeep research, implementation, the quality gate, debugging and verification in a fixed order, so no task reaches \"done\" on a skipped gate. The active surface changes which resources and evidence each phase uses. It never changes the order or the gates.\n\n### When to Use\n\n- Planning code work on any sk-code surface.\n- Choosing which phase doctrine and checklists to load next.\n- Routing a failed quality or verification gate back to debugging or implementation.\n- Judging whether the completion evidence is enough to claim \"done\".\n\n### Core Principle\n\nA phase ends on evidence, not on effort. The next phase starts only when the current one has produced what its exit condition names.\n\n### Key Sources\n\n- [stack-detection.md](./stack-detection.md) selects the surface. It returns SKILLED or UNKNOWN and never `sk-code-ci` or `sk-code-frontend`, which the hub bundles from request vocabulary alone (`code-ci-aliases` and `code-ci-runtime`, and `code-frontend-aliases`, in `hub-router.json`).\n- `mode-registry.json` declares the workflow modes (`sk-code-quality`, `sk-code-review`) and the surfaces (`sk-code-ci`, `sk-code-frontend`, `sk-code-skilled`).\n===ROUTER 20-40===\n# sk-code Surface Router — routing logic and resource maps\n\nLeaf-set authority for sk-code. The routing authority is named once in `SKILL.md` §2 SMART ROUTING: `hub-router.json` is the mode vocabulary, and this document is the leaf-set router. Consolidates intent classification + resource loading into the single Barter-style smart-routing entry point. Detection logic lives in [`stack-detection.md`](shared/references/stack-detection.md); phase lifecycle lives in [`phase-detection.md`](shared/references/phase-detection.md).\n\n---\n\n## 1. OVERVIEW\n\n### Purpose\n\nThe smart router maps a detected surface (a declared specific surface, then the SKILLED fallback, then UNKNOWN) plus a classified intent (IMPLEMENTATION / DEBUGGING / VERIFICATION / etc.) to the exact references, assets, and verification commands needed for the active task. Loads only the smallest relevant set so the active context window stays focused.\n\n### When to Use\n\n- After surface detection (see [`stack-detection.md`](shared/references/stack-detection.md)) determines the surface: a declared specific surface, then the SKILLED fallback, then UNKNOWN\n- When classifying task wording into the dominant work intent\n- When mapping intent to concrete reference paths\n- When picking verification commands for the surface\n\n### Core Principle\n\nRouting is a two-stage decision: **surface-first, intent-second**. Surface narrows the resource family: a declared specific surface first, then the SKILLED fallback, then UNKNOWN. Intent narrows the specific files within that family. The detected surface also goes to the compiled front door as `--surface-hint`. When a prompt ties between surfaces, a hinted specific surface leads the bundle. SKILLED already ranks last, so a SKILLED hint changes nothing (`SKILL.md` §3).\n\n### Key Sources\n\n- [`stack-detection.md`](shared/references/stack-detection.md): surface detection (a declared specific surface, then the SKILLED fallback, then UNKNOWN) and SKILLED language sub-detection\n- [`phase-detection.md`](shared/references/phase-detection.md) — Phase 1/2/3 lifecycle and phase-specific resource expectations\n===ALIGN 136-155===\n\n---\n\n## 5. RELATED RESOURCES\n\n- [code-organization/overview-and-module-organization.md](code-organization/overview-and-module-organization.md)\n- [universal-patterns/naming-and-commenting.md](universal-patterns/naming-and-commenting.md)\n- [hooks.md](./hooks.md)\n- Drift-guard entry point: `.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/scripts/run-all-drift-guards.sh` — runs this verifier, `verify_stack_folders.py`, `verify-router-sync.cjs` (every leg: 1a, 1b, 2, 3 and 4) and `verify-doc-claims.cjs` together as one gate.\n\n---\n\n## 6. ALIGNMENT AUTHORITY INTERFACE (sk-code-skilled)\n\nsk-code-skilled has exactly one alignment source of truth: the three-part interface\nbelow. Later coverage and activation work must consume this interface rather than\nre-derive a second RESOURCE_MAP parser or a local eligibility map.\n\n1. **Doc pointer.** This file plus the sk-code-skilled `SKILL.md` SMART ROUTING\n   block, which record that the `sk-code-router-sync.vitest.ts` suite was\n","title":"shell","metadata":{"metadata":{"status":"completed","truncated":false,"exit":0},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"---\n\n## 1. OVERVIEW\n\n### Purpose\n\nKeep research, implementation, the quality gate, debugging and verification in a fixed order, so no task reaches \"done\" on a skipped gate. The active surface changes which resources and evidence each phase uses. It never changes the order or the gates.\n\n### When to Use\n\n- Planning code work on any sk-code surface.\n- Choosing which phase doctrine and checklists to load next.\n- Routing a failed quality or verification gate back to debugging or implementation.\n- Judging whether the completion evidence is enough to claim \"done\".\n\n### Core Principle\n\nA phase ends on evidence, not on effort. The next phase starts only when the current one has produced what its exit condition names.\n\n### Key Sources\n\n- [stack-detection.md](./stack-detection.md) selects the surface. It returns SKILLED or UNKNOWN and never `sk-code-ci` or `sk-code-frontend`, which the hub bundles from request vocabulary alone (`code-ci-aliases` and `code-ci-runtime`, and `code-frontend-aliases`, in `hub-router.json`).\n- `mode-registry.json` declares the workflow modes (`sk-code-quality`, `sk-code-review`) and the surfaces (`sk-code-ci`, `sk-code-frontend`, `sk-code-skilled`).\n===ROUTER 20-40===\n# sk-code Surface Router — routing logic and resource maps\n\nLeaf-set authority for sk-code. The routing authority is named once in `SKILL.md` §2 SMART ROUTING: `hub-router.json` is the mode vocabulary, and this document is the leaf-set router. Consolidates intent classification + resource loading into the single Barter-style smart-routing entry point. Detection logic lives in [`stack-detection.md`](shared/references/stack-detection.md); phase lifecycle lives in [`phase-detection.md`](shared/references/phase-detection.md).\n\n---\n\n## 1. OVERVIEW\n\n### Purpose\n\nThe smart router maps a detected surface (a declared specific surface, then the SKILLED fallback, then UNKNOWN) plus a classified intent (IMPLEMENTATION / DEBUGGING / VERIFICATION / etc.) to the exact references, assets, and verification commands needed for the active task. Loads only the smallest relevant set so the active context window stays focused.\n\n### When to Use\n\n- After surface detection (see [`stack-detection.md`](shared/references/stack-detection.md)) determines the surface: a declared specific surface, then the SKILLED fallback, then UNKNOWN\n- When classifying task wording into the dominant work intent\n- When mapping intent to concrete reference paths\n- When picking verification commands for the surface\n\n### Core Principle\n\nRouting is a two-stage decision: **surface-first, intent-second**. Surface narrows the resource family: a declared specific surface first, then the SKILLED fallback, then UNKNOWN. Intent narrows the specific files within that family. The detected surface also goes to the compiled front door as `--surface-hint`. When a prompt ties between surfaces, a hinted specific surface leads the bundle. SKILLED already ranks last, so a SKILLED hint changes nothing (`SKILL.md` §3).\n\n### Key Sources\n\n- [`stack-detection.md`](shared/references/stack-detection.md): surface detection (a declared specific surface, then the SKILLED fallback, then UNKNOWN) and SKILLED language sub-detection\n- [`phase-detection.md`](shared/references/phase-detection.md) — Phase 1/2/3 lifecycle and phase-specific resource expectations\n===ALIGN 136-155===\n\n---\n\n## 5. RELATED RESOURCES\n\n- [code-organization/overview-and-module-organization.md](code-organization/overview-and-module-organization.md)\n- [universal-patterns/naming-and-commenting.md](universal-patterns/naming-and-commenting.md)\n- [hooks.md](./hooks.md)\n- Drift-guard entry point: `.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/scripts/run-all-drift-guards.sh` — runs this verifier, `verify_stack_folders.py`, `verify-router-sync.cjs` (every leg: 1a, 1b, 2, 3 and 4) and `verify-doc-claims.cjs` together as one gate.\n\n---\n\n## 6. ALIGNMENT AUTHORITY INTERFACE (sk-code-skilled)\n\nsk-code-skilled has exactly one alignment source of truth: the three-part interface\nbelow. Later coverage and activation work must consume this interface rather than\nre-derive a second RESOURCE_MAP parser or a local eligibility map.\n\n1. **Doc pointer.** This file plus the sk-code-skilled `SKILL.md` SMART ROUTING\n   block, which record that the `sk-code-router-sync.vitest.ts` suite was\n"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"command\": \"sed -n '18,40p' .skilled/skills/sk-code/shared/references/phase-detection.md; echo \\\"===ROUTER 20-40===\\\"; sed -n '16,42p' .skilled/skills/sk-code/ROUTER.md; echo \\\"===ALIGN 136-155===\\\"; sed -n '136,155p' .skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/references/shared/alignment-verification-automation.md\"}"},"time":{"start":17937424900