{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1793742508383,"sessionID":"ses_e5ca055c6ffeOZO66b0AKuGGW6","part":{"id":"prt_1a362915f001jMKIgwFRQ8QLez","sessionID":"ses_e5ca055c6ffeOZO66b0AKuGGW6","messageID":"msg_1a3628b2c001ezoo2Jihur8lgf","type":"step-start","snapshot":"c3a54b45009e35532c8c01d01e0b5799195854af"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1793742519056,"sessionID":"ses_e5ca055c6ffeOZO66b0AKuGGW6","part":{"partID":"prt_1a362badd001ThyUOfLOazuaux","sessionID":"ses_e5ca055c6ffeOZO66b0AKuGGW6","messageID":"msg_1a3628b2c001ezoo2Jihur8lgf","type":"tool","id":"call_01_ppoosv4521g6h9q1ybxd0bb8","tool":"shell","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"sed -n '200,230p' .skilled/skills/sk-code/ROUTER.md; echo \"===TAIL===\"; sed -n '440,470p' .skilled/skills/sk-code/ROUTER.md; echo \"===SHARED README 1-40===\"; sed -n '1,40p' .skilled/skills/sk-code/shared/README.md"},"output":"---\n\n## 7. LOADING DISCIPLINE (DO / DON'T)\n\n### DO\n\n- Load only references needed for current intent + surface\n- Use exact canonical file paths in playbook result YAML (never directory placeholders)\n- Cite surface + top-1 intent + loaded resource paths + verification command in your final answer\n\n### DON'T\n\n- Load all of `sk-code-skilled/` indiscriminately (\"just in case\")\n- Load Go, Next.js server code or React Native resources from canonical sk-code; those live in a project fork\n- Drop directory placeholders in playbook result YAML\n\n---\n\n## 8. RELATED RESOURCES\n\n- [`stack-detection.md`](shared/references/stack-detection.md): surface detection (a declared specific surface, then the SKILLED fallback, then UNKNOWN) + SKILLED language sub-detection\n- [`phase-detection.md`](shared/references/phase-detection.md) — Phase 1/2/3 lifecycle and per-phase resource loading\n- `SKILL.md` §2 SMART ROUTING — names the routing authority split and the compiled front door\n- Barter equivalent: `barter/.skilled/skills/sk-code/shared/references/smart-routing.md` (different routing key — git-remote project — but same structural pattern)\n\n---\n\n## 9. MACHINE-READABLE ROUTER (replay / benchmark source)\n\nThis block is the authored source for the intent vocabulary and weights. §2 explains how it is scored and does not repeat the keywords; §4 renders the same rows as prose. When an intent row changes here, update the matching prose row in §4.\n\n===TAIL===\n\n## 10. HOW TO READ THIS\n\n- Every code task first loads the always-on preamble (stack + phase detection and the universal quality baseline); routing then narrows to the detected surface's slice for the matched intents.\n- One dominant intent on one surface loads that surface's slice; two near-tied intents (within the router's ambiguity delta) load both intent sets, deduped by canonical pair.\n- A single-surface task never pulls another surface's resources.\n- No keyword match, or an `UNKNOWN` surface, falls back to the preamble plus the shared-tier entries its matched intents map — confirm the surface and intent before loading more. See §6 (UNKNOWN FALLBACK) and §7 (LOADING DISCIPLINE) for the full rules.\n\n---\n\n## 11. MACHINE ROUTER\n\nThe hub's detailed routing prose, moved here from `SKILL.md` so the skill body keeps only a short routing summary and a pointer to this file. It sits last, after the stage-two map above, and it declares no map of its own: the `INTENT_SIGNALS` and `RESOURCE_MAP` block in section 9 stays the only replay source.\n\n> **Compiled routing (default-on, flag-gated, additive).** Resolve the mode via the compiled router contract first:\n> ```bash\n> node .skilled/bin/compiled-route.cjs --hub sk-code --prompt \"<task>\"\n> ```\n> Follow the returned decision: `route` (use its `targets`), `clarify`/`defer` (disambiguate) or `reject` (refuse). On a `{\"servingAuthority\":\"legacy\"}` sentinel or any error, use the routing below. The front door self-gates on serving-authority. Compiled routing is now the default for `sk-code`. Set `SPECKIT_COMPILED_ROUTING=0`, the explicit kill-switch, to force legacy routing fleet-wide.\n\n**Session surface hint.** Before you call the front door, run the surface detection in [`stack-detection.md`](shared/references/stack-detection.md) on the session's working directory and the files it is editing.\nThen add its result as `--surface-hint <SURFACE>`, for example `--surface-hint SKILLED`.\nWhen the prompt's keywords tie between surfaces, a hinted specific surface leads them.\nWorkflow modes keep first place, and the targets and the bundle kind stay the same.\n`SKILLED` is the fallback surface and always ranks last, so a `SKILLED` hint changes nothing.\nWith `UNKNOWN`, no hint or a surface the prompt did not match, the `routerPolicy.tieBreak` order applies.\n\n### Surface Router: per-intent leaf sets\n\nRoot `ROUTER.md` owns the second-stage `INTENT_SIGNALS` / `RESOURCE_MAP`, while packet-owned resources stay typed through `leaf-manifest.json`.\nThe hub-level shared controls are declared once, in `ROUTER.md` §9: the `SHARED_CONTROL_RESOURCES` list plus the always-loaded `DEFAULT_RESOURCE` preamble. They resolve on disk but never project as leaves.\n===SHARED README 1-40===\n---\ntitle: sk-code shared\ndescription: Index of the sk-code hub shared folder, which holds only doctrine about the parent hub and its nested modes, such as surface detection, the phase lifecycle, the implement, debug and verify workflow doctrine and the universal quality standards.\ntrigger_phrases:\n  - \"sk-code shared folder\"\n  - \"sk-code shared references\"\n  - \"shared workflow doctrine\"\n  - \"sk-code surface detection\"\nimportance_tier: normal\ncontextType: general\nversion: 1.2.5.0\n---\n\n# sk-code shared\n\n> The hub-level doctrine every sk-code mode and surface reads: how a surface is chosen, how the phases run and which quality bar applies. Mode-owned material lives in its mode, never here.\n\n---\n\n## 1. OVERVIEW\n\n`shared/` holds the material that belongs to the `sk-code` parent hub and to no single nested mode. The workflow modes `sk-code-quality` and `sk-code-review` act. The surface packets `sk-code-ci`, `sk-code-frontend` and `sk-code-skilled` supply read-only evidence. All of them read the files below, so each file lives once here instead of being copied into every packet.\n\nThis README is also the hub's zero-signal fallback. [`../hub-router.json`](../hub-router.json) names this file as `routerPolicy.defaultResource`, and the router offers it with the disambiguation checklist when a prompt scores no mode. A scored route never loads it.\n\nThe folder ships reference files, one linter that both workflow modes read their rule overrides through and the registry of rule ids it is checked against, with no assets or graph metadata. Authoritative content lives in the linked files. This page only says what each one is for and who reads it.\n\n---\n\n## 2. STRUCTURE\n\n```text example\nshared/\n+-- README.md                          # this index and the hub's zero-signal fallback\n+-- references/\n|   +-- stack-detection.md             # surface detection, the primary routing key\n|   +-- phase-detection.md             # phase lifecycle and per-surface evidence\n|   +-- workflow-implement.md          # implementation doctrine, symlinked into surfaces\n|   +-- workflow-debug.md              # debugging doctrine, symlinked into surfaces\n|   +-- workflow-verify.md             # verification doctrine, symlinked into surfaces\n","title":"shell","metadata":{"metadata":{"status":"completed","truncated":false,"exit":0},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"---\n\n## 7. LOADING DISCIPLINE (DO / DON'T)\n\n### DO\n\n- Load only references needed for current intent + surface\n- Use exact canonical file paths in playbook result YAML (never directory placeholders)\n- Cite surface + top-1 intent + loaded resource paths + verification command in your final answer\n\n### DON'T\n\n- Load all of `sk-code-skilled/` indiscriminately (\"just in case\")\n- Load Go, Next.js server code or React Native resources from canonical sk-code; those live in a project fork\n- Drop directory placeholders in playbook result YAML\n\n---\n\n## 8. RELATED RESOURCES\n\n- [`stack-detection.md`](shared/references/stack-detection.md): surface detection (a declared specific surface, then the SKILLED fallback, then UNKNOWN) + SKILLED language sub-detection\n- [`phase-detection.md`](shared/references/phase-detection.md) — Phase 1/2/3 lifecycle and per-phase resource loading\n- `SKILL.md` §2 SMART ROUTING — names the routing authority split and the compiled front door\n- Barter equivalent: `barter/.skilled/skills/sk-code/shared/references/smart-routing.md` (different routing key — git-remote project — but same structural pattern)\n\n---\n\n## 9. MACHINE-READABLE ROUTER (replay / benchmark source)\n\nThis block is the authored source for the intent vocabulary and weights. §2 explains how it is scored and does not repeat the keywords; §4 renders the same rows as prose. When an intent row changes here, update the matching prose row in §4.\n\n===TAIL===\n\n## 10. HOW TO READ THIS\n\n- Every code task first loads the always-on preamble (stack + phase detection and the universal quality baseline); routing then narrows to the detected surface's slice for the matched intents.\n- One dominant intent on one surface loads that surface's slice; two near-tied intents (within the router's ambiguity delta) load both intent sets, deduped by canonical pair.\n- A single-surface task never pulls another surface's resources.\n- No keyword match, or an `UNKNOWN` surface, falls back to the preamble plus the shared-tier entries its matched intents map — confirm the surface and intent before loading more. See §6 (UNKNOWN FALLBACK) and §7 (LOADING DISCIPLINE) for the full rules.\n\n---\n\n## 11. MACHINE ROUTER\n\nThe hub's detailed routing prose, moved here from `SKILL.md` so the skill body keeps only a short routing summary and a pointer to this file. It sits last, after the stage-two map above, and it declares no map of its own: the `INTENT_SIGNALS` and `RESOURCE_MAP` block in section 9 stays the only replay source.\n\n> **Compiled routing (default-on, flag-gated, additive).** Resolve the mode via the compiled router contract first:\n> ```bash\n> node .skilled/bin/compiled-route.cjs --hub sk-code --prompt \"<task>\"\n> ```\n> Follow the returned decision: `route` (use its `targets`), `clarify`/`defer` (disambiguate) or `reject` (refuse). On a `{\"servingAuthority\":\"legacy\"}` sentinel or any error, use the routing below. The front door self-gates on serving-authority. Compiled routing is now the default for `sk-code`. Set `SPECKIT_COMPILED_ROUTING=0`, the explicit kill-switch, to force legacy routing fleet-wide.\n\n**Session surface hint.** Before you call the front door, run the surface detection in [`stack-detection.md`](shared/references/stack-detection.md) on the session's working directory and the files it is editing.\nThen add its result as `--surface-hint <SURFACE>`, for example `--surface-hint SKILLED`.\nWhen the prompt's keywords tie between surfaces, a hinted specific surface leads them.\nWorkflow modes keep first place, and the targets and the bundle kind stay the same.\n`SKILLED` is the fallback surface and always ranks last, so a `SKILLED` hint changes nothing.\nWith `UNKNOWN`, no hint or a surface the prompt did not match, the `routerPolicy.tieBreak` order applies.\n\n### Surface Router: per-intent leaf sets\n\nRoot `ROUTER.md` owns the second-stage `INTENT_SIGNALS` / `RESOURCE_MAP`, while packet-owned resources stay typed through `leaf-manifest.json`.\nThe hub-level shared controls are declared once, in `ROUTER.md` §9: the `SHARED_CONTROL_RESOURCES` list plus the always-loaded `DEFAULT_RESOURCE` preamble. They resolve on disk but never project as leaves.\n===SHARED README 1-40===\n---\ntitle: sk-code shared\ndescription: Index of the sk-code hub shared folder, which holds only doctrine about the parent hub and its nested modes, such as surface detection, the phase lifecycle, the implement, debug and verify workflow doctrine and the universal quality standards.\ntrigger_phrases:\n  - \"sk-code shared folder\"\n  - \"sk-code shared references\"\n  - \"shared workflow doctrine\"\n  - \"sk-code surface detection\"\nimportance_tier: normal\ncontextType: general\nversion: 1.2.5.0\n---\n\n# sk-code shared\n\n> The hub-level doctrine every sk-code mode and surface reads: how a surface is chosen, how the phases run and which quality bar applies. Mode-owned material lives in its mode, never here.\n\n---\n\n## 1. OVERVIEW\n\n`shared/` holds the material that belongs to the `sk-code` parent hub and to no single nested mode. The workflow modes `sk-code-quality` and `sk-code-review` act. The surface packets `sk-code-ci`, `sk-code-frontend` and `sk-code-skilled` supply read-only evidence. All of them read the files below, so each file lives once here instead of being copied into every packet.\n\nThis README is also the hub's zero-signal fallback. [`../hub-router.json`](../hub-router.json) names this file as `routerPolicy.defaultResource`, and the router offers it with the disambiguation checklist when a prompt scores no mode. A scored route never loads it.\n\nThe folder ships reference files, one linter that both workflow modes read their rule overrides through and the registry of rule ids it is checked against, with no assets or graph metadata. Authoritative content lives in the linked files. This page only says what each one is for and who reads it.\n\n---\n\n## 2. STRUCTURE\n\n```text example\nshared/\n+-- README.md                          # this index and the hub's zero-signal fallback\n+-- references/\n|   +-- stack-detection.md             # surface detection, the primary routing key\n|   +-- phase-detection.md             # phase lifecycle and per-surface evidence\n|   +-- workflow-implement.md          # implementation doctrine, symlinked into surfaces\n|   +-- workflow-debug.md              # debugging doctrine, symlinked into surfaces\n|   +-- workflow-verify.md             # verification doctrine, symlinked into surfaces\n"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"command\": \"sed -n '200,230p' .skilled/skills/sk-code/ROUTER.md; echo \\\"===TAIL===\\\"; sed -n '440,470p' .skilled/skills/sk-code/ROUTER.md; echo \\\"===SHARED README 1-40===\\\"; sed -n '1,40p' .skilled/skills/sk-code/shared/README.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1793742519005,"end":1793742519056}}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1793742519074,"sessionID":"ses_e5ca055c6ffeOZO66b0AKuGGW6","part":{"partID":"prt_1a362baa1001Vmv5UroPktn6KI","sessionID":"ses_e5ca055c6ffeOZO66b0AKuGGW6","messageID":"msg_1a3628b2c001ezoo2Jihur8lgf","type":"tool","id":"call_00_sve475lcx0g08k5nqshv3d1c","tool":"shell","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"for f in .skilled/skills/sk-code/ROUTER.md .skilled/skills/sk-code/shared/README.md .skilled/skills/sk-code/shared/references/phase-detection.md .skilled/skills/sk-code/shared/references/stack-detection.md .skilled/skills/sk-code/shared/references/workflow-implement.md .skilled/skills/sk-code/shared/references/workflow-verify.md .skilled/skills/sk-code/shared/references/workflow-debug.md .skilled/skills/sk-code/shared/references/universal/code-quality-standards.md .skilled/skills/sk-code/shared/references/universal/rule-overrides.md; do echo \"=== $f\"; grep -n '^#\\{1,3\\} ' \"$f\"; done"},"output":"=== .skilled/skills/sk-code/ROUTER.md\n16:# sk-code Surface Router — routing logic and resource maps\n22:## 1. OVERVIEW\n24:### Purpose\n28:### When to Use\n35:### Core Principle\n39:### Key Sources\n47:## 2. INTENT MODEL\n51:### Scoring algorithm\n59:### Doc-only edit anti-signals\n75:### Surface-specific intent notes\n82:## 3. LOAD TIERS\n94:## 4. SKILLED MAP\n98:### Implementation shared tier\n105:### Intent overlay\n115:### Language overlay\n126:### Authoring checklist gating\n138:### CI Workflow Map\n151:### Frontend Map\n171:## 5. VERIFICATION COMMANDS\n180:## 6. UNKNOWN FALLBACK\n191:### UNKNOWN fallback checklist\n202:## 7. LOADING DISCIPLINE (DO / DON'T)\n204:### DO\n210:### DON'T\n218:## 8. RELATED RESOURCES\n227:## 9. MACHINE-READABLE ROUTER (replay / benchmark source)\n237:# Always-loaded routing preamble: every code task detects stack, detects phase,\n238:# consults the router, and applies the universal quality baseline before any\n239:# surface-specific resource. These are loaded on every route.\n414:# Hub-level shared controls, declared once here. This list holds every\n415:# contained shared/ path that RESOURCE_MAP references but that is exempt from\n416:# typed-leaf projection, because it has no single packet owner. The root-router\n417:# contract validates each entry and requires RESOURCE_MAP to reference it.\n418:# DEFAULT_RESOURCE above is the other half of the set: its always-loaded\n419:# preamble paths are hub-level controls too. No other shared/ path is a\n420:# control, so a guard that needs the control set reads these two lists.\n429:### Surface-aware loading (route-time)\n441:## 10. HOW TO READ THIS\n450:## 11. MACHINE ROUTER\n467:### Surface Router: per-intent leaf sets\n473:### The discriminator\n484:### Surface detection\n493:### Fallback route\n499:### Routing rule\n=== .skilled/skills/sk-code/shared/README.md\n14:# sk-code shared\n20:## 1. OVERVIEW\n30:## 2. STRUCTURE\n57:## 3. SURFACE ROUTING AND LIFECYCLE\n68:## 4. SHARED WORKFLOW DOCTRINE\n96:## 5. UNIVERSAL STANDARDS\n109:## 6. WHAT BELONGS HERE\n123:## 7. RELATED RESOURCES\n=== .skilled/skills/sk-code/shared/references/phase-detection.md\n14:# Phase Detection and Lifecycle\n20:## 1. OVERVIEW\n22:### Purpose\n26:### When to Use\n33:### Core Principle\n37:### Key Sources\n44:## 2. PHASE MAP\n69:## 3. PER-SURFACE EVIDENCE\n73:### SKILLED (`sk-code-skilled`)\n89:### CI (`sk-code-ci`)\n101:### Frontend (`sk-code-frontend`)\n115:## 4. IRON LAWS\n125:## 5. TRANSITIONS\n141:## 6. RELATED RESOURCES\n=== .skilled/skills/sk-code/shared/references/stack-detection.md\n14:# Router Reference - Code Surface Detection\n20:## 1. OVERVIEW\n22:### Core Principle\n26:### Surfaces\n38:## 2. DETECTION ORDER\n40:### The Rule\n52:### Why SKILLED Is Last\n58:### Declaring a Hub Surface\n80:### Declaring an External Surface\n105:### The Fallback and the Symlink Guard\n117:### Where Detection Runs\n130:## 3. SKILLED LANGUAGE SUB-DETECTION\n151:## 4. TEST CASES\n169:## 5. RELATED RESOURCES\n=== .skilled/skills/sk-code/shared/refer