n21:## 1. OVERVIEW\n23:### Purpose\n27:### Core Principle\n31:### Scope\n35:### Key Sources\n44:## 2. THE SECTION AND ITS ROWS\n51:## Rule overrides\n71:## 3. RULE IDS\n87:## 4. THE HARD FLOOR\n113:## 5. PRECEDENCE\n127:## 6. WHERE A SECTION MAY LIVE\n141:## 7. READING AND RECORDING AN OVERRIDE\n160:## 8. CHECKING A SECTION\n178:## 9. RELATED RESOURCES\n"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"command\": \"for f in .skilled/skills/sk-code/ROUTER.md .skilled/skills/sk-code/shared/README.md .skilled/skills/sk-code/shared/references/phase-detection.md .skilled/skills/sk-code/shared/references/stack-detection.md .skilled/skills/sk-code/shared/references/workflow-implement.md .skilled/skills/sk-code/shared/references/workflow-verify.md .skilled/skills/sk-code/shared/references/workflow-debug.md .skilled/skills/sk-code/shared/references/universal/code-quality-standards.md .skilled/skills/sk-code/shared/references/universal/rule-overrides.md; do echo \\\"=== $f\\\"; grep -n '^#\\\\{1,3\\\\} ' \\\"$f\\\"; done\"}"},"time":{"start":1793742518945,"end":1793742519074}}}}
{"type":"step_finish","timestamp":1793742519745,"sessionID":"ses_e5ca055c6ffeOZO66b0AKuGGW6","part":{"id":"prt_1a362bdc10017MC952BV9rSEVH","sessionID":"ses_e5ca055c6ffeOZO66b0AKuGGW6","messageID":"msg_1a3628b2c001ezoo2Jihur8lgf","type":"step-finish","reason":"tool-calls","snapshot":"c3a54b45009e35532c8c01d01e0b5799195854af","cost":0,"tokens":{"input":2133,"output":345,"reasoning":1509,"cache":{"read":114048,"write":0}}}}
{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1793742521324,"sessionID":"ses_e5ca055c6ffeOZO66b0AKuGGW6","part":{"id":"prt_1a362c3ec001j4n0BchQ6oGPae","sessionID":"ses_e5ca055c6ffeOZO66b0AKuGGW6","messageID":"msg_1a362bdc5001Rp92VcLMdAWtp7","type":"step-start","snapshot":"c3a54b45009e35532c8c01d01e0b5799195854af"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1793742524975,"sessionID":"ses_e5ca055c6ffeOZO66b0AKuGGW6","part":{"partID":"prt_1a362d1bd001nQ9SrN0KRWNrar","sessionID":"ses_e5ca055c6ffeOZO66b0AKuGGW6","messageID":"msg_1a362bdc5001Rp92VcLMdAWtp7","type":"tool","id":"call_00_fmzx0lw1g4k1ibwkqkdsxy25","tool":"shell","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"sed -n '44,72p' .skilled/skills/sk-code/shared/references/universal/rule-overrides.md; echo \"===STACK 20-40===\"; sed -n '20,40p' .skilled/skills/sk-code/shared/references/stack-detection.md; echo \"===IMPL 20-40===\"; sed -n '20,40p' .skilled/skills/sk-code/shared/references/workflow-implement.md"},"output":"## 2. THE SECTION AND ITS ROWS\n\nAn override lives in an optional section of a checklist file. The heading reads `## Rule overrides`. A checklist that numbers its headings in upper case may write `## 7. RULE OVERRIDES`, and the linter reads both. The section runs to the next H1 or H2 heading. A file carries at most one.\n\nThe section holds one table with three columns:\n\n```markdown\n## Rule overrides\n\n| Rule | Action | Reason |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `floor.documentation` | none | Public functions carry no doc comments here |\n| `review.function-length` | replace: A function stays under 60 lines | The team's own limit |\n```\n\n| Column | Required | Meaning |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Rule | Yes | One rule id from section 3. Backticks are optional |\n| Action | Yes | `none` drops the rule. `replace: <text>` puts the text in its place, with the same severity |\n| Reason | No | Why the repository differs. A reviewer reads it when the override is recorded |\n\nA row names a single rule, and a section names each rule once. Write a literal pipe in the replacement text as `\\|`. Prose lines and fenced examples inside the section are ignored.\n\nA row for a machine-checked id (`hygiene.*`, `debug.*`, `dist.*`) changes what the agent and review apply. The runner's own check changes only through `commentHygiene.disableRules` in the repository's quality config. A `replace:` row on a machine-checked id is refused, because no row can rewrite what the runner matches.\n\n---\n\n## 3. RULE IDS\n\n===STACK 20-40===\n## 1. OVERVIEW\n\n### Core Principle\n\nDetect **where the work is happening** before deciding which standards apply. The narrowest declared surface that matches leads, and the hub's own code comes last.\n\n### Surfaces\n\n| Surface | Owns | Does Not Own |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Specific surfaces | The stack each one declares, such as an operator-private `local-*` skill or a surface packet another repository adds to the hub | Work that matches none of their markers |\n| SKILLED | The hub's own code under `.skilled/`: skills, agents, commands, MCP servers, plugins, scripts, tests and JSON or YAML config. It is the one fallback surface | A stack a specific surface declares |\n| UNKNOWN | The result when no declared surface matches | Any standard beyond the universal floor, the quality runner's built-in checks and the checks and checklists the repository's quality config names, each with its overrides applied, until the operator names the surface and its commands. The spec-folder authoring checklist needs the SKILLED surface |\n\n`sk-code-ci` and `sk-code-frontend` are surface packets with no detection block. The hub bundles each from request vocabulary alone, so detection never returns either.\n\n---\n\n## 2. DETECTION ORDER\n\n### The Rule\n===IMPL 20-40===\n## 1. OVERVIEW\n\n### Purpose\n\nImplementation is the one lifecycle phase that builds new behavior. It reads before it writes, lands the smallest correct edit and hands the result on with the facts later phases need. It takes the active surface from the shared router and loads only that surface's resources.\n\n### Core Principle\n\nRead first, change the least and write the baseline down before the first edit. Debugging and verification can only measure a change against the starting state this phase recorded.\n\n### When to Use\n\n- Writing, modifying, refactoring or building code in the active surface.\n- Implementing a feature, script, component, module, fallback, parameter, authoring packet or runtime behavior.\n- Researching unfamiliar, risky, config-sensitive, security-sensitive or multi-file code before changing it.\n- Applying surface authoring patterns once the shared router has resolved the surface.\n- Using a library or framework overlay as peer reference after surface detection, never as a replacement for surface standards.\n\n### When Not to Use\n\n- A concrete failing symptom dominates the task. Use [debugging](./workflow-debug.md).\n","title":"shell","metadata":{"metadata":{"status":"completed","truncated":false,"exit":0},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"## 2. THE SECTION AND ITS ROWS\n\nAn override lives in an optional section of a checklist file. The heading reads `## Rule overrides`. A checklist that numbers its headings in upper case may write `## 7. RULE OVERRIDES`, and the linter reads both. The section runs to the next H1 or H2 heading. A file carries at most one.\n\nThe section holds one table with three columns:\n\n```markdown\n## Rule overrides\n\n| Rule | Action | Reason |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `floor.documentation` | none | Public functions carry no doc comments here |\n| `review.function-length` | replace: A function stays under 60 lines | The team's own limit |\n```\n\n| Column | Required | Meaning |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Rule | Yes | One rule id from section 3. Backticks are optional |\n| Action | Yes | `none` drops the rule. `replace: <text>` puts the text in its place, with the same severity |\n| Reason | No | Why the repository differs. A reviewer reads it when the override is recorded |\n\nA row names a single rule, and a section names each rule once. Write a literal pipe in the replacement text as `\\|`. Prose lines and fenced examples inside the section are ignored.\n\nA row for a machine-checked id (`hygiene.*`, `debug.*`, `dist.*`) changes what the agent and review apply. The runner's own check changes only through `commentHygiene.disableRules` in the repository's quality config. A `replace:` row on a machine-checked id is refused, because no row can rewrite what the runner matches.\n\n---\n\n## 3. RULE IDS\n\n===STACK 20-40===\n## 1. OVERVIEW\n\n### Core Principle\n\nDetect **where the work is happening** before deciding which standards apply. The narrowest declared surface that matches leads, and the hub's own code comes last.\n\n### Surfaces\n\n| Surface | Owns | Does Not Own |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Specific surfaces | The stack each one declares, such as an operator-private `local-*` skill or a surface packet another repository adds to the hub | Work that matches none of their markers |\n| SKILLED | The hub's own code under `.skilled/`: skills, agents, commands, MCP servers, plugins, scripts, tests and JSON or YAML config. It is the one fallback surface | A stack a specific surface declares |\n| UNKNOWN | The result when no declared surface matches | Any standard beyond the universal floor, the quality runner's built-in checks and the checks and checklists the repository's quality config names, each with its overrides applied, until the operator names the surface and its commands. The spec-folder authoring checklist needs the SKILLED surface |\n\n`sk-code-ci` and `sk-code-frontend` are surface packets with no detection block. The hub bundles each from request vocabulary alone, so detection never returns either.\n\n---\n\n## 2. DETECTION ORDER\n\n### The Rule\n===IMPL 20-40===\n## 1. OVERVIEW\n\n### Purpose\n\nImplementation is the one lifecycle phase that builds new behavior. It reads before it writes, lands the smallest correct edit and hands the result on with the facts later phases need. It takes the active surface from the shared router and loads only that surface's resources.\n\n### Core Principle\n\nRead first, change the least and write the baseline down before the first edit. Debugging and verification can only measure a change against the starting state this phase recorded.\n\n### When to Use\n\n- Writing, modifying, refactoring or building code in the active surface.\n- Implementing a feature, script, component, module, fallback, parameter, authoring packet or runtime behavior.\n- Researching unfamiliar, risky, config-sensitive, security-sensitive or multi-file code before changing it.\n- Applying surface authoring patterns once the shared router has resolved the surface.\n- Using a library or framework overlay as peer reference after surface detection, never as a replacement for surface standards.\n\n### When Not to Use\n\n- A concrete failing symptom dominates the task. Use [debugging](./workflow-debug.md).\n"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"command\": \"sed -n '44,72p' .skilled/skills/sk-code/shared/references/universal/rule-overrides.md; echo \\\"===STACK 20-40===\\\"; sed -n '20,40p' .skilled/skills/sk-code/shared/references/stack-detection.md; echo \\\"===IMPL 20-40===\\\"; sed -n '20,40p' .skilled/skills/sk-code/shared/references/workflow-implement.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1793742524861,"end":1793742524975}}}}
