{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1793742544135,"sessionID":"ses_e5ca055c6ffeOZO66b0AKuGGW6","part":{"id":"prt_1a3631d07001m2ezhJquA5rA6m","sessionID":"ses_e5ca055c6ffeOZO66b0AKuGGW6","messageID":"msg_1a363150a001s2TjnBuWFljLUs","type":"step-start","snapshot":"16a8965fd4f0c9c05dbbe47f4da8595c9fc05bca"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1793742559567,"sessionID":"ses_e5ca055c6ffeOZO66b0AKuGGW6","part":{"partID":"prt_1a3635718001360O5U17fL5Sth","sessionID":"ses_e5ca055c6ffeOZO66b0AKuGGW6","messageID":"msg_1a363150a001s2TjnBuWFljLUs","type":"tool","id":"call_00_bigb42678ccebtp8lvtzzkj7","tool":"write","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"content":"# Iteration 1: Template Contract and Hub Root Files\n\n## Focus\n\nEstablish the normative section-order contract from the sk-doc reference and asset templates, then test the hub root files (`SKILL.md`, `ROUTER.md`, `shared/README.md`, `shared/references/**`) against it. This is the baseline every later family audit compares to.\n\n## Actions Taken\n\n1. Read `.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-skill/assets/skill/skill-reference-template.md` §2 (document structure, intro rules, frontmatter) and the section map of both templates.\n2. Read the asset template's document-structure rules, including the \"Related Resources must be last\" clause.\n3. Extracted the heading map of all nine hub `shared/` documents plus the hub `ROUTER.md` and `shared/README.md`.\n4. Ran a 121-file mechanical structural scan (headings, bold-label adjacency, setext hazards, overview bodies, duplicate headings, bare URLs, related-section placement) over the scoped corpus; results staged at `.executor-state/scan.json`.\n5. Read the flagged regions in `ROUTER.md`, `runner-contract.md`, `stack-detection.md`, `phase-detection.md`, `workflow-implement.md`, `workflow-verify.md` and `code-quality-standards.md`.\n\n## Findings\n\n1. The reference template makes the contract explicit: the H1 intro is 1-2 short sentences with no subsections, the first section is `## 1. OVERVIEW` with `### Purpose`, `### When to Use` and `### Core Principle`, and every reference carries six frontmatter fields. Yet the same template teaches duplication: `### When to Create References` under Section 1 restates the whole `## 3. WHEN TO CREATE REFERENCE FILES` section, so the normative document itself models duplicated content. [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-skill/assets/skill/skill-reference-template.md:58] [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-skill/assets/skill/skill-reference-template.md:100] [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-skill/assets/skill/skill-reference-template.md:36] [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-skill/assets/skill/skill-reference-template.md:182]\n\n2. The asset template states the placement rule the corpus violates: \"N. Related Resources (optional, if present must be last)\". The hub `ROUTER.md` breaks it by placing `## 8. RELATED RESOURCES` at line 218 before `## 9. MACHINE-READABLE ROUTER` (227), `## 10. HOW TO READ THIS` (441) and `## 11. MACHINE ROUTER` (450), so the reader's \"where next\" section interrupts the routing content it should close. [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-skill/assets/skill/skill-asset-template.md:166] [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/sk-code/ROUTER.md:218] [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/sk-code/ROUTER.md:227] [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/sk-code/ROUTER.md:450]\n\n3. `sk-code-quality/references/runner-contract.md` opens its body with `## 1. OVERVIEW AND CLI` and then goes straight into a code fence: there is no overview paragraph and no `### Purpose` or `### When to Use`, so the section named \"overview\" contains only the CLI synopsis and option tables. The combined heading is also the accretion pattern the brief names: two topics welded onto one heading instead of an overview followed by a CLI section. [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-quality/references/runner-contract.md:20] [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-quality/references/runner-contract.md:22]\n\n4. `shared/references/stack-detection.md` has an OVERVIEW section with `### Core Principle` and `### Surfaces` only, omitting the template's `### Purpose` and `### When to Use`. A reader meets the detection rule before learning what the file is for or when to load it. [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/sk-code/shared/references/stack-detection.md:20] [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/sk-code/shared/references/stack-detection.md:22] [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/sk-code/shared/references/stack-detection.md:26] [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-skill/assets/skill/skill-reference-template.md:100]\n\n5. OVERVIEW subheading order is not consistent across sibling references, which is the \"items ordered by authoring history\" symptom at section level: `phase-detection.md` runs Purpose (22), When to Use (26), Core Principle (33); `workflow-implement.md` runs Purpose (22), Core Principle (26), When to Use (30); `workflow-verify.md` runs Purpose (22), Iron Law (26), When to Use (32); `code-quality-standards.md` runs Purpose (23), Core Principle (27), When to Use (31). Four files, three orders, and one file replaces Core Principle with Iron Law. [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/sk-code/shared/references/phase-detection.md:22] [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/sk-code/shared/references/workflow-implement.md:22] [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/sk-code/shared/references/workflow-verify.md:22] [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/sk-code/shared/references/universal/code-quality-standards.md:23]\n\n## Ruled Out\n\n- Treating every `firstH2NotOverview` hit on a `SKILL.md` as a template violation: skill bodies follow a different template, so only reference and asset files were scored against the reference/asset contract.\n- Treating long prose lines as rendering defects: they wrap in rendered Markdown, so they were recorded as a style signal, not a finding.\n- Treating `## Rule overrides` in `rule-overrides.md:51` as a stray heading: it sits inside a fenced example, and the fence-aware scan correctly excluded it.\n\n## Dead Ends\n\n- A purely regex-driven \"authoring order\" detector: section order can only be judged against a named reader task, so this iteration compares sibling files against each other and the template rather than inventing a universal order.\n\n## Edge Cases\n\n- Ambiguous input: \"every reference and asset\" excludes `manual-testing-playbook/`, `changelog/`, `benchmark/` and `feature-catalog/`; the scan scope was fixed to `references/`, `assets/`, `shared/`, `SKILL.md` and `ROUTER.md` (121 files).\n- Contradictory evidence: the reference template's GOOD structure puts `When to Use` before `Core Principle` while several hub files put `Core Principle` first; the template wins because it is the stated baseline, but the inconsistency is recorded as a finding rather than a hard error.\n- Missing dependencies: none.\n- Partial success: none; all planned reads succeeded.\n\n## Sources Consulted\n\n- `.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-skill/assets/skill/skill-reference-template.md:36-213`\n- `.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-skill/assets/skill/skill-asset-template.md:166`\n- `.skilled/skills/sk-code/ROUTER.md:16-450`\n- `.skilled/skills/sk-code/shared/references/stack-detection.md:20-40`\n- `.skilled/skills/sk-code/shared/references/phase-detection.md:20-44`\n- `.skilled/skills/sk-code/shared/references/workflow-implement.md:20-40`\n- `.skilled/skills/sk-code/shared/references/workflow-verify.md:20-36`\n- `.skilled/skills/sk-code/shared/references/universal/code-quality-standards.md:21-40`\n- `.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-quality/references/runner-contract.md:14-30`\n- `.skilled/skills/sk-code/shared/README.md:20-123`\n\n## Assessment\n\n- New information ratio: 0.80\n- Questions addressed: template contract; hub root and shared-reference conformance.\n- Questions answered: \"What section-order and container contract does the sk-doc skill-reference template define, and how do the hub SKILL.md, hub ROUTER.md and shared/ references diverge from it?\"\n\n## Reflection\n\n- What worked and why: deriving the contract from the template first made the sibling-order inconsistency visible; without a named baseline, four different OVERVIEW orders all look locally reasonable.\n- What did not work and why: a naive \"first H2 is not OVERVIEW\" rule produced false positives on SKILL.md, because skill bodies use a different template; the scan now separates reference/asset files from skill files.\n- What I would do differently: read the template's own section map before the corpus scan, because the template duplicates its own guidance and that duplication sets the tolerance level for every file it governs.\n\n## Recommended Next Focus\n\nIteration 2: the `sk-code-ci` family (`SKILL.md`, `ROUTER.md`, four references), checking heading shape, section order, item order and the same six defect classes against the contract established here.\n","path":"specs/sk-code/018-frontend-upstream-proposals/010-reference-readability-research/research/lineages/deepseek/iterations/iteration-001.md"},"output":"Created file successfully: specs/sk-code/018-frontend-upstream-proposals/010-reference-readability-research/research/lineages/deepseek/iterations/iteration-001.md","title":"write","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Created file successfully: specs/sk-code/018-frontend-upstream-proposals/010-reference-readability-research/research/lineages/deepseek/iterations/iteration-001.md"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"content\": \"# Iteration 1: Template Contract and Hub Root Files\\n\\n## Focus\\n\\nEstablish the normative section-order contract from the sk-doc reference and asset templates, then test the hub root files (`SKILL.md`, `ROUTER.md`, `shared/README.md`, `shared/references/**`) against it. This is the baseline every later family audit compares to.\\n\\n## Actions Taken\\n\\n1. Read `.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-skill/assets/skill/skill-reference-template.md` §2 (document structure, intro rules, frontmatter) and the section map of both templates.\\n2. Read the asset template's document-structure rules, including the \\\"Related Resources must be last\\\" clause.\\n3. Extracted the heading map of all nine hub `shared/` documents plus the hub `ROUTER.md` and `shared/README.md`.\\n4. Ran a 121-file mechanical structural scan (headings, bold-label adjacency, setext hazards, overview bodies, duplicate headings, bare URLs, related-section placement) over the scoped corpus; results staged at `.executor-state/scan.json`.\\n5. Read the flagged regions in `ROUTER.md`, `runner-contract.md`, `stack-detection.md`, `phase-detection.md`, `workflow-implement.md`, `workflow-verify.md` and `code-quality-standards.md`.\\n\\n## Findings\\n\\n1. The reference template makes the contract explicit: the H1 intro is 1-2 short sentences with no subsections, the first section is `## 1. OVERVIEW` with `### Purpose`, `### When to Use` and `### Core Principle`, and every reference carries six frontmatter fields. Yet the same template teaches duplication: `### When to Create References` under Section 1 restates the whole `## 3. WHEN TO CREATE REFERENCE FILES` section, so the normative document itself models duplicated content. [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-skill/assets/skill/skill-reference-template.md:58] [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-skill/assets/skill/skill-reference-template.md:100] [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-skill/assets/skill/skill-reference-template.md:36] [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-skill/assets/skill/skill-reference-template.md:182]\\n\\n2. The asset template states the placement rule the corpus violates: \\\"N. Related Resources (optional, if present must be last)\\\". The hub `ROUTER.md` breaks it by placing `## 8. RELATED RESOURCES` at line 218 before `## 9. MACHINE-READABLE ROUTER` (227), `## 10. HOW TO READ THIS` (441) and `## 11. MACHINE ROUTER` (450), so the reader's \\\"where next\\\" section interrupts the routing content it should close. [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-skill/assets/skill/skill-asset-template.md:166] [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/sk-code/ROUTER.md:218] [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/sk-code/ROUTER.md:227] [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/sk-code/ROUTER.md:450]\\n\\n3. `sk-code-quality/references/runner-contract.md` opens its body with `## 1. OVERVIEW AND CLI` and then goes straight into a code fence: there is no overview paragraph and no `### Purpose` or `### When to Use`, so the section named \\\"overview\\\" contains only the CLI synopsis and option tables. The combined heading is also the accretion pattern the brief names: two topics welded onto one heading instead of an overview followed by a CLI section. [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-quality/references/runner-contract.md:20] [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-quality/references/runner-contract.md:22]\\n\\n4. `shared/references/stack-detection.md` has an OVERVIEW section with `### Core Principle` and `### Surfaces` only, omitting the template's `### Purpose` and `### When to Use`. A reader meets the detection rule before learning what the file is for or when to load it. [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/sk-code/shared/references/stack-detection.md:20] [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/sk-code/shared/references/stack-detection.md:22] [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/sk-code/shared/references/stack-detection.md:26] [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-skill/assets/skill/skill-reference-template.md:100]\\n\\n5. OVERVIEW subheading order is not consistent across sibling references, which is the \\\"items ordered by authoring history\\\" symptom at section level: `phase-detection.md` runs Purpose (22), When to Use (26), Core Principle (33); `workflow-implement.md` runs Purpose (22), Core Principle (26), When to Use (30); `workflow-verify.md` runs Purpose (22), Iron Law (26), When to Use (32); `code-quality-standards.md` runs Purpose (23), Core Principle (27), When to Use (31). Four files, three orders, and one file replaces Core Principle with Iron Law. [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/sk-code/shared/references/phase-detection.md:22] [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/sk-code/shared/references/workflow-implement.md:22] [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/sk-code/shared/references/workflow-verify.md:22] [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/sk-code/shared/references/universal/code-quality-standards.md:23]\\n\\n## Ruled Out\\n\\n- Treating every `firstH2NotOverview` hit on a `SKILL.md` as a template violation: skill bodies follow a different template, so only reference and asset files were scored against the reference/asset contract.\\n- Treating long prose lines as rendering defects: they wrap in rendered Markdown, so they were recorded as a style signal, not a finding.\\n- Treating `## Rule overrides` in `rule-overrides.md:51` as a stray heading: it sits inside a fenced example, and the fence-aware scan correctly excluded it.\\n\\n## Dead Ends\\n\\n- A purely regex-driven \\\"authoring order\\\" detector: section order can only be judged against a named reader task, so this iteration compares sibling files against each other and the template rather than inventing a universal order.\\n\\n## Edge Cases\\n\\n- Ambiguous input: \\\"every reference and asset\\\" excludes `manual-testing-playbook/`, `changelog/`, `benchmark/` and `feature-catalog/`; the scan scope was fixed to `references/`, `assets/`, `shared/`, `SKILL.md` and `ROUTER.md` (121 files).\\n- Contradictory evidence: the reference template's GOOD structure puts `When to Use` before `Core Principle` while several hub files put `Core Principle` first; the template wins because it is the stated baseline, but the inconsistency is recorded as a finding rather than a hard error.\\n- Missing dependencies: none.\\n- Partial success: none; all planned reads succeeded.\\n\\n## Sources Consulted\\n\\n- `.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-skill/assets/skill/skill-reference-template.md:36-213`\\n- `.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-skill/assets/skill/skill-asset-template.md:166`\\n- `.skilled/skills/sk-code/ROUTER.md:16-450`\\n- `.skilled/skills/sk-code/shared/references/stack-detection.md:20-40`\\n- `.skilled/skills/sk-code/shared/references/phase-detection.md:20-44`\\n- `.skilled/skills/sk-code/shared/references/workflow-implement.md:20-40`\\n- `.skilled/skills/sk-code/shared/references/workflow-verify.md:20-36`\\n- `.skilled/skills/sk-code/shared/references/universal/code-quality-standards.md:21-40`\\n- `.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-quality/references/runner-contract.md:14-30`\\n- `.skilled/skills/sk-code/shared/README.md:20-123`\\n\\n## Assessment\\n\\n- New information ratio: 0.80\\n- Questions addressed: template contract; hub root and shared-reference conformance.\\n- Questions answered: \\\"What section-order and container contract does the sk-doc skill-reference template define, and how do the hub SKILL.md, hub ROUTER.md and shared/ references diverge from it?\\\"\\n\\n## Reflection\\n\\n- What worked and why: deriving the contract from the template first made the sibling-order inconsistency visible; without a named baseline, four different OVERVIEW orders all look locally reasonable.\\n- What did not work and why: a naive \\\"first H2 is not OVERVIEW\\\" rule produced false positives on SKILL.md, because skill bodies use a different template; the scan now separates reference/asset files from skill files.\\n- What I would do differently: read the template's own section map before the corpus scan, because the template duplicates its own guidance and that duplication sets the tolerance level for every file it governs.\\n\\n## Recommended Next Focus\\n\\nIteration 2: the `sk-code-ci` family (`SKILL.md`, `ROUTER.md`, four references), checking heading shape, section order, item order and the same six defect classes against the contract established here.\\n\", \"path\": \"specs/sk-code/018-frontend-upstream-proposals/010-reference-readability-research/research/lineages/deepseek/iterations/iteration-001.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1793742559000,"end":1793742559567}}}}
