{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1793742586571,"sessionID":"ses_e5ca055c6ffeOZO66b0AKuGGW6","part":{"partID":"prt_1a363c27c001qnUXDBGoQJp7QO","sessionID":"ses_e5ca055c6ffeOZO66b0AKuGGW6","messageID":"msg_1a363b6a2001DRGLXhpqs8m1nn","type":"tool","id":"call_01_eg7uhd3ruuw9vd3brxrpgvpf","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":".skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-ci/references/job-shape-rules.md"},"output":"Read file .skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-ci/references/job-shape-rules.md, lines 1-137\n1: ---\n2: title: Job Shape Rules\n3: description: Timeout sizing, least-privilege permissions, the concurrency template, the composite setup action and the switch condition shape for a GitHub Actions job.\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"workflow timeout\"\n6:   - \"workflow permissions\"\n7:   - \"workflow concurrency\"\n8:   - \"composite setup action\"\n9:   - \"ci switch condition\"\n10: importance_tier: normal\n11: contextType: general\n12: version: 1.1.1.0\n13: ---\n14: \n15: # Job Shape Rules\n16: \n17: What every job carries beyond its steps: a timeout, a permissions scope, a concurrency group, a shared provisioning step and a switch condition.\n18: \n19: ---\n20: \n21: ## 1. OVERVIEW\n22: \n23: Five parts of a job sit here: its timeout, its permissions, its concurrency group, the composite setup action that provisions it and the switch condition that can turn it off. Each part states the rule, the file and line that show it and what enforces it.\n24: \n25: The shape of each part is universal. The values a repository fills in, its timeout floor, its setup action and its switch variables, come from the repository CI profile described in `references/protected-set.md`. A line marked Skilled example shows this repository's value and holds nowhere else.\n26: \n27: \n28: ---\n29: \n30: ## 2. TIMEOUTS\n31: \n32: A job with no `timeout-minutes` runs until GitHub stops it at 360 minutes, so a hang costs six hours of runner time. Size the timeout from measured runs and never guess it.\n33: \n34: **Formula.** `max(<floor>, ceil(3 x job p50 minutes), ceil(2 x job p100 minutes))` with a floor of 10 minutes. Pull at least 40 recent runs of job-level timings (`gh run view --json jobs`), compute p50 and p100 per job and state the observed p100 next to the chosen value in the commit or the workflow comment. A successful job in the sample should never exceed half of its new timeout.\n35: \n36: **Today.** `44 of 44` jobs in the 32 workflows carry a timeout, on one job-level line each. `35` of them sit at the 10 minute floor. The other **9 jobs** sit above it.\n37: \n38: | Workflow | Job | Line | Value |\n39: |---|---|---|---|\n40: | `skill-doc-frontmatter.yml` | `doc-version-raise` | `:69` | 15 |\n41: | `message-contract.yml` | `message-contract` | `:25` | 20 |\n42: | `changed-packet-validation.yml` | `changed-packets` | `:21` | 20 |\n43: | `skill-advisor-stress.yml` | `skill-advisor-stress` | `:53` | 25 |\n44: | `advisory-checks.yml` | `advisory` | `:29` | 28 |\n45: | `deep-loop-runtime.yml` | `deep-loop-runtime` | `:41` | 34 |\n46: | `routing-registry-drift.yml` | `golden-prompt-gate` | `:258` | 37 |\n47: | `spec-kit-check.yml` | `check` | `:77` | 51 |\n48: | `strict-pass-freshness-report.yml` | `strict-pass-freshness` | `:30` | 143 |\n49: \n50: Enforcement: honour-system. Re-run `grep -n 'timeout-minutes' .github/workflows/*.yml` to refresh the table.\n51: \n52: ---\n53: \n54: ## 3. PERMISSIONS\n55: \n56: Least privilege is the default. A workflow declares `permissions: contents: read` at the top, and a job widens it only for what that job needs.\n57: \n58: - **Read-only workflow.** `permissions: contents: read` written as a block, as in `.github/workflows/message-contract.yml:13`.\n59: - **No scope at the workflow level.** `permissions: {}` at the top and a scope on each job, as in `.github/workflows/trigger-index-rebuild.yml:12`. The rebuild job then asks for `contents: read` at `:40` and the push job asks for `actions: read` and `contents: write` at `:195-196`.\n60: - **A write scope belongs only to a job that runs no repository code.** The rebuild job installs, builds and runs the repository's generator, so it holds no write scope and no secret. The push job runs `gh` and `git` only and is the one place the push token is visible. Splitting the two keeps repository code away from the token.\n61: - **A job that holds a write scope, or pushes, checks out with `persist-credentials: false`.** The checkout then leaves no token in the git config for a later step to reuse, and the job passes its token only to the command that needs it, as in `.github/workflows/trigger-index-rebuild.yml:203`. Enforcement: honour-system.\n62: \n63: Today all 32 workflows declare `permissions:` at the top. Thirty grant `contents: read` and nothing else. `strict-pass-freshness-report.yml` adds `actions: read` at `:19` and `trigger-index-rebuild.yml` grants nothing at the top. Four jobs set a scope of their own: the two jobs of `trigger-index-rebuild.yml` above, the `metadata` job of `dependabot-auto-merge.yml` with `contents: read` and `pull-requests: read` at `:20-21` and its `auto-merge` job with `contents: write` and `pull-requests: write` at `:41-42`.\n64: \n65: Enforcement: honour-system.\n66: \n67: ---\n68: \n69: ## 4. CONCURRENCY\n70: \n71: A workflow that answers to pushes and pull requests carries one group, so a newer push does not leave an older run burning minutes.\n72: \n73: ```yaml\n74: concurrency:\n75:   group: ${{ github.workflow }}-${{ github.event.pull_request.number || github.ref }}\n76:   cancel-in-progress: ${{ github.event_name == 'pull_request' }}\n77: ```\n78: \n79: The recommended default cancels a superseded run on a pull request and never cancels a run on a branch push. The alternative is `cancel-in-progress: true` on main as well, which saves more runner minutes and leaves some commits on the branch with no completed run.\n80: \n81: A workflow whose runs must queue instead of cancel sets `cancel-in-progress: false` and says why. `.github/workflows/trigger-index-rebuild.yml:21-23` does, because a newer pending run replaces an older pending one and the run for the newest push is never dropped.\n82: \n83: Today 32 of 32 workflows carry a `concurrency:` block. Thirty use the pull-request-only value above. Two use `false`: the rebuild workflow, and `dependabot-auto-merge.yml`, because a cancelled run could stop before it turns auto-merge on.\n84: \n85: Enforcement: honour-system.\n86: \n87: ---\n88: \n89: ## 5. THE COMPOSITE SETUP ACTION\n90: \n91: status: landed\n92: \n93: Every workflow that needs the repository's dependency trees calls one composite action instead of repeating the install and build steps. The profile's `setupAction` names it, and the job template adds the step only when the profile does. Everything below in this section is the Skilled example.\n94: \n95: ```yaml\n96: - name: Provision the dependency trees\n97:   uses: ./.github/actions/setup\n98: ```\n99: \n100: The action is `.github/actions/setup/action.yml`. The rules it follows:\n101: \n102: - **The key covers every lockfile plus the Node major.** The cache key is built from the runner OS, the Node major read from the runner, the bun version and a hash of every lockfile and package manifest the trees install from. A change to any of them is a different key.\n103: - **No `restore-keys`.** A partial match would restore a tree built from another lockfile and pass a stale install. A miss means a cold provision.\n104: - **The provisioning walk runs on a hit.** The repository's own `provision_worktree` still runs after a restore and checks that each tree and each build output exists, so a hit shortens the install and is never a version check.\n105: - **The cache is saved only on a miss**, and an input named `cache` set to `false` forces a cold provision so the install path stays exercised.\n106: - **Bun comes from a SHA-pinned setup action**, never from an `npm install` of bun in the workflow.\n107: - **The scan reads it.** `.github/scripts/check-gate-inputs.sh:499-503` (the `if [[ -d \"$ROOT/.github/actions\" ]]` block) scans `.github/actions/**` with the workflow parser, so a path in an action is held to the twin rule.\n108: \n109: Today five jobs in three workflows call the action: `opencode-dual-version.yml` at `:113` and `:192`, `opencode-plugin-tests.yml` at `:92` and `routing-registry-drift.yml` at `:141` and `:248`. A new provisioning workflow calls the action and does not copy its steps.\n110: \n111: ---\n112: \n113: ## 6. THE SWITCH IF SHAPE\n114: \n115: A job can be switched off from repository variables with one condition between `name:` and `runs-on:`. With no variable set, everything runs. A switch is any repository variable the condition compares against `off`, so `/doctor:ci report` reads any repository's names. The profile's `switch.master` and `switch.groups` name them, and the example below uses neutral names.\n116: \n117: ```yaml\n118: jobs:\n119:   example:\n120:     name: Example job\n121:     if: vars.ACME_CI != 'off' && vars.ACME_CI_TESTS != 'off'\n122:     runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n123: ```\n124: \n125: Skilled example: `if: vars.SKILLED_CI != 'off' && vars.SKILLED_CI_FRESHNESS != 'off'`.\n126: \n127: - The condition is **job-level**, because `on:` filters see no contexts.\n128: - Where a job already has a condition, the switch is ANDed onto it and never replaces it.\n129: - `off` is the only disable token. `0`, `false` or a typo leaves the job on. That is fail-safe and must be documented wherever the variables are described.\n130: - A job whose status context the branch ruleset requires is never switched off, and the other protected workflows follow `references/protected-set.md`.\n131: - A workflow in the profile's `switch.exempt` gets no switch. When `switch.required` is true, every other job carries one.\n132: - Skilled example: `SKILLED_CI` is the master switch. The five group switches are `SKILLED_CI_TESTS`, `SKILLED_CI_REPORTS`, `SKILLED_CI_REVIEW`, `SKILLED_CI_FRESHNESS` and `SKILLED_CI_MESSAGES`.\n133: - Skilled example: `message-contract.yml` holds the required context, and `dependabot-auto-merge.yml` is the exempt workflow.\n134: \n135: Skilled example: the shape is the rule. Today 42 of the 44 jobs carry it, and all 42 test `SKILLED_CI`. The group variables split as 24 on `SKILLED_CI_TESTS`, 11 on `SKILLED_CI_FRESHNESS`, 3 on `SKILLED_CI_REVIEW`, 3 on `SKILLED_CI_REPORTS` and 1 on `SKILLED_CI_MESSAGES`. Three jobs AND the switch onto an existing condition: `skill-doc-frontmatter.yml:67`, `trigger-index-rebuild.yml:33` and `trigger-index-rebuild.yml:189`. The two jobs without the switch are the `metadata` and `auto-merge` jobs of `dependabot-auto-merge.yml`, so a reader who finds no switch there is reading the current tree and not a gap in this file.\n136: \n137: Enforcement: honour-system.","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file .skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-ci/references/job-shape-rules.md, lines 1-137\n1: ---\n2: title: Job Shape Rules\n3: description: Timeout sizing, least-privilege permissions, the concurrency template, the composite setup action and the switch condition shape for a GitHub Actions job.\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"workflow timeout\"\n6:   - \"workflow permissions\"\n7:   - \"workflow concurrency\"\n8:   - \"composite setup action\"\n9:   - \"ci switch condition\"\n10: importance_tier: normal\n11: contextType: general\n12: version: 1.1.1.0\n13: ---\n14: \n15: # Job Shape Rules\n16: \n17: What every job carries beyond its steps: a timeout, a permissions scope, a concurrency group, a shared provisioning step and a switch condition.\n18: \n19: ---\n20: \n21: ## 1. OVERVIEW\n22: \n23: Five parts of a job sit here: its timeout, its permissions, its concurrency group, the composite setup action that provisions it and the switch condition that can turn it off. Each part states the rule, the file and line that show it and what enforces it.\n24: \n25: The shape of each part is universal. The values a repository fills in, its timeout floor, its setup action and its switch variables, come from the repository CI profile described in `references/protected-set.md`. A line marked Skilled example shows this repository's value and holds nowhere else.\n26: \n27: \n28: ---\n29: \n30: ## 2. TIMEOUTS\n31: \n32: A job with no `timeout-minutes` runs until GitHub stops it at 360 minutes, so a hang costs six hours of runner time. Size the timeout from measured runs and never guess it.\n33: \n34: **Formula.** `max(<floor>, ceil(3 x job p50 minutes), ceil(2 x job p100 minutes))` with a floor of 10 minutes. Pull at least 40 recent runs of job-level timings (`gh run view --json jobs`), compute p50 and p100 per job and state the observed p100 next to the chosen value in the commit or the workflow comment. A successful job in the sample should never exceed half of its new timeout.\n35: \n36: **Today.** `44 of 44` jobs in the 32 workflows carry a timeout, on one job-level line each. `35` of them sit at the 10 minute floor. The other **9 jobs** sit above it.\n37: \n38: | Workflow | Job | Line | Value |\n39: |---|---|---|---|\n40: | `skill-doc-frontmatter.yml` | `doc-version-raise` | `:69` | 15 |\n41: | `message-contract.yml` | `message-contract` | `:25` | 20 |\n42: | `changed-packet-validation.yml` | `changed-packets` | `:21` | 20 |\n43: | `skill-advisor-stress.yml` | `skill-advisor-stress` | `:53` | 25 |\n44: | `advisory-checks.yml` | `advisory` | `:29` | 28 |\n45: | `deep-loop-runtime.yml` | `deep-loop-runtime` | `:41` | 34 |\n46: | `routing-registry-drift.yml` | `golden-prompt-gate` | `:258` | 37 |\n47: | `spec-kit-check.yml` | `check` | `:77` | 51 |\n48: | `strict-pass-freshness-report.yml` | `strict-pass-freshness` | `:30` | 143 |\n49: \n50: Enforcement: honour-system. Re-run `grep -n 'timeout-minutes' .github/workflows/*.yml` to refresh the table.\n51: \n52: ---\n53: \n54: ## 3. PERMISSIONS\n55: \n56: Least privilege is the default. A workflow declares `permissions: contents: read` at the top, and a job widens it only for what that job needs.\n57: \n58: - **Read-only workflow.** `permissions: contents: read` written as a block, as in `.github/workflows/message-contract.yml:13`.\n59: - **No scope at the workflow level.** `permissions: {}` at the top and a scope on each job, as in `.github/workflows/trigger-index-rebuild.yml:12`. The rebuild job then asks for `contents: read` at `:40` and the push job asks for `actions: read` and `contents: write` at `:195-196`.\n60: - **A write scope belongs only to a job that runs no repository code.** The rebuild job installs, builds and runs the repository's generator, so it holds no write scope and no secret. The push job runs `gh` and `git` only and is the one place the push token is visible. Splitting the two keeps repository code away from the token.\n61: - **A job that holds a write scope, or pushes, checks out with `persist-credentials: false`.** The checkout then leaves no token in the git config for a later step to reuse, and the job passes its token only to the command that needs it, as in `.github/workflows/trigger-index-rebuild.yml:203`. Enforcement: honour-system.\n62: \n63: Today all 32 workflows declare `permissions:` at the top. Thirty grant `contents: read` and nothing else. `strict-pass-freshness-report.yml` adds `actions: read` at `:19` and `trigger-index-rebuild.yml` grants nothing at the top. Four jobs set a scope of their own: the two jobs of `trigger-index-rebuild.yml` above, the `metadata` job of `dependabot-auto-merge.yml` with `contents: read` and `pull-requests: read` at `:20-21` and its `auto-merge` job with `contents: write` and `pull-requests: write` at `:41-42`.\n64: \n65: Enforcement: honour-system.\n66: \n67: ---\n68: \n69: ## 4. CONCURRENCY\n70: \n71: A workflow that answers to pushes and pull requests carries one group, so a newer push does not leave an older run burning minutes.\n72: \n73: ```yaml\n74: concurrency:\n75:   group: ${{ github.workflow }}-${{ github.event.pull_request.number || github.ref }}\n76:   cancel-in-progress: ${{ github.event_name == 'pull_request' }}\n77: ```\n78: \n79: The recommended default cancels a superseded run on a pull request and never cancels a run on a branch push. The alternative is `cancel-in-progress: true` on main as well, which saves more runner minutes and leaves some commits on the branch with no completed run.\n80: \n81: A workflow whose runs must queue instead of cancel sets `cancel-in-progress: false` and says why. `.github/workflows/trigger-index-rebuild.yml:21-23` does, because a newer pending run replaces an older pending one and the run for the newest push is never dropped.\n82: \n83: Today 32 of 32 workflows carry a `concurrency:` block. Thirty use the pull-request-only value above. Two use `false`: the rebuild workflow, and `dependabot-auto-merge.yml`, because a cancelled run could stop before it turns auto-merge on.\n84: \n85: Enforcement: honour-system.\n86: \n87: ---\n88: \n89: ## 5. THE COMPOSITE SETUP ACTION\n90: \n91: status: landed\n92: \n93: Every workflow that needs the repository's dependency trees calls one composite action instead of repeating the install and build steps. The profile's `setupAction` names it, and the job template adds the step only when the profile does. Everything below in this section is the Skilled example.\n94: \n95: ```yaml\n96: - name: Provision the dependency trees\n97:   uses: ./.github/actions/setup\n98: ```\n99: \n100: The action is `.github/actions/setup/action.yml`. The rules it follows:\n101: \n102: - **The key covers every lockfile plus the Node major.** The cache key is built from the runner OS, the Node major read from the runner, the bun version and a hash of every lockfile and package manifest the trees install from. A change to any of them is a different key.\n103: - **No `restore-keys`.** A partial match would restore a tree built from another lockfile and pass a stale install. A miss means a cold provision.\n104: - **The provisioning walk runs on a hit.** The repository's own `provision_worktree` still runs after a restore and checks that each tree and each build output exists, so a hit shortens the install and is never a version check.\n105: - **The cache is saved only on a miss**, and an input named `cache` set to `false` forces a cold provision so the install path stays exercised.\n106: - **Bun comes from a SHA-pinned setup action**, never from an `npm install` of bun in the workflow.\n107: - **The scan reads it.** `.github/scripts/check-gate-inputs.sh:499-503` (the `if [[ -d \"$ROOT/.github/actions\" ]]` block) scans `.github/actions/**` with the workflow parser, so a path in an action is held to the twin rule.\n108: \n109: Today five jobs in three workflows call the action: `opencode-dual-version.yml` at `:113` and `:192`, `opencode-plugin-tests.yml` at `:92` and `routing-registry-drift.yml` at `:141` and `:248`. A new provisioning workflow calls the action and does not copy its steps.\n110: \n111: ---\n112: \n113: ## 6. THE SWITCH IF SHAPE\n114: \n115: A job can be switched off from repository variables with one condition between `name:` and `runs-on:`. With no variable set, everything runs. A switch is any repository variable the condition compares against `off`, so `/doctor:ci report` reads any repository's names. The profile's `switch.master` and `switch.groups` name them, and the example below uses neutral names.\n116: \n117: ```yaml\n118: jobs:\n119:   example:\n120:     name: Example job\n121:     if: vars.ACME_CI != 'off' && vars.ACME_CI_TESTS != 'off'\n122:     runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n123: ```\n124: \n125: Skilled example: `if: vars.SKILLED_CI != 'off' && vars.SKILLED_CI_FRESHNESS != 'off'`.\n126: \n127: - The condition is **job-level**, because `on:` filters see no contexts.\n128: - Where a job already has a condition, the switch is ANDed onto it and never replaces it.\n129: - `off` is the only disable token. `0`, `false` or a typo leaves the job on. That is fail-safe and must be documented wherever the variables are described.\n130: - A job whose status context the branch ruleset requires is never switched off, and the other protected workflows follow `references/protected-set.md`.\n131: - A workflow in the profile's `switch.exempt` gets no switch. When `switch.required` is true, every other job carries one.\n132: - Skilled example: `SKILLED_CI` is the master switch. The five group switches are `SKILLED_CI_TESTS`, `SKILLED_CI_REPORTS`, `SKILLED_CI_REVIEW`, `SKILLED_CI_FRESHNESS` and `SKILLED_CI_MESSAGES`.\n133: - Skilled example: `message-contract.yml` holds the required context, and `dependabot-auto-merge.yml` is the exempt workflow.\n134: \n135: Skilled example: the shape is the rule. Today 42 of the 44 jobs carry it, and all 42 test `SKILLED_CI`. The group variables split as 24 on `SKILLED_CI_TESTS`, 11 on `SKILLED_CI_FRESHNESS`, 3 on `SKILLED_CI_REVIEW`, 3 on `SKILLED_CI_REPORTS` and 1 on `SKILLED_CI_MESSAGES`. Three jobs AND the switch onto an existing condition: `skill-doc-frontmatter.yml:67`, `trigger-index-rebuild.yml:33` and `trigger-index-rebuild.yml:189`. The two jobs without the switch are the `metadata` and `auto-merge` jobs of `dependabot-auto-merge.yml`, so a reader who finds no switch there is reading the current tree and not a gap in this file.\n136: \n137: Enforcement: honour-system."}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\": \".skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-ci/references/job-shape-rules.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1793742586492,"end":1793742586571}}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1793742586572,"sessionID":"ses_e5ca055c6ffeOZO66b0AKuGGW6","part":{"partID":"prt_1a363c263001jpngWIz26ljnf9","sessionID":"ses_e5ca055c6ffeOZO66b0AKuGGW6","messageID":"msg_1a363b6a2001DRGLXhpqs8m1nn","type":"tool","id":"call_00_ejmnado34zkmi96uxgd6pqv8","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":".skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-ci/references/ci-workflow-standards.md"},"output":"Read file .skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-ci/references/ci-workflow-standards.md, lines 1-67\n1: ---\n2: title: CI Workflow Standards - Rule Index\n3: description: The index of the repository's GitHub Actions workflow rules, each with the file and line that prove it and whether a machine gate enforces it.\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"ci workflow standards\"\n6:   - \"github actions workflow rules\"\n7:   - \"workflow rule index\"\n8:   - \"sha pinned actions\"\n9: importance_tier: normal\n10: contextType: general\n11: version: 1.1.0.0\n12: ---\n13: \n14: # CI Workflow Standards - Rule Index\n15: \n16: The normative list of rules the repository's workflows follow, with the evidence behind each and an honest note on what enforces it.\n17: \n18: ---\n19: \n20: ## 1. OVERVIEW\n21: \n22: ### Purpose\n23: \n24: A reader lands here first, then follows a row to the detailed file. The counts are the values read on the tree when this packet was written. Re-run the quoted command before relying on one, because the workflow files change faster than this index.\n25: \n26: ### Universal Rules and Repository Values\n27: \n28: Each statement is universal. The names it applies to, the twin roots, the switch variables, the branches and the README tables, come from the repository CI profile described in `references/protected-set.md`. The evidence and the counts are read from this repository, so a line marked Skilled example shows this repository's value and holds nowhere else.\n29: \n30: ### Reading the Enforcement Column\n31: \n32: A row says **machine gate** when a script or workflow fails on a violation. It says **honour-system** when nothing checks the rule, so only a reviewer who knows it will catch a lapse. A rule with no enforcement is honour-system, and this index says so rather than implying a check exists.\n33: \n34: ---\n35: \n36: ## 2. THE RULES\n37: \n38: | Rule | Statement | Followed today | Evidence | Enforcement |\n39: |---|---|---|---|---|\n40: | Path parity | When the profile lists path twins, every path filter and hook input that names one root names its twin. Skilled example: `.skilled/` and `.opencode/` stay mirrored | Yes | `.github/scripts/check-gate-inputs.sh:112-124` (the twin matcher), run by `.github/workflows/gate-inputs.yml` | Machine gate. The only workflow rule one enforces |\n41: | SHA-pinned `uses:` | Every remote action is pinned to a full 40-character commit SHA with the release tag as a trailing comment | 32 of 32 workflows, 89 remote `uses:` lines | `.github/workflows/advisory-checks.yml:31` (`actions/checkout@3d3c42e5aac5ba805825da76410c181273ba90b1 # v7.0.1`). Dependabot keeps the pins current through the `github-actions` ecosystem in `.github/dependabot.yml:33` | Honour-system. Dependabot refreshes a pin but nothing rejects an unpinned one |\n42: | README row per workflow | When the profile names a workflows README, every file in `.github/workflows/` has one row in each table its `rowTables` lists. Skilled example: the contents table and the push versus pull-request table | 32 of 32 | `.github/workflows/README.md:26-57` (contents) and `.github/workflows/README.md:72` (the push versus pull-request row for `message-contract.yml`) | Honour-system |\n43: | `set -euo pipefail` | A multi-line `run:` block starts with `set -euo pipefail`. A report-only step that must keep going on a failure uses `set -uo pipefail` and says why | 29 of the 30 workflow files with a multi-line `run:` carry `set -euo pipefail`. Eight steps are deliberately `-uo`: `.github/workflows/advisory-checks.yml:39`, `:53` and `:70` (report-only steps), `.github/workflows/spec-kit-check.yml:143-144` (two background vitest processes, each wait collected into `status`), `.github/workflows/strict-pass-freshness-report.yml:60` and `:110` (baseline fetch and sweep keep going to record a report), `.github/workflows/trigger-index-rebuild.yml:74` and `:283` (commit and push steps handle their own exit codes) | `.github/workflows/message-contract.yml:81` (`set -euo pipefail`), `.github/workflows/advisory-checks.yml:39` | Honour-system here. The shell doctrine is owned by `sk-code-skilled` |\n44: | Fork and secret safety | No `pull_request_target` trigger. A free-text event field (branch name, body, title, commit message) reaches a script only through `env:`, never interpolated into `run:`. Only hex SHAs and numbers are interpolated in a script | Yes. No workflow uses `pull_request_target` | `.github/workflows/message-contract.yml:72-79` (head ref and body passed through `env:`) | Honour-system |\n45: | Matrix and `fail-fast` | A matrix whose entries report independently sets `fail-fast: false`, so one failing entry does not cancel the others | Both matrix workflows set it | `.github/workflows/pi-extension-tests.yml:61` and `.github/workflows/opencode-dual-version.yml:92` | Honour-system |\n46: | Trigger and filter shape | Generated-path rules are push-only, a rebuild commit skips the downstream workflows and one trigger is a recorded exception | Yes | `references/trigger-and-filter-rules.md` | Mixed. See that file |\n47: | Job shape | Timeouts, least-privilege permissions, the concurrency template, the composite setup action and the switch condition | Partly. Each row in `references/job-shape-rules.md` states its own count | `references/job-shape-rules.md` | Honour-system |\n48: | Protected workflows | A workflow protected by function is never edited or switched off without a second named confirmation | Yes | `references/protected-set.md` | Honour-system |\n49: \n50: ---\n51: \n52: ## 3. HOW TO APPLY THE INDEX\n53: \n54: 1. Find the rule row for the change. A new workflow touches every row, so start from `assets/workflow-template.yml` instead.\n55: 2. Re-run the quoted command or open the cited line. A count that no longer matches means the tree moved, so trust the tree and refresh this file.\n56: 3. Where the enforcement is honour-system, check the rule by hand, because a green CI run proves nothing about it.\n57: 4. Where the change reaches a protected workflow, stop and follow `references/protected-set.md`.\n58: \n59: ### Quick Checks\n60: \n61: ```bash\n62: grep -hE '^\\s*-?\\s*uses: ' .github/workflows/*.yml | grep -v 'uses: \\./' | grep -vE '@[0-9a-f]{40}( |$)'\n63: for f in .github/workflows/*.yml; do grep -qF \"$(basename \"$f\")\" .github/workflows/README.md || echo \"no row: $f\"; done  # Skilled example README path\n64: grep -l 'pull_request_target' .github/workflows/*.yml\n65: ```\n66: \n67: The first prints nothing when every remote action is pinned. The second prints a line for each workflow that has no row in the workflow README. The third prints nothing when no workflow uses `pull_request_target`.","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file .skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-ci/references/ci-workflow-standards.md, lines 1-67\n1: ---\n2: title: CI Workflow Standards - Rule Index\n3: description: The index of the repository's GitHub Actions workflow rules, each with the file and line that prove it and whether a machine gate enforces it.\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"ci workflow standards\"\n6:   - \"github actions workflow rules\"\n7:   - \"workflow rule index\"\n8:   - \"sha pinned actions\"\n9: importance_tier: normal\n10: contextType: general\n11: version: 1.1.0.0\n12: ---\n13: \n14: # CI Workflow Standards - Rule Index\n15: \n16: The normative list of rules the repository's workflows follow, with the evidence behind each and an honest note on what enforces it.\n17: \n18: ---\n19: \n20: ## 1. OVERVIEW\n21: \n22: ### Purpose\n23: \n24: A reader lands here first, then follows a row to the detailed file. The counts are the values read on the tree when this packet was written. Re-run the quoted command before relying on one, because the workflow files change faster than this index.\n25: \n26: ### Universal Rules and Repository Values\n27: \n28: Each statement is universal. The names it applies to, the twin roots, the switch variables, the branches and the README tables, come from the repository CI profile described in `references/protected-set.md`. The evidence and the counts are read from this repository, so a line marked Skilled example shows this repository's value and holds nowhere else.\n29: \n30: ### Reading the Enforcement Column\n31: \n32: A row says **machine gate** when a script or workflow fails on a violation. It says **honour-system** when nothing checks the rule, so only a reviewer who knows it will catch a lapse. A rule with no enforcement is honour-system, and this index says so rather than implying a check exists.\n33: \n34: ---\n35: \n36: ## 2. THE RULES\n37: \n38: | Rule | Statement | Followed today | Evidence | Enforcement |\n39: |---|---|---|---|---|\n40: | Path parity | When the profile lists path twins, every path filter and hook input that names one root names its twin. Skilled example: `.skilled/` and `.opencode/` stay mirrored | Yes | `.github/scripts/check-gate-inputs.sh:112-124` (the twin matcher), run by `.github/workflows/gate-inputs.yml` | Machine gate. The only workflow rule one enforces |\n41: | SHA-pinned `uses:` | Every remote action is pinned to a full 40-character commit SHA with the release tag as a trailing comment | 32 of 32 workflows, 89 remote `uses:` lines | `.github/workflows/advisory-checks.yml:31` (`actions/checkout@3d3c42e5aac5ba805825da76410c181273ba90b1 # v7.0.1`). Dependabot keeps the pins current through the `github-actions` ecosystem in `.github/dependabot.yml:33` | Honour-system. Dependabot refreshes a pin but nothing rejects an unpinned one |\n42: | README row per workflow | When the profile names a workflows README, every file in `.github/workflows/` has one row in each table its `rowTables` lists. Skilled example: the contents table and the push versus pull-request table | 32 of 32 | `.github/workflows/README.md:26-57` (contents) and `.github/workflows/README.md:72` (the push versus pull-request row for `message-contract.yml`) | Honour-system |\n43: | `set -euo pipefail` | A multi-line `run:` block starts with `set -euo pipefail`. A report-only step that must keep going on a failure uses `set -uo pipefail` and says why | 29 of the 30 workflow files with a multi-line `run:` carry `set -euo pipefail`. Eight steps are deliberately `-uo`: `.github/workflows/advisory-checks.yml:39`, `:53` and `:70` (report-only steps), `.github/workflows/spec-kit-check.yml:143-144` (two background vitest processes, each wait collected into `status`), `.github/workflows/strict-pass-freshness-report.yml:60` and `:110` (baseline fetch and sweep keep going to record a report), `.github/workflows/trigger-index-rebuild.yml:74` and `:283` (commit and push steps handle their own exit codes) | `.github/workflows/message-contract.yml:81` (`set -euo pipefail`), `.github/workflows/advisory-checks.yml:39` | Honour-system here. The shell doctrine is owned by `sk-code-skilled` |\n44: | Fork and secret safety | No `pull_request_target` trigger. A free-text event field (branch name, body, title, commit message) reaches a script only through `env:`, never interpolated into `run:`. Only hex SHAs and numbers are interpolated in a script | Yes. No workflow uses `pull_request_target` | `.github/workflows/message-contract.yml:72-79` (head ref and body passed through `env:`) | Honour-system |\n45: | Matrix and `fail-fast` | A matrix whose entries report independently sets `fail-fast: false`, so one failing entry does not cancel the others | Both matrix workflows set it | `.github/workflows/pi-extension-tests.yml:61` and `.github/workflows/opencode-dual-version.yml:92` | Honour-system |\n46: | Trigger and filter shape | Generated-path rules are push-only, a rebuild commit skips the downstream workflows and one trigger is a recorded exception | Yes | `references/trigger-and-filter-rules.md` | Mixed. See that file |\n47: | Job shape | Timeouts, least-privilege permissions, the concurrency template, the composite setup action and the switch condition | Partly. Each row in `references/job-shape-rules.md` states its own count | `references/job-shape-rules.md` | Honour-system |\n48: | Protected workflows | A workflow protected by function is never edited or switched off without a second named confirmation | Yes | `references/protected-set.md` | Honour-system |\n49: \n50: ---\n51: \n52: ## 3. HOW TO APPLY THE INDEX\n53: \n54: 1. Find the rule row for the change. A new workflow touches every row, so start from `assets/workflow-template.yml` instead.\n55: 2. Re-run the quoted command or open the cited line. A count that no longer matches means the tree moved, so trust the tree and refresh this file.\n56: 3. Where the enforcement is honour-system, check the rule by hand, because a green CI run proves nothing about it.\n57: 4. Where the change reaches a protected workflow, stop and follow `references/protected-set.md`.\n58: \n59: ### Quick Checks\n60: \n61: ```bash\n62: grep -hE '^\\s*-?\\s*uses: ' .github/workflows/*.yml | grep -v 'uses: \\./' | grep -vE '@[0-9a-f]{40}( |$)'\n63: for f in .github/workflows/*.yml; do grep -qF \"$(basename \"$f\")\" .github/workflows/README.md || echo \"no row: $f\"; done  # Skilled example README path\n64: grep -l 'pull_request_target' .github/workflows/*.yml\n65: ```\n66: \n67: The first prints nothing when every remote action is pinned. The second prints a line for each workflow that has no row in the workflow README. The third prints nothing when no workflow uses `pull_request_target`."}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\": \".skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-ci/references/ci-workflow-standards.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1793742586467,"end":1793742586572}}}}
{"type":"step_finish","timestamp":1793742587238,"sessionID":"ses_e5ca055c6ffeOZO66b0AKuGGW6","part":{"id":"prt_1a363c566001oS5aO3NiUJUK61","sessionID":"ses_e5ca055c6ffeOZO66b0AKuGGW6","messageID":"msg_1a363b6a2001DRGLXhpqs8m1nn","type":"step-finish","reason":"tool-calls","snapshot":"e6c82dd4f9652a65294271a2bbfb69b920d4a695","cost":0,"tokens":{"input":674,"output":107,"reasoning":220,"cache":{"read":132864,"write":0}}}}
{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1793742588942,"sessionID":"ses_e5ca055c6ffeOZO66b0AKuGGW6","part":{"id":"prt_1a363cc0e0014f2zUtlGP9iYSc","sessionID":"ses_e5ca055c6ffeOZO66b0AKuGGW6","messageID":"msg_1a363c56a001hViXqpEPNRwUd9","type":"step-start","snapshot":"e6c82dd4f9652a65294271a2bbfb69b920d4a695"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1793742591610,"sessionID":"ses_e5ca055c6ffeOZO66b0AKuGGW6","part":{"partID":"prt_1a363d60f001FBpJWjeAhv4Jr1","sessionID":"ses_e5ca055c6ffeOZO66b0AKuGGW6","messageID":"msg_1a363c56a001hViXqpEPNRwUd9","type":"tool","id":"call_00_icaqe8keihdlbve6wunm01xe","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":".skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-ci/references/trigger-and-filter-rules.md"},"output":"Read file .skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-ci/references/trigger-and-filter-rules.md, lines 1-88\n1: ---\n2: title: Trigger and Filter Rules\n3: description: The twin rule for path filters, the rule that keeps a rebuild commit from starting the downstream workflows, push-only scope for generated-path filters and the table of intentional trigger exceptions.\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"workflow path filters\"\n6:   - \"workflow trigger rules\"\n7:   - \"twin path filter rule\"\n8:   - \"intentional trigger exception\"\n9: importance_tier: normal\n10: contextType: general\n11: version: 1.1.0.0\n12: ---\n13: \n14: # Trigger and Filter Rules\n15: \n16: Which events start a workflow, which paths it ignores and which triggers only look like drift.\n17: \n18: ---\n19: \n20: ## 1. OVERVIEW\n21: \n22: Four topics sit here: the twin rule for path filters, the rule that stops a rebuild commit from starting the downstream workflows, the push-only scope of generated-path filters and the table of intentional trigger exceptions. A review reads the exceptions table before it flags a trigger as drift.\n23: \n24: Each rule is universal. The values it applies to, the twin roots, the integration branches and the rebuild trailer, come from the repository CI profile described in `references/protected-set.md`. A line marked Skilled example shows this repository's value and holds nowhere else.\n25: \n26: \n27: ---\n28: \n29: ## 2. THE TWIN RULE\n30: \n31: When the profile lists `pathTwins`, every path filter, hook input and dependabot entry that names one root of a pair must name its twin, in the same filter group. A repository whose profile lists no pair has no twin rule.\n32: \n33: Skilled example: the profile pairs `.skilled/` with `.opencode/`, so a `.skilled/` entry needs the matching `.opencode/` entry and the reverse. The gate-input check enforces this here, so a filter that names one root fails with a `filter-twins` verdict.\n34: \n35: Skilled example: four generated paths times two source roots make **8 entries**. A filter that ignores the four trigger-index outputs therefore lists eight lines, never four.\n36: \n37: A twin is **inert** where its tree is absent. The rule still requires it, because a check reads the filter and not the disk. Skilled example: `repo-rules-corpus.yml` names `.opencode/repo-rules/**` next to `.skilled/repo-rules/**` and says why in a comment.\n38: \n39: Evidence (Skilled example):\n40: \n41: - Skilled example: `.github/scripts/check-gate-inputs.sh:112-124` holds the twin matcher. `function twin` at `:116` swaps `.opencode` for `.skilled` and the reverse.\n42: - `.github/scripts/check-gate-inputs.sh:339` fails a pathspec that has no twin with `twin-one`. The same verdict repeats at `:423` for path filters and `:478` for directories.\n43: - `.github/scripts/check-gate-inputs.sh:372-375` recognises the `paths:` and `paths-ignore:` keys, in block and inline form.\n44: - Skilled example: `.github/workflows/repo-rules-corpus.yml:9-12` records the inert `.opencode/repo-rules/**` twin, and `:21-24` repeats it for the `push:` filter.\n45: \n46: ---\n47: \n48: ## 3. REBUILD COMMITS\n49: \n50: The trigger-index rebuild workflow commits four generated files to the branch it ran on. No downstream workflow reads them as an input, so every other workflow that answers to a push would run for nothing.\n51: \n52: **The rule.** A rebuild commit carries `[skip ci]` in its message body. GitHub then starts no push-triggered or pull-request-triggered workflow for that commit, and the downstream workflows stay quiet without a single filter edit. The commit also ends with a trailer the profile names as `rebuildTrailer`, so the report can skip it when it looks for the newest commit worth reading.\n53: \n54: Evidence (Skilled example):\n55: \n56: - `.github/workflows/trigger-index-rebuild.yml:78-84` holds the list of the four generated paths the commit stages.\n57: - `.github/workflows/trigger-index-rebuild.yml:152` holds the commit body line that ends in `[skip ci]`.\n58: - `.github/workflows/trigger-index-rebuild.yml:33` guards the rebuild job itself with the `Trigger-Index-Rebuild: ci` trailer, so a run GitHub creates anyway does not rebuild a rebuild.\n59: \n60: **The alternative.** A push-only `paths-ignore` that lists the eight twin entries on each downstream workflow does the same job, at the price of 104 twin-matched entries across 13 files and an amended ordering rule. Use it only if the skip token proves unreliable.\n61: \n62: **What it saves.** Skipping the downstream workflows on the four generated paths saves about 2,073 runner-min/day, roughly 54% of the repository's daily total. The push-only filter form saves about 2,002 runner-min/day when `message-contract.yml` is left unfiltered.\n63: \n64: **Reading statuses.** A skipped rebuild commit leaves no runs on the branch tip. Any tool that reads statuses on the tip must resolve the newest commit that is not a rebuild commit.\n65: \n66: ---\n67: \n68: ## 4. PUSH-ONLY SCOPE\n69: \n70: A rule that ignores generated paths is push-only.\n71: \n72: The rebuild workflow triggers on `push` alone (`.github/workflows/trigger-index-rebuild.yml:6-9`) and has no `pull_request:` event, so a generated-path commit only ever arrives through a push. A `pull_request:` ignore for the same paths would add nothing, and where the workflow supplies a required status check it would leave that check **Pending**, because GitHub reports a path-filtered required workflow as pending rather than passed.\n73: \n74: When a workflow already lists `paths:` for an event, GitHub refuses `paths-ignore:` on the same event. Add `!` negations inside the `paths:` list instead, and keep both roots in each negation.\n75: \n76: ---\n77: \n78: ## 5. INTENTIONAL EXCEPTIONS\n79: \n80: A trigger that departs from the norm for a reason is recorded here so a review or a report reads it as intentional and not as drift. The norm is that a push-triggered workflow answers to pushes on the profile's `integrationBranches` only. Skilled example: those are `main` and `skilled/**`. Each row carries the label `intentional-exception`, and every row below is a Skilled example.\n81: \n82: | Label | Workflow | Trigger | Norm departed from | Why | Evidence |\n83: |---|---|---|---|---|---|\n84: | intentional-exception | `message-contract.yml` | `push` on `branches: ['**']` | Push to `main` and `skilled/**` only (Skilled example) | Every pushed branch's name and commits are checked, because a local hook can be skipped on any branch. The workflow also holds the only required status context in the branch ruleset | `.github/workflows/message-contract.yml:9`, the comment at `.github/workflows/message-contract.yml:2-6` and the row at `.github/workflows/README.md:72` |\n85: \n86: No YAML change is made for this row. The workflow file is correct as written. A report route reads this row before it flags the trigger, so the every-branch push is never reported as drift.\n87: \n88: If `branches: ['**']` or the comment block disappears from the workflow, the row is stale. Remove it, or re-record the new trigger with its own reason.","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file .skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-ci/references/trigger-and-filter-rules.md, lines 1-88\n1: ---\n2: title: Trigger and Filter Rules\n3: description: The twin rule for path filters, the rule that keeps a rebuild commit from starting the downstream workflows, push-only scope for generated-path filters and the table of intentional trigger exceptions.\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"workflow path filters\"\n6:   - \"workflow trigger rules\"\n7:   - \"twin path filter rule\"\n8:   - \"intentional trigger exception\"\n9: importance_tier: normal\n10: contextType: general\n11: version: 1.1.0.0\n12: ---\n13: \n14: # Trigger and Filter Rules\n15: \n16: Which events start a workflow, which paths it ignores and which triggers only look like drift.\n17: \n18: ---\n19: \n20: ## 1. OVERVIEW\n21: \n22: Four topics sit here: the twin rule for path filters, the rule that stops a rebuild commit from starting the downstream workflows, the push-only scope of generated-path filters and the table of intentional trigger exceptions. A review reads the exceptions table before it flags a trigger as drift.\n23: \n24: Each rule is universal. The values it applies to, the twin roots, the integration branches and the rebuild trailer, come from the repository CI profile described in `references/protected-set.md`. A line marked Skilled example shows this repository's value and holds nowhere else.\n25: \n26: \n27: ---\n28: \n29: ## 2. THE TWIN RULE\n30: \n31: When the profile lists `pathTwins`, every path filter, hook input and dependabot entry that names one root of a pair must name its twin, in the same filter group. A repository whose profile lists no pair has no twin rule.\n32: \n33: Skilled example: the profile pairs `.skilled/` with `.opencode/`, so a `.skilled/` entry needs the matching `.opencode/` entry and the reverse. The gate-input check enforces this here, so a filter that names one root fails with a `filter-twins` verdict.\n34: \n35: Skilled example: four generated paths times two source roots make **8 entries**. A filter that ignores the four trigger-index outputs therefore lists eight lines, never four.\n36: \n37: A twin is **inert** where its tree is absent. The rule still requires it, because a check reads the filter and not the disk. Skilled example: `repo-rules-corpus.yml` names `.opencode/repo-rules/**` next to `.skilled/repo-rules/**` and says why in a comment.\n38: \n39: Evidence (Skilled example):\n40: \n41: - Skilled example: `.github/scripts/check-gate-inputs.sh:112-124` holds the twin matcher. `function twin` at `:116` swaps `.opencode` for `.skilled` and the reverse.\n42: - `.github/scripts/check-gate-inputs.sh:339` fails a pathspec that has no twin with `twin-one`. The same verdict repeats at `:423` for path filters and `:478` for directories.\n43: - `.github/scripts/check-gate-inputs.sh:372-375` recognises the `paths:` and `paths-ignore:` keys, in block and inline form.\n44: - Skilled example: `.github/workflows/repo-rules-corpus.yml:9-12` records the inert `.opencode/repo-rules/**` twin, and `:21-24` repeats it for the `push:` filter.\n45: \n46: ---\n47: \n48: ## 3. REBUILD COMMITS\n49: \n50: The trigger-index rebuild workflow commits four generated files to the branch it ran on. No downstream workflow reads them as an input, so every other workflow that answers to a push would run for nothing.\n51: \n52: **The rule.** A rebuild commit carries `[skip ci]` in its message body. GitHub then starts no push-triggered or pull-request-triggered workflow for that commit, and the downstream workflows stay quiet without a single filter edit. The commit also ends with a trailer the profile names as `rebuildTrailer`, so the report can skip it when it looks for the newest commit worth reading.\n53: \n54: Evidence (Skilled example):\n55: \n56: - `.github/workflows/trigger-index-rebuild.yml:78-84` holds the list of the four generated paths the commit stages.\n57: - `.github/workflows/trigger-index-rebuild.yml:152` holds the commit body line that ends in `[skip ci]`.\n58: - `.github/workflows/trigger-index-rebuild.yml:33` guards the rebuild job itself with the `Trigger-Index-Rebuild: ci` trailer, so a run GitHub creates anyway does not rebuild a rebuild.\n59: \n60: **The alternative.** A push-only `paths-ignore` that lists the eight twin entries on each downstream workflow does the same job, at the price of 104 twin-matched entries across 13 files and an amended ordering rule. Use it only if the skip token proves unreliable.\n61: \n62: **What it saves.** Skipping the downstream workflows on the four generated paths saves about 2,073 runner-min/day, roughly 54% of the repository's daily total. The push-only filter form saves about 2,002 runner-min/day when `message-contract.yml` is left unfiltered.\n63: \n64: **Reading statuses.** A skipped rebuild commit leaves no runs on the branch tip. Any tool that reads statuses on the tip must resolve the newest commit that is not a rebuild commit.\n65: \n66: ---\n67: \n68: ## 4. PUSH-ONLY SCOPE\n69: \n70: A rule that ignores generated paths is push-only.\n71: \n72: The rebuild workflow triggers on `push` alone (`.github/workflows/trigger-index-rebuild.yml:6-9`) and has no `pull_request:` event, so a generated-path commit only ever arrives through a push. A `pull_request:` ignore for the same paths would add nothing, and where the workflow supplies a required status check it would leave that check **Pending**, because GitHub reports a path-filtered required workflow as pending rather than passed.\n73: \n74: When a workflow already lists `paths:` for an event, GitHub refuses `paths-ignore:` on the same event. Add `!` negations inside the `paths:` list instead, and keep both roots in each negation.\n75: \n76: ---\n77: \n78: ## 5. INTENTIONAL EXCEPTIONS\n79: \n80: A trigger that departs from the norm for a reason is recorded here so a review or a report reads it as intentional and not as drift. The norm is that a push-triggered workflow answers to pushes on the profile's `integrationBranches` only. Skilled example: those are `main` and `skilled/**`. Each row carries the label `intentional-exception`, and every row below is a Skilled example.\n81: \n82: | Label | Workflow | Trigger | Norm departed from | Why | Evidence |\n83: |---|---|---|---|---|---|\n84: | intentional-exception | `message-contract.yml` | `push` on `branches: ['**']` | Push to `main` and `skilled/**` only (Skilled example) | Every pushed branch's name and commits are checked, because a local hook can be skipped on any branch. The workflow also holds the only required status context in the branch ruleset | `.github/workflows/message-contract.yml:9`, the comment at `.github/workflows/message-contract.yml:2-6` and the row at `.github/workflows/README.md:72` |\n85: \n86: No YAML change is made for this row. The workflow file is correct as written. A report route reads this row before it flags the trigger, so the every-branch push is never reported as drift.\n87: \n88: If `branches: ['**']` or the comment block disappears from the workflow, the row is stale. Remove it, or re-record the new trigger with its own reason."}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\": \".skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-ci/references/trigger-and-filter-rules.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1793742591503,"end":1793742591610}}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1793742591611,"sessionID":"ses_e5ca055c6ffeOZO66b0AKuGGW6","part":{"partID":"prt_1a363d65f001F55Kcs8CIDlVZL","sessionID":"ses_e5ca055c6ffeOZO66b0AKuGGW6","messageID":"msg_1a363c56a001hViXqpEPNRwUd9","type":"tool","id":"call_01_gxgrm5ywor6wekoxul6yqqys","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":".skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-ci/references/protected-set.md"},"output":"Read file .skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-ci/references/protected-set.md, lines 1-102\n1: ---\n2: title: Repository CI Profile and Protected Workflows\n3: description: The repository CI profile the /doctor:ci routes read, the workflows protected by function and the rules that follow, with this repository's Skilled profile as a labelled section.\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"protected workflow\"\n6:   - \"protected workflow set\"\n7:   - \"required status check\"\n8:   - \"never switch off workflow\"\n9:   - \"repository ci profile\"\n10: importance_tier: normal\n11: contextType: general\n12: version: 1.1.1.0\n13: ---\n14: \n15: # Repository CI Profile and Protected Workflows\n16: \n17: Where a repository states its own CI conventions, and which workflows are never weakened, edited or switched off on a light confirmation.\n18: \n19: ---\n20: \n21: ## 1. OVERVIEW\n22: \n23: ### The Profile\n24: \n25: Branch names, switch variables, path twins, README tables and the setup action differ from one repository to the next, so no rule in this packet hard-codes them. A repository states them in one file, `repository/ci.json` in its `sk-code-ci` packet, and `/doctor:ci report`, `scaffold` and `toggle` read it through `.skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/lib/ci-profile.cjs`.\n26: \n27: The lookup order is `--profile <path>`, then the file-scoped git config value `skcode.ciProfile`, then `repository/ci.json` in this packet, then a generic default. A `repository` field limits the file to the repository it names, so a copy shipped with the skills into another repository is ignored, and a file left at the former root-level `.sk-code/` path is refused rather than read. A named path that does not exist is an error and never falls back, because a typo would otherwise drop every protection without a word. An unknown key is an error too.\n28: \n29: The generic default forces nothing. It requires no switch, checks no README row, assumes no branch and protects no workflow. A repository without a profile gets the parser checks that hold everywhere and nothing that only this repository needs. To start one, copy `assets/ci-profile.template.json`, which reads as the default, and fill in the keys that differ.\n30: \n31: ### Profile Keys\n32: \n33: | Key | What reads it | Default |\n34: |---|---|---|\n35: | `schemaVersion` | Every reader. It must be `1` | required |\n36: | `defaultBranch` | The report's run list and the toggle's ruleset read. Without it the report asks GitHub, then falls back to `main` | `null` |\n37: | `integrationBranches` | The scaffold's push `branches:` filter | `[]`, no filter |\n38: | `workflowsReadme` | `{ path, rowTables }`. The report reads purposes from the README's `Workflow` and `Responsibility` table and flags a missing row. The scaffold prints one row per table. The toggle reads a `<NAME> must stay unset` sentence | `null`, no README check |\n39: | `switch.master`, `switch.groups` | The switch variables, in the order the report lists them and the toggle accepts them. Without them the toggle accepts the names the workflows compare against `off` | `null`, `[]` |\n40: | `switch.required` | Whether every job must carry a switch. Only then does the report flag `switch-missing` | `false` |\n41: | `switch.exempt` | Workflow files that need no switch even when one is required | `[]` |\n42: | `neverOff` | Variables the toggle never sets to `off`, whether or not the ruleset can be read | `[]` |\n43: | `protected` | `{ file, role }` entries. The scaffold refuses the name and the toggle asks a second named confirmation before it switches one off | `[]` |\n44: | `pathTwins` | `[root, twin]` pairs a path filter must name together | `[]`, no twin rule |\n45: | `rebuildTrailer` | The commit trailer that marks a rebuild commit the report skips | `null`, nothing skipped |\n46: | `setupAction` | `{ uses, with, note }` for the composite setup step the job template adds | `null`, no setup step |\n47: | `timeoutFloorMinutes` | The timeout floor the templates start from | `10` |\n48: \n49: ---\n50: \n51: ## 2. PROTECTED BY FUNCTION\n52: \n53: A workflow is protected because of what it does, so a new workflow that does the same job joins the set. Three classes qualify.\n54: \n55: | Class | Test |\n56: |---|---|\n57: | (a) Sole enforcer | It is the only thing that enforces an invariant, and no local gate backs it up |\n58: | (b) Token-eligibility split | It splits the work that runs repository code from the work that holds a write token |\n59: | (c) Input guard | Its failure invalidates the inputs other workflows trust |\n60: \n61: A workflow that holds a status context the branch ruleset requires is protected as well, because switching it off removes the requirement. The profile's `protected` list names the protected workflows with the role of each, and the toggle reads the required contexts from the live ruleset.\n62: \n63: ---\n64: \n65: ## 3. THE RULES\n66: \n67: 1. **Never switch off a job whose status context the branch ruleset requires.** A switched-off job is skipped, and a skipped job reports Success, so the required context stops blocking merges. A workflow-level path filter is the opposite case and leaves the context Pending (`references/trigger-and-filter-rules.md`). `/doctor:ci toggle` refuses such a variable from the live ruleset, from the profile's `neverOff` list and from a `must stay unset` sentence in the workflows README.\n68: 2. **A protected workflow is never edited or switched off without a second named confirmation that restates its role.** The first confirmation approves the change. The second names the workflow, says in one sentence what it protects and approves again. `/doctor:ci toggle` asks it whenever a variable would switch off a workflow in the profile's `protected` list, and only the variable name counts as the answer. A scaffold or a review that would weaken a protected workflow stops and asks.\n69: \n70: ---\n71: \n72: ## 4. HOW TO USE THE SET\n73: \n74: - Before editing a workflow, search the profile's `protected` list for its file name and apply the class tests above.\n75: - When a request reads \"switch off\", \"skip\" or \"disable\" a workflow, check rule 1 first.\n76: - When two protected classes both fit, apply the stricter rule.\n77: - When the list looks wrong for a workflow that was added or renamed, correct the profile and this file's Skilled section, and do not rely on memory.\n78: \n79: ---\n80: \n81: ## 5. SKILLED PROFILE\n82: \n83: This repository commits its profile as `repository/ci.json`. Everything in this section is the Skilled example, and none of it applies to another repository.\n84: \n85: | Class | Workflows |\n86: |---|---|\n87: | (a) Sole enforcer | `gate-inputs.yml` |\n88: | (b) Token-eligibility split | `trigger-index-rebuild.yml` |\n89: | (c) Input guard | `dispatch-enforcement-guard.yml`, `naming-standard-guard.yml` |\n90: | Required check | `message-contract.yml` |\n91: \n92: `gate-inputs.yml` is the only enforcer of path parity between the profile's twin roots, `.skilled/` and `.opencode/` (Skilled example). Without it a moved source tree or a one-sided path filter passes unseen.\n93: \n94: `trigger-index-rebuild.yml` keeps the job that runs repository code at `contents: read` and gives the write token only to a job that runs `gh` and `git`. Merging the two jobs would hand the token to repository code.\n95: \n96: `dispatch-enforcement-guard.yml` and `naming-standard-guard.yml` check the rules that other workflows and tools assume. A failure here means those assumptions no longer hold.\n97: \n98: `message-contract.yml` holds the only required status context in the branch ruleset, `Commit messages, branch names and PR description follow the templates`. The workflow also pushes on every branch as a recorded exception. See the intentional exceptions table in `references/trigger-and-filter-rules.md`.\n99: \n100: The Skilled example switches are the master `SKILLED_CI` and the groups `SKILLED_CI_TESTS`, `SKILLED_CI_REPORTS`, `SKILLED_CI_REVIEW`, `SKILLED_CI_FRESHNESS` and `SKILLED_CI_MESSAGES`. `SKILLED_CI` and `SKILLED_CI_MESSAGES` are in `neverOff` (Skilled example) because both skip `message-contract.yml`. `SKILLED_CI_FRESHNESS` reaches `gate-inputs.yml`, `dispatch-enforcement-guard.yml` and `naming-standard-guard.yml`, and `SKILLED_CI_REPORTS` reaches `trigger-index-rebuild.yml` (Skilled example), so the toggle asks the second confirmation for both. `dependabot-auto-merge.yml` is in `switch.exempt`, the rebuild trailer is `Trigger-Index-Rebuild: ci` and the setup action is `./.github/actions/setup`.\n101: \n102: A contract test fails when `repository/ci.json` goes missing or loses a protected entry, because without it this repository would read the generic default and a single yes could switch off a guard.","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file .skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-ci/references/protected-set.md, lines 1-102\n1: ---\n2: title: Repository CI Profile and Protected Workflows\n3: description: The repository CI profile the /doctor:ci routes read, the workflows protected by function and the rules that follow, with this repository's Skilled profile as a labelled section.\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"protected workflow\"\n6:   - \"protected workflow set\"\n7:   - \"required status check\"\n8:   - \"never switch off workflow\"\n9:   - \"repository ci profile\"\n10: importance_tier: normal\n11: contextType: general\n12: version: 1.1.1.0\n13: ---\n14: \n15: # Repository CI Profile and Protected Workflows\n16: \n17: Where a repository states its own CI conventions, and which workflows are never weakened, edited or switched off on a light confirmation.\n18: \n19: ---\n20: \n21: ## 1. OVERVIEW\n22: \n23: ### The Profile\n24: \n25: Branch names, switch variables, path twins, README tables and the setup action differ from one repository to the next, so no rule in this packet hard-codes them. A repository states them in one file, `repository/ci.json` in its `sk-code-ci` packet, and `/doctor:ci report`, `scaffold` and `toggle` read it through `.skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/lib/ci-profile.cjs`.\n26: \n27: The lookup order is `--profile <path>`, then the file-scoped git config value `skcode.ciProfile`, then `repository/ci.json` in this packet, then a generic default. A `repository` field limits the file to the repository it names, so a copy shipped with the skills into another repository is ignored, and a file left at the former root-level `.sk-code/` path is refused rather than read. A named path that does not exist is an error and never falls back, because a typo would otherwise drop every protection without a word. An unknown key is an error too.\n28: \n29: The generic default forces nothing. It requires no switch, checks no README row, assumes no branch and protects no workflow. A repository without a profile gets the parser checks that hold everywhere and nothing that only this repository needs. To start one, copy `assets/ci-profile.template.json`, which reads as the default, and fill in the keys that differ.\n30: \n31: ### Profile Keys\n32: \n33: | Key | What reads it | Default |\n34: |---|---|---|\n35: | `schemaVersion` | Every reader. It must be `1` | required |\n36: | `defaultBranch` | The report's run list and the toggle's ruleset read. Without it the report asks GitHub, then falls back to `main` | `null` |\n37: | `integrationBranches` | The scaffold's push `branches:` filter | `[]`, no filter |\n38: | `workflowsReadme` | `{ path, rowTables }`. The report reads purposes from the README's `Workflow` and `Responsibility` table and flags a missing row. The scaffold prints one row per table. The toggle reads a `<NAME> must stay unset` sentence | `null`, no README check |\n39: | `switch.master`, `switch.groups` | The switch variables, in the order the report lists them and the toggle accepts them. Without them the toggle accepts the names the workflows compare against `off` | `null`, `[]` |\n40: | `switch.required` | Whether every job must carry a switch. Only then does the report flag `switch-missing` | `false` |\n41: | `switch.exempt` | Workflow files that need no switch even when one is required | `[]` |\n42: | `neverOff` | Variables the toggle never sets to `off`, whether or not the ruleset can be read | `[]` |\n43: | `protected` | `{ file, role }` entries. The scaffold refuses the name and the toggle asks a second named confirmation before it switches one off | `[]` |\n44: | `pathTwins` | `[root, twin]` pairs a path filter must name together | `[]`, no twin rule |\n45: | `rebuildTrailer` | The commit trailer that marks a rebuild commit the report skips | `null`, nothing skipped |\n46: | `setupAction` | `{ uses, with, note }` for the composite setup step the job template adds | `null`, no setup step |\n47: | `timeoutFloorMinutes` | The timeout floor the templates start from | `10` |\n48: \n49: ---\n50: \n51: ## 2. PROTECTED BY FUNCTION\n52: \n53: A workflow is protected because of what it does, so a new workflow that does the same job joins the set. Three classes qualify.\n54: \n55: | Class | Test |\n56: |---|---|\n57: | (a) Sole enforcer | It is the only thing that enforces an invariant, and no local gate backs it up |\n58: | (b) Token-eligibility split | It splits the work that runs repository code from the work that holds a write token |\n59: | (c) Input guard | Its failure invalidates the inputs other workflows trust |\n60: \n61: A workflow that holds a status context the branch ruleset requires is protected as well, because switching it off removes the requirement. The profile's `protected` list names the protected workflows with the role of each, and the toggle reads the required contexts from the live ruleset.\n62: \n63: ---\n64: \n65: ## 3. THE RULES\n66: \n67: 1. **Never switch off a job whose status context the branch ruleset requires.** A switched-off job is skipped, and a skipped job reports Success, so the required context stops blocking merges. A workflow-level path filter is the opposite case and leaves the context Pending (`references/trigger-and-filter-rules.md`). `/doctor:ci toggle` refuses such a variable from the live ruleset, from the profile's `neverOff` list and from a `must stay unset` sentence in the workflows README.\n68: 2. **A protected workflow is never edited or switched off without a second named confirmation that restates its role.** The first confirmation approves the change. The second names the workflow, says in one sentence what it protects and approves again. `/doctor:ci toggle` asks it whenever a variable would switch off a workflow in the profile's `protected` list, and only the variable name counts as the answer. A scaffold or a review that would weaken a protected workflow stops and asks.\n69: \n70: ---\n71: \n72: ## 4. HOW TO USE THE SET\n73: \n74: - Before editing a workflow, search the profile's `protected` list for its file name and apply the class tests above.\n75: - When a request reads \"switch off\", \"skip\" or \"disable\" a workflow, check rule 1 first.\n76: - When two protected classes both fit, apply the stricter rule.\n77: - When the list looks wrong for a workflow that was added or renamed, correct the profile and this file's Skilled section, and do not rely on memory.\n78: \n79: ---\n80: \n81: ## 5. SKILLED PROFILE\n82: \n83: This repository commits its profile as `repository/ci.json`. Everything in this section is the Skilled example, and none of it applies to another repository.\n84: \n85: | Class | Workflows |\n86: |---|---|\n87: | (a) Sole enforcer | `gate-inputs.yml` |\n88: | (b) Token-eligibility split | `trigger-index-rebuild.yml` |\n89: | (c) Input guard | `dispatch-enforcement-guard.yml`, `naming-standard-guard.yml` |\n90: | Required check | `message-contract.yml` |\n91: \n92: `gate-inputs.yml` is the only enforcer of path parity between the profile's twin roots, `.skilled/` and `.opencode/` (Skilled example). Without it a moved source tree or a one-sided path filter passes unseen.\n93: \n94: `trigger-index-rebuild.yml` keeps the job that runs repository code at `contents: read` and gives the write token only to a job that runs `gh` and `git`. Merging the two jobs would hand the token to repository code.\n95: \n96: `dispatch-enforcement-guard.yml` and `naming-standard-guard.yml` check the rules that other workflows and tools assume. A failure here means those assumptions no longer hold.\n97: \n98: `message-contract.yml` holds the only required status context in the branch ruleset, `Commit messages, branch names and PR description follow the templates`. The workflow also pushes on every branch as a recorded exception. See the intentional exceptions table in `references/trigger-and-filter-rules.md`.\n99: \n100: The Skilled example switches are the master `SKILLED_CI` and the groups `SKILLED_CI_TESTS`, `SKILLED_CI_REPORTS`, `SKILLED_CI_REVIEW`, `SKILLED_CI_FRESHNESS` and `SKILLED_CI_MESSAGES`. `SKILLED_CI` and `SKILLED_CI_MESSAGES` are in `neverOff` (Skilled example) because both skip `message-contract.yml`. `SKILLED_CI_FRESHNESS` reaches `gate-inputs.yml`, `dispatch-enforcement-guard.yml` and `naming-standard-guard.yml`, and `SKILLED_CI_REPORTS` reaches `trigger-index-rebuild.yml` (Skilled example), so the toggle asks the second confirmation for both. `dependabot-auto-merge.yml` is in `switch.exempt`, the rebuild trailer is `Trigger-Index-Rebuild: ci` and the setup action is `./.github/actions/setup`.\n101: \n102: A contract test fails when `repository/ci.json` goes missing or loses a protected entry, because without it this repository would read the generic default and a single yes could switch off a guard."}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\": \".skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-ci/references/protected-set.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1793742591583,"end":1793742591611}}}}
{"type":"step_finish","timestamp":1793742592270,"sessionID":"ses_e5ca055c6ffeOZO66b0AKuGGW6","part":{"id":"prt_1a363d90e001TYxa1xNZV1XaA4","sessionID":"ses_e5ca055c6ffeOZO66b0AKuGGW6","messageID":"msg_1a363c56a001hViXqpEPNRwUd9","type":"step-finish","reason":"tool-calls","snapshot":"e6c82dd4f9652a65294271a2bbfb69b920d4a695","cost":0,"tokens":{"input":5030,"output":104,"reasoning":372,"cache":{"read":133504,"write":0}}}}
{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1793742593778,"sessionID":"ses_e5ca055c6ffeOZO66b0AKuGGW6","part":{"id":"prt_1a363def2001hs7bSJdk2a0gy8","sessionID":"ses_e5ca055c6ffeOZO66b0AKuGGW6","messageID":"msg_1a363d912001t5JtmKgL1lN2Gp","type":"step-start","snapshot":"e6c82dd4f9652a65294271a2bbfb69b920d4a695"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1793742599009,"sessionID":"ses_e5ca055c6ffeOZO66b0AKuGGW6","part":{"partID":"prt_1a363f336001RQZ6VqDtznYJxZ","sessionID":"ses_e5ca055c6ffeOZO66b0AKuGGW6","messageID":"msg_1a363d912001t5JtmKgL1lN2Gp","type":"tool","id":"call_01_t6f26paetd8u85cqhliiqom1","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":".skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-ci/ROUTER.md"},"output":"Read file .skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-ci/ROUTER.md, lines 1-66\n1: ---\n2: title: \"CI Surface: GitHub Actions Workflow Evidence Router\"\n3: description: \"Sibling router for sk-code-ci: the intent signals, the resource map and the no-match checklist a reader parses first.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"sk-code-ci router\"\n6:   - \"sk-code-ci routing\"\n7: importance_tier: normal\n8: contextType: general\n9: version: 1.0.0.0\n10: router_state: active\n11: skill_pointer: SKILL.md\n12: ---\n13: \n14: # CI Surface: GitHub Actions Workflow Evidence Router\n15: \n16: The machine router for [`SKILL.md`](./SKILL.md). `SKILL.md` keeps the short routing prose and the\n17: link that points here; this file holds the declaration a reader parses to decide what loads.\n18: \n19: ---\n20: \n21: ## 1. OVERVIEW\n22: \n23: Requests reach this surface when a CI-workflow signal fires: the request names a GitHub Actions workflow, a path filter, a job timeout, a concurrency group, a permissions block or a required status check, the task touches `.github/workflows/`, `.github/actions/` or `.github/scripts/`, or the request asks whether a workflow trigger is intentional or drift, or for the shape of a new workflow or job before it is written. It routes to two resource families: the rule data under `references/`, which starts at `references/ci-workflow-standards.md` and extends to `references/trigger-and-filter-rules.md`, `references/job-shape-rules.md` and `references/protected-set.md`, and the scaffold templates under `assets/`, which hold the whole-workflow template, the single-job template and the CI profile template. The default resource is `references/ci-workflow-standards.md`, the rule index the other three references extend, and no other reference loads before the single intent selects it.\n24: \n25: The router answers one question: given a request, which skill-local resources load, in what order, and\n26: what happens when nothing matches. Every path below stays inside this skill folder, and no resource\n27: loads before an intent selects it.\n28: \n29: ---\n30: \n31: ## 2. INTENT MODEL\n32: \n33: - **`CI_WORKFLOW`** fires on `github actions workflow`, `ci workflow`, `github actions yaml`, `workflow path filters`, `github workflow timeout`, `github workflow concurrency`, `github workflow permissions`, `required status check workflow`, `ci job`, `action to a sha`, `sha-pinned action` and `.github/workflows/` at weight 4, the only intent in the packet. It loads `references/ci-workflow-standards.md`, `references/trigger-and-filter-rules.md`, `references/job-shape-rules.md` and `references/protected-set.md`, and `references/ci-workflow-standards.md` is also the default resource a run loads before intent scoring.\n34: \n35: ---\n36: \n37: ## 3. MACHINE ROUTER\n38: \n39: The one machine-readable projection of the intent model above, written as `## MACHINE ROUTER` so every\n40: reader finds it. This section is the last in the file, and it holds the only pseudocode.\n41: \n42: The packet's deterministic intent-to-reference routing, kept equal to the projection the parent hub union carries.\n43: \n44: ```python\n45: # sk-code-ci owns its intent -> reference routing. Paths are relative to this\n46: # skill root. The parent sk-code hub RESOURCE_MAP carries the same four paths\n47: # under the same key, re-prefixed with sk-code-ci/. The router-sync guard\n48: # (sk-code-skilled/assets/scripts/verify-router-sync.cjs, leg 2) checks that equality.\n49: # Every keyword is multi-word, so a bare \"workflow\" never scores this surface.\n50: DEFAULT_RESOURCE = [\n51:     \"references/ci-workflow-standards.md\",\n52: ]\n53: \n54: INTENT_SIGNALS = {\n55:     \"CI_WORKFLOW\": {\"weight\": 4, \"keywords\": [\"github actions workflow\", \"ci workflow\", \"github actions yaml\", \"workflow path filters\", \"github workflow timeout\", \"github workflow concurrency\", \"github workflow permissions\", \"required status check workflow\", \"ci job\", \"action to a sha\", \"sha-pinned action\", \".github/workflows/\"]},\n56: }\n57: \n58: RESOURCE_MAP = {\n59:     \"CI_WORKFLOW\": [\n60:         \"references/ci-workflow-standards.md\",\n61:         \"references/trigger-and-filter-rules.md\",\n62:         \"references/job-shape-rules.md\",\n63:         \"references/protected-set.md\",\n64:     ],\n65: }\n66: ```","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file .skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-ci/ROUTER.md, lines 1-66\n1: ---\n2: title: \"CI Surface: GitHub Actions Workflow Evidence Router\"\n3: description: \"Sibling router for sk-code-ci: the intent signals, the resource map and the no-match checklist a reader parses first.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"sk-code-ci router\"\n6:   - \"sk-code-ci routing\"\n7: importance_tier: normal\n8: contextType: general\n9: version: 1.0.0.0\n10: router_state: active\n11: skill_pointer: SKILL.md\n12: ---\n13: \n14: # CI Surface: GitHub Actions Workflow Evidence Router\n15: \n16: The machine router for [`SKILL.md`](./SKILL.md). `SKILL.md` keeps the short routing prose and the\n17: link that points here; this file holds the declaration a reader parses to decide what loads.\n18: \n19: ---\n20: \n21: ## 1. OVERVIEW\n22: \n23: Requests reach this surface when a CI-workflow signal fires: the request names a GitHub Actions workflow, a path filter, a job timeout, a concurrency group, a permissions block or a required status check, the task touches `.github/workflows/`, `.github/actions/` or `.github/scripts/`, or the request asks whether a workflow trigger is intentional or drift, or for the shape of a new workflow or job before it is written. It routes to two resource families: the rule data under `references/`, which starts at `references/ci-workflow-standards.md` and extends to `references/trigger-and-filter-rules.md`, `references/job-shape-rules.md` and `references/protected-set.md`, and the scaffold templates under `assets/`, which hold the whole-workflow template, the single-job template and the CI profile template. The default resource is `references/ci-workflow-standards.md`, the rule index the other three references extend, and no other reference loads before the single intent selects it.\n24: \n25: The router answers one question: given a request, which skill-local resources load, in what order, and\n26: what happens when nothing matches. Every path below stays inside this skill folder, and no resource\n27: loads before an intent selects it.\n28: \n29: ---\n30: \n31: ## 2. INTENT MODEL\n32: \n33: - **`CI_WORKFLOW`** fires on `github actions workflow`, `ci workflow`, `github actions yaml`, `workflow path filters`, `github workflow timeout`, `github workflow concurrency`, `github workflow permissions`, `required status check workflow`, `ci job`, `action to a sha`, `sha-pinned action` and `.github/workflows/` at weight 4, the only intent in the packet. It loads `references/ci-workflow-standards.md`, `references/trigger-and-filter-rules.md`, `references/job-shape-rules.md` and `references/protected-set.md`, and `references/ci-workflow-standards.md` is also the default resource a run loads before intent scoring.\n34: \n35: ---\n36: \n37: ## 3. MACHINE ROUTER\n38: \n39: The one machine-readable projection of the intent model above, written as `## MACHINE ROUTER` so every\n40: reader finds it. This section is the last in the file, and it holds the only pseudocode.\n41: \n42: The packet's deterministic intent-to-reference routing, kept equal to the projection the parent hub union carries.\n43: \n44: ```python\n45: # sk-code-ci owns its intent -> reference routing. Paths are relative to this\n46: # skill root. The parent sk-code hub RESOURCE_MAP carries the same four paths\n47: # under the same key, re-prefixed with sk-code-ci/. The router-sync guard\n48: # (sk-code-skilled/assets/scripts/verify-router-sync.cjs, leg 2) checks that equality.\n49: # Every keyword is multi-word, so a bare \"workflow\" never scores this surface.\n50: DEFAULT_RESOURCE = [\n51:     \"references/ci-workflow-standards.md\",\n52: ]\n53: \n54: INTENT_SIGNALS = {\n55:     \"CI_WORKFLOW\": {\"weight\": 4, \"keywords\": [\"github actions workflow\", \"ci workflow\", \"github actions yaml\", \"workflow path filters\", \"github workflow timeout\", \"github workflow concurrency\", \"github workflow permissions\", \"required status check workflow\", \"ci job\", \"action to a sha\", \"sha-pinned action\", \".github/workflows/\"]},\n56: }\n57: \n58: RESOURCE_MAP = {\n59:     \"CI_WORKFLOW\": [\n60:         \"references/ci-workflow-standards.md\",\n61:         \"references/trigger-and-filter-rules.md\",\n62:         \"references/job-shape-rules.md\",\n63:         \"references/protected-set.md\",\n64:     ],\n65: }\n66: ```"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\": \".skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-ci/ROUTER.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1793742598966,"end":1793742599009}}}}
