{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1793742603519,"sessionID":"ses_e5ca055c6ffeOZO66b0AKuGGW6","part":{"partID":"prt_1a36404e4001vvOa6AbrUs2LeA","sessionID":"ses_e5ca055c6ffeOZO66b0AKuGGW6","messageID":"msg_1a363f619001p92J3c9Fz4Lc0G","type":"tool","id":"call_00_n6nel9gsexs9wsjx9hhs4cjw","tool":"shell","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"sed -n '1,70p' .skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-ci/SKILL.md"},"output":"---\nname: sk-code-ci\ndescription: \"Read-only GitHub Actions workflow evidence: trigger, filter, permission, timeout, concurrency and caching rules, the protected set and the scaffold templates.\"\nallowed-tools: [Read, Bash, Grep, Glob]\nversion: 1.1.13.0\nmetadata:\n  family: sk-code\n  packetKind: surface\n---\n\n# CI Surface: GitHub Actions Workflow Evidence\n\n**Domain evidence** for the repository's GitHub Actions workflows under `.github/workflows/`.\n\nThe packet records four things:\n\n- Each workflow rule, with the file and line that prove it.\n- The workflows that must never be weakened.\n- The one trigger that looks like drift and is not.\n- Two scaffold templates.\n\nIt writes nothing. Read-only means this packet writes nothing, and it does not limit what the acting agent may do.\n\nThe workflow mode the hub pairs it with reads the evidence and applies it. When the hub routes this packet alone because no workflow mode scored, the acting agent reads and applies it directly.\n\nA workflow change looks small and has a wide effect. One path filter decides which workflows a commit starts, one missing twin entry fails the gate that guards the source tree, and one switched-off workflow can remove the only check a branch ruleset requires. This packet keeps those facts in one place so a reviewer or author does not rebuild them from the workflow files and review history.\n\n## 1. WHEN THE HUB BUNDLES THIS\n\n- The task adds, edits or reviews a file under `.github/workflows/`, `.github/actions/` or `.github/scripts/`.\n- A request names a GitHub Actions workflow, a path filter, a job timeout, a concurrency group, a permissions block or a required status check.\n- A request asks whether a workflow trigger is intentional or drift.\n- A request asks for the shape of a new workflow or job before it is written.\n- The task edits, reviews or scaffolds a workflow, so the acting mode needs the rules and the protected set rather than a guess.\n\nFormal findings-first review stays with `sk-code-review` and author-side quality gates stay with `sk-code-quality`. This surface supplies the workflow evidence both apply.\n\n---\n\n## 2. REFERENCE MAP\n\nRule data (`references/`):\n- `references/ci-workflow-standards.md`: the rule index. Path parity, SHA-pinned actions, a README row per workflow, `set -euo pipefail`, fork and secret safety, matrix and `fail-fast`. Each row says whether a machine gate enforces it.\n- `references/trigger-and-filter-rules.md`: the twin rule, the rebuild-commit skip rule, push-only scope for generated-path filters and the intentional exceptions table.\n- `references/job-shape-rules.md`: timeouts, least-privilege permissions, the concurrency template, the composite setup action and the switch `if:` shape.\n- `references/protected-set.md`: the repository CI profile and its keys, the workflows protected by function and the rules that follow from it.\n\nScaffold templates (`assets/`):\n- `assets/workflow-template.yml`: a whole-workflow starting point.\n- `assets/job-block-template.yml`: a single job starting point.\n- `assets/ci-profile.template.json`: the profile a repository fills in so `/doctor:ci` reads its own switch names, branches, protected workflows and README instead of this repository's.\n\nWorkflow doctrine: the implement, debug and verify phases are the shared doctrine symlinked in as `references/workflow-implement.md`, `references/workflow-debug.md` and `references/workflow-verify.md`. The acting agent applies it.\n\n## 2b. SMART ROUTING\n\nThe hub bundles this surface when a CI-workflow signal fires: the request names a GitHub Actions workflow, a path filter, a job timeout, a concurrency group, a permissions block or a required status check, or the task touches `.github/workflows/`. The first leaf it loads is `references/ci-workflow-standards.md`, the rule index the other three references extend.\n\nThe parent hub `RESOURCE_MAP` carries the same four paths under `CI_WORKFLOW`, re-prefixed with `sk-code-ci/`, and the router-sync guard (`sk-code-skilled/assets/scripts/verify-router-sync.cjs`, leg 2) checks that the two lists stay equal.\n\nRead [`ROUTER.md`](./ROUTER.md) before selecting skill-local resources; open only the matched files.\n\n---\n\n## 3. SURFACE STANDARDS (the non-negotiables)\n\nThe rules below hold for every workflow change, whoever applies it.\n\n### ✅ ALWAYS\n\n","title":"shell","metadata":{"metadata":{"status":"completed","truncated":false,"exit":0},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"---\nname: sk-code-ci\ndescription: \"Read-only GitHub Actions workflow evidence: trigger, filter, permission, timeout, concurrency and caching rules, the protected set and the scaffold templates.\"\nallowed-tools: [Read, Bash, Grep, Glob]\nversion: 1.1.13.0\nmetadata:\n  family: sk-code\n  packetKind: surface\n---\n\n# CI Surface: GitHub Actions Workflow Evidence\n\n**Domain evidence** for the repository's GitHub Actions workflows under `.github/workflows/`.\n\nThe packet records four things:\n\n- Each workflow rule, with the file and line that prove it.\n- The workflows that must never be weakened.\n- The one trigger that looks like drift and is not.\n- Two scaffold templates.\n\nIt writes nothing. Read-only means this packet writes nothing, and it does not limit what the acting agent may do.\n\nThe workflow mode the hub pairs it with reads the evidence and applies it. When the hub routes this packet alone because no workflow mode scored, the acting agent reads and applies it directly.\n\nA workflow change looks small and has a wide effect. One path filter decides which workflows a commit starts, one missing twin entry fails the gate that guards the source tree, and one switched-off workflow can remove the only check a branch ruleset requires. This packet keeps those facts in one place so a reviewer or author does not rebuild them from the workflow files and review history.\n\n## 1. WHEN THE HUB BUNDLES THIS\n\n- The task adds, edits or reviews a file under `.github/workflows/`, `.github/actions/` or `.github/scripts/`.\n- A request names a GitHub Actions workflow, a path filter, a job timeout, a concurrency group, a permissions block or a required status check.\n- A request asks whether a workflow trigger is intentional or drift.\n- A request asks for the shape of a new workflow or job before it is written.\n- The task edits, reviews or scaffolds a workflow, so the acting mode needs the rules and the protected set rather than a guess.\n\nFormal findings-first review stays with `sk-code-review` and author-side quality gates stay with `sk-code-quality`. This surface supplies the workflow evidence both apply.\n\n---\n\n## 2. REFERENCE MAP\n\nRule data (`references/`):\n- `references/ci-workflow-standards.md`: the rule index. Path parity, SHA-pinned actions, a README row per workflow, `set -euo pipefail`, fork and secret safety, matrix and `fail-fast`. Each row says whether a machine gate enforces it.\n- `references/trigger-and-filter-rules.md`: the twin rule, the rebuild-commit skip rule, push-only scope for generated-path filters and the intentional exceptions table.\n- `references/job-shape-rules.md`: timeouts, least-privilege permissions, the concurrency template, the composite setup action and the switch `if:` shape.\n- `references/protected-set.md`: the repository CI profile and its keys, the workflows protected by function and the rules that follow from it.\n\nScaffold templates (`assets/`):\n- `assets/workflow-template.yml`: a whole-workflow starting point.\n- `assets/job-block-template.yml`: a single job starting point.\n- `assets/ci-profile.template.json`: the profile a repository fills in so `/doctor:ci` reads its own switch names, branches, protected workflows and README instead of this repository's.\n\nWorkflow doctrine: the implement, debug and verify phases are the shared doctrine symlinked in as `references/workflow-implement.md`, `references/workflow-debug.md` and `references/workflow-verify.md`. The acting agent applies it.\n\n## 2b. SMART ROUTING\n\nThe hub bundles this surface when a CI-workflow signal fires: the request names a GitHub Actions workflow, a path filter, a job timeout, a concurrency group, a permissions block or a required status check, or the task touches `.github/workflows/`. The first leaf it loads is `references/ci-workflow-standards.md`, the rule index the other three references extend.\n\nThe parent hub `RESOURCE_MAP` carries the same four paths under `CI_WORKFLOW`, re-prefixed with `sk-code-ci/`, and the router-sync guard (`sk-code-skilled/assets/scripts/verify-router-sync.cjs`, leg 2) checks that the two lists stay equal.\n\nRead [`ROUTER.md`](./ROUTER.md) before selecting skill-local resources; open only the matched files.\n\n---\n\n## 3. SURFACE STANDARDS (the non-negotiables)\n\nThe rules below hold for every workflow change, whoever applies it.\n\n### ✅ ALWAYS\n\n"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"command\": \"sed -n '1,70p' .skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-ci/SKILL.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1793742603492,"end":1793742603519}}}}
