{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1793742704084,"sessionID":"ses_e5ca055c6ffeOZO66b0AKuGGW6","part":{"id":"prt_1a3658dd4001cRRED9FT1Z4dW2","sessionID":"ses_e5ca055c6ffeOZO66b0AKuGGW6","messageID":"msg_1a3658691001SIL7vyKY4iCmRs","type":"step-start","snapshot":"79a4d55fd6bc95fdd899449a27a350d3ba4f6bb0"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1793742707591,"sessionID":"ses_e5ca055c6ffeOZO66b0AKuGGW6","part":{"partID":"prt_1a3659b4a0015zyEPfMlTA9FW8","sessionID":"ses_e5ca055c6ffeOZO66b0AKuGGW6","messageID":"msg_1a3658691001SIL7vyKY4iCmRs","type":"tool","id":"call_01_r2nbi9tssrfwr8ow4rskchq7","tool":"shell","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"sed -n '1,40p' .skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-quality/references/quality-config.md; echo \"===STYLE 1-30===\"; sed -n '1,30p' .skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-quality/references/code-style-guide.md"},"output":"---\ntitle: Repository Quality Config\ndescription: The schema, discovery order, merge rule, error codes, trust boundary and known limits of the committed .skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-quality/repository/quality.json file that names a repository's own quality checks.\ntrigger_phrases:\n  - \"sk-code quality config\"\n  - \".skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-quality/repository/quality.json\"\n  - \"configured quality checks\"\n  - \"skcode.qualityConfig\"\nimportance_tier: normal\ncontextType: implementation\nversion: 1.0.8.0\n---\n\n# Repository Quality Config\n\n## 1. OVERVIEW\n\nA repository names its own quality checks in one committed file, `.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-quality/repository/quality.json`. The quality runner reads it on every run, and `/doctor:sk-code quality` shows, checks and creates it. Both read it through `scripts/lib/quality-config.cjs`, so they never disagree about what the file says. The file's additions tighten the author gate only. The CI job and the pre-commit hook keep the core hygiene set at whole-file scope, minus the rules `commentHygiene.disableRules` switches off, which they read through the same loader by way of `scripts/hygiene-config-flags.cjs`. So a rule the file switches off blocks nowhere, and a ticket prefix the file adds can fail locally and pass on push. A file that does not validate leaves both layers on every core rule.\n\n---\n\n## 2. DISCOVERY\n\nThe runner picks exactly one source, the most explicit first.\n\n1. `--config <path>`, read relative to the working directory.\n2. The git config value `skcode.qualityConfig`, read relative to the repository root. Only a value stored in a config file counts. A value set with `git -c` or a `GIT_CONFIG_*` variable reports scope `command` and is ignored, because one invocation must not swap the commands a repository committed. When several files set it, the last value git reads wins. Discovery follows an include, and trust reads only this repository's own `.git/config` with no include, so a value an include supplies can name the config a run reads and still never make that config trusted.\n3. `.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-quality/repository/quality.json` inside the skill folder, or the same path under `.opencode/` when the skills are installed there.\n4. None. The runner uses the shipped base layer alone and fills `suggested_checks`.\n\nA file that carries `\"repository\": \"<owner>/<name>\"` applies only when the current repository matches, read from `GITHUB_REPOSITORY` in CI and from the origin remote otherwise, so a copy shipped with the skills into another repository is reported as `ignored` and not applied, and a file left at the old root path `.sk-code/quality.json` is refused with `CONFIG_LEGACY_LOCATION`, exit 3, and its message names the move. The same refusal applies to any path inside `.sk-code/` that `--config` or `skcode.qualityConfig` names, so no repository can keep its config there by naming it.\n\n`config.source` names the choice: `cli`, `git-config`, `repo` or `none`. `config.path` prints the file relative to the root, or absolute outside it, and `config.bytes` gives its size.\n\n- **A named path that does not exist** exits 3 with `CONFIG_PATH_MISSING` and names the path. The runner never falls back to the next source, because a named path that points nowhere is a broken install, not an opt-out.\n- **The committed file must be the repository's own.** When `.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-quality/repository/quality.json` resolves outside the repository, for example through a linked `.skilled` folder, the run exits 3 with `CONFIG_INVALID`. A path named with `--config` or `skcode.qualityConfig` may point anywhere, because an operator chose it.\n- **Cost.** Discovery and validation start one process, the `git config` read, and none outside git.\n\n---\n\n===STYLE 1-30===\n---\ntitle: Universal Code Style Guide\ndescription: Language-agnostic naming, file structure, commenting and formatting principles that hold on every surface, with house style left to each surface.\ntrigger_phrases:\n  - \"universal code style guide\"\n  - \"language agnostic naming\"\n  - \"universal formatting principles\"\n  - \"commenting universal rules\"\nimportance_tier: normal\ncontextType: implementation\nversion: 2.3.0.0\n---\n\n# Universal Code Style Guide\n\nLanguage-agnostic style principles that hold in any repository. Identifier schemes, file-size limits, line length, folder and test layout, formatter picks and header forms are house style, so each surface states its own. This file keeps only the universal rules.\n\n---\n\n## 1. OVERVIEW\n\n### Purpose\n\nCodifies the style principles that apply regardless of code surface: naming intent, file organization, commenting discipline and formatter use. The concrete conventions are surface-specific and live in each surface packet. For this repository's own code that is SKILLED, under `.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/references/shared/`. Use this doc when the project lacks a surface-specific style guide or when a contributor is new to the codebase.\n\n### Core Principle\n\nNames communicate intent. Files express one concept. Comments explain WHY (never WHAT) and describe the code as it is now. Use the formatter the project picked.\n\n### When to Use\n","title":"shell","metadata":{"metadata":{"status":"completed","truncated":false,"exit":0},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"---\ntitle: Repository Quality Config\ndescription: The schema, discovery order, merge rule, error codes, trust boundary and known limits of the committed .skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-quality/repository/quality.json file that names a repository's own quality checks.\ntrigger_phrases:\n  - \"sk-code quality config\"\n  - \".skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-quality/repository/quality.json\"\n  - \"configured quality checks\"\n  - \"skcode.qualityConfig\"\nimportance_tier: normal\ncontextType: implementation\nversion: 1.0.8.0\n---\n\n# Repository Quality Config\n\n## 1. OVERVIEW\n\nA repository names its own quality checks in one committed file, `.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-quality/repository/quality.json`. The quality runner reads it on every run, and `/doctor:sk-code quality` shows, checks and creates it. Both read it through `scripts/lib/quality-config.cjs`, so they never disagree about what the file says. The file's additions tighten the author gate only. The CI job and the pre-commit hook keep the core hygiene set at whole-file scope, minus the rules `commentHygiene.disableRules` switches off, which they read through the same loader by way of `scripts/hygiene-config-flags.cjs`. So a rule the file switches off blocks nowhere, and a ticket prefix the file adds can fail locally and pass on push. A file that does not validate leaves both layers on every core rule.\n\n---\n\n## 2. DISCOVERY\n\nThe runner picks exactly one source, the most explicit first.\n\n1. `--config <path>`, read relative to the working directory.\n2. The git config value `skcode.qualityConfig`, read relative to the repository root. Only a value stored in a config file counts. A value set with `git -c` or a `GIT_CONFIG_*` variable reports scope `command` and is ignored, because one invocation must not swap the commands a repository committed. When several files set it, the last value git reads wins. Discovery follows an include, and trust reads only this repository's own `.git/config` with no include, so a value an include supplies can name the config a run reads and still never make that config trusted.\n3. `.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-quality/repository/quality.json` inside the skill folder, or the same path under `.opencode/` when the skills are installed there.\n4. None. The runner uses the shipped base layer alone and fills `suggested_checks`.\n\nA file that carries `\"repository\": \"<owner>/<name>\"` applies only when the current repository matches, read from `GITHUB_REPOSITORY` in CI and from the origin remote otherwise, so a copy shipped with the skills into another repository is reported as `ignored` and not applied, and a file left at the old root path `.sk-code/quality.json` is refused with `CONFIG_LEGACY_LOCATION`, exit 3, and its message names the move. The same refusal applies to any path inside `.sk-code/` that `--config` or `skcode.qualityConfig` names, so no repository can keep its config there by naming it.\n\n`config.source` names the choice: `cli`, `git-config`, `repo` or `none`. `config.path` prints the file relative to the root, or absolute outside it, and `config.bytes` gives its size.\n\n- **A named path that does not exist** exits 3 with `CONFIG_PATH_MISSING` and names the path. The runner never falls back to the next source, because a named path that points nowhere is a broken install, not an opt-out.\n- **The committed file must be the repository's own.** When `.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-quality/repository/quality.json` resolves outside the repository, for example through a linked `.skilled` folder, the run exits 3 with `CONFIG_INVALID`. A path named with `--config` or `skcode.qualityConfig` may point anywhere, because an operator chose it.\n- **Cost.** Discovery and validation start one process, the `git config` read, and none outside git.\n\n---\n\n===STYLE 1-30===\n---\ntitle: Universal Code Style Guide\ndescription: Language-agnostic naming, file structure, commenting and formatting principles that hold on every surface, with house style left to each surface.\ntrigger_phrases:\n  - \"universal code style guide\"\n  - \"language agnostic naming\"\n  - \"universal formatting principles\"\n  - \"commenting universal rules\"\nimportance_tier: normal\ncontextType: implementation\nversion: 2.3.0.0\n---\n\n# Universal Code Style Guide\n\nLanguage-agnostic style principles that hold in any repository. Identifier schemes, file-size limits, line length, folder and test layout, formatter picks and header forms are house style, so each surface states its own. This file keeps only the universal rules.\n\n---\n\n## 1. OVERVIEW\n\n### Purpose\n\nCodifies the style principles that apply regardless of code surface: naming intent, file organization, commenting discipline and formatter use. The concrete conventions are surface-specific and live in each surface packet. For this repository's own code that is SKILLED, under `.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/references/shared/`. Use this doc when the project lacks a surface-specific style guide or when a contributor is new to the codebase.\n\n### Core Principle\n\nNames communicate intent. Files express one concept. Comments explain WHY (never WHAT) and describe the code as it is now. Use the formatter the project picked.\n\n### When to Use\n"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"command\": \"sed -n '1,40p' .skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-quality/references/quality-config.md; echo \\\"===STYLE 1-30===\\\"; sed -n '1,30p' .skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-quality/references/code-style-guide.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1793742707530,"end":1793742707591}}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1793742707610,"sessionID":"ses_e5ca055c6ffeOZO66b0AKuGGW6","part":{"partID":"prt_1a36599e7001Pxh6CmYTnnaaFc","sessionID":"ses_e5ca055c6ffeOZO66b0AKuGGW6","messageID":"msg_1a3658691001SIL7vyKY4iCmRs","type":"tool","id":"call_00_a9i3gf8ef4vadesgsm9ufmfu","tool":"shell","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"for f in .skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-quality/SKILL.md .skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-quality/ROUTER.md .skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-quality/references/*.md; do echo \"=== $f\"; grep -n '^#\\{1,3\\} ' \"$f\" | head -30; done; echo \"===RULE IDS===\"; grep -n '^### CHK-\\|^### Q-\\|^### [A-Z]*-[A-Z]*-' .skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-quality/references/code-style-guide.md | head -30"},"output":"=== .skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-quality/SKILL.md\n10:# Code Quality (quality)\n18:## 1. WHEN TO USE\n20:### Activation Triggers\n31:### When NOT to Use\n40:### Family Boundary\n52:## 2. SMART ROUTING\n67:## 3. RESOURCES\n69:### Resource Domains\n87:### Resource Loading Levels\n108:### Target-Path Checklist Map\n123:### Comment-Hygiene Enforcement Gates\n153:## 4. HOW IT WORKS\n155:### Quality Gate Workflow\n172:### P0/P1/P2 Author Checks\n183:### Author-Side, Not Review-Side\n187:### Comment Hygiene\n191:### Advisory Evidence-Handoff Envelope\n220:## 5. RULES\n222:### ✅ ALWAYS\n233:### ⛔ NEVER\n246:### ⚠️ ESCALATE IF\n256:## 6. SUCCESS CRITERIA\n270:## 7. INTEGRATION POINTS\n279:## 8. REFERENCES AND RELATED RESOURCES\n281:### Parent And Shared Router\n289:### Quality References And Assets\n310:### Scripts\n321:### Universal Standards\n=== .skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-quality/ROUTER.md\n14:# Code Quality (quality) Router\n21:## 1. OVERVIEW\n31:## 2. INTENT MODEL\n37:## 3. MACHINE ROUTER\n44:### Primary Detection Signal\n64:### Phase Detection\n78:### Thin Prompt-Intent Projection\n83:# Thin prompt-intent router: sk-code-quality owns a single routable checklist. Its\n84:# real routing is target-path-keyed (the checklists --explain names), and its parent\n85:# discoverability is the hub quality signal. This block only made the one asset\n86:# scoreable by the retired deterministic router-replay.\n104:### Call Sequence\n=== .skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-quality/references/code-style-guide.md\n14:# Universal Code Style Guide\n20:## 1. OVERVIEW\n22:### Purpose\n26:### Core Principle\n30:### When to Use\n37:### Key Sources\n45:## 2. NAMING (universal principles)\n47:### Names communicate intent\n53:### Surface-appropriate convention\n57:### Universal anti-patterns\n67:## 3. FILE STRUCTURE (universal principles)\n69:### One thing per file\n74:### Stable import / structure order\n78:### File size, folders and tests\n84:## 4. COMMENTING (universal, with the strict version in the repository's AGENTS.md when present)\n86:### Default to no comments\n95:### Comment density\n99:### Never comment what the code does\n102:# BAD — explains the WHAT\n105:# GOOD — explains the WHY\n106:# Skip the first row because it's the header.\n110:### No commented-out code\n114:### Describe current behavior, not history\n118:### No ephemeral-artifact pointers\n158:### Mark intentional simplifications\n162:### Surface-specific commenting notes\n168:## 5. FORMATTING\n170:### Use the project's formatter\n178:## 6. SURFACE-SPECIFIC POINTERS\n189:## 7. RELATED RESOURCES\n=== .skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-quality/references/quality-config.md\n14:# Repository Quality Config\n16:## 1. OVERVIEW\n22:## 2. DISCOVERY\n41:## 3. SCHEMA\n86:### A Repository Outside the Hub\n99:## Rule overrides\n115:## 4. MERGE RULE\n129:## 5. HOW A CONFIGURED CHECK RUNS\n141:## 6. SUGGESTIONS, NEVER RUN\n158:## 7. ERROR CODES\n173:## 8. TRUST BOUNDARY\n184:## 9. THE DOCTOR\n196:## 10. KNOWN LIMITS\n=== .skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-quality/references/runner-contract.md\n14:# Quality Runner Contract\n20:## 1. OVERVIEW AND CLI\n63:## 2. EXIT CODES AND STATES\n130:## 3. VERDICT SCHEMA\n192:## 4. ENVELOPE GRAMMAR\n218:## 5. RULE IDS\n237:## 6. READER RULES\n251:## 7. CHECKER INPUT FIELDS\n269:## 8. DIST ADAPTER\n===RULE IDS===\n","title":"shell","metadata":{"metadata":{"status":"completed","truncated":false,"exit":0},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"=== .skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-quality/SKILL.md\n10:# Code Quality (quality)\n18:## 1. WHEN TO USE\n20:### Activation Triggers\n31:### When NOT to Use\n40:### Family Boundary\n52:## 2. SMART ROUTING\n67:## 3. RESOURCES\n69:### Resource Domains\n87:### Resource Loading Levels\n108:### Target-Path Checklist Map\n123:### Comment-Hygiene Enforcement Gates\n153:## 4. HOW IT WORKS\n155:### Quality Gate Workflow\n172:### P0/P1/P2 Author Checks\n183:### Author-Side, Not Review-Side\n187:### Comment Hygiene\n191:### Advisory Evidence-Handoff Envelope\n220:## 5. RULES\n222:### ✅ ALWAYS\n233:### ⛔ NEVER\n246:### ⚠️ ESCALATE IF\n256:## 6. SUCCESS CRITERIA\n270:## 7. INTEGRATION POINTS\n279:## 8. REFERENCES AND RELATED RESOURCES\n281:### Parent And Shared Router\n289:### Quality References And Assets\n310:### Scripts\n321:### Universal Standards\n=== .skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-quality/ROUTER.md\n14:# Code Quality (quality) Router\n21:## 1. OVERVIEW\n31:## 2. INTENT MODEL\n37:## 3. MACHINE ROUTER\n44:### Primary Detection Signal\n64:### Phase Detection\n78:### Thin Prompt-Intent Projection\n83:# Thin prompt-intent router: sk-code-quality owns a single routable checklist. Its\n84:# real routing is target-path-keyed (the checklists --explain names), and its parent\n85:# discoverability is the hub quality signal. This block only made the one asset\n86:# scoreable by the retired deterministic router-replay.\n104:### Call Sequence\n=== .skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-quality/references/code-style-guide.md\n14:# Universal Code Style Guide\n20:## 1. OVERVIEW\n22:### Purpose\n26:### Core Principle\n30:### When to Use\n37:### Key Sources\n45:## 2. NAMING (universal principles)\n47:### Names communicate intent\n53:### Surface-appropriate convention\n57:### Universal anti-patterns\n67:## 3. FILE STRUCTURE (universal principles)\n69:### One thing per file\n74:### Stable import / structure order\n78:### File size, folders and tests\n84:## 4. COMMENTING (universal, with the strict version in the repository's AGENTS.md when present)\n86:### Default to no comments\n95:### Comment density\n99:### Never comment what the code does\n102:# BAD — explains the WHAT\n105:# GOOD — explains the WHY\n106:# Skip the first row because it's the header.\n110:### No commented-out code\n114:### Describe current behavior, not history\n118:### No ephemeral-artifact pointers\n158:### Mark intentional simplifications\n162:### Surface-specific commenting notes\n168:## 5. FORMATTING\n170:### Use the project's formatter\n178:## 6. SURFACE-SPECIFIC POINTERS\n189:## 7. RELATED RESOURCES\n=== .skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-quality/references/quality-config.md\n14:# Repository Quality Config\n16:## 1. OVERVIEW\n22:## 2. DISCOVERY\n41:## 3. SCHEMA\n86:### A Repository Outside the Hub\n99:## Rule overrides\n115:## 4. MERGE RULE\n129:## 5. HOW A CONFIGURED CHECK RUNS\n141:## 6. SUGGESTIONS, NEVER RUN\n158:## 7. ERROR CODES\n173:## 8. TRUST BOUNDARY\n184:## 9. THE DOCTOR\n196:## 10. KNOWN LIMITS\n=== .skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-quality/references/runner-contract.md\n14:# Quality Runner Contract\n20:## 1. OVERVIEW AND CLI\n63:## 2. EXIT CODES AND STATES\n130:## 3. VERDICT SCHEMA\n192:## 4. ENVELOPE GRAMMAR\n218:## 5. RULE IDS\n237:## 6. READER RULES\n251:## 7. CHECKER INPUT FIELDS\n269:## 8. DIST ADAPTER\n===RULE IDS===\n"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"command\": \"for f in .skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-quality/SKILL.md .skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-quality/ROUTER.md .skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-quality/references/*.md; do echo \\\"=== $f\\\"; grep -n '^#\\\\{1,3\\\\} ' \\\"$f\\\" | head -30; done; echo \\\"===RULE IDS===\\\"; grep -n '^### CHK-\\\\|^### Q-\\\\|^### [A-Z]*-[A-Z]*-' .skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-quality/references/code-style-guide.md | head -30\"}"},"time":{"start":1793742707175,"end":1793742707610}}}}
{"type":"step_finish","timestamp":1793742708321,"sessionID":"ses_e5ca055c6ffeOZO66b0AKuGGW6","part":{"id":"prt_1a3659e61001XlpTaRHHK5Xsj7","sessionID":"ses_e5ca055c6ffeOZO66b0AKuGGW6","messageID":"msg_1a3658691001SIL7vyKY4iCmRs","type":"step-finish","reason":"tool-calls","snapshot":"79a4d55fd6bc95fdd899449a27a350d3ba4f6bb0","cost":0,"tokens":{"input":1106,"output":282,"reasoning":271,"cache":{"read":167552,"write":0}}}}
{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1793742710005,"sessionID":"ses_e5ca055c6ffeOZO66b0AKuGGW6","part":{"id":"prt_1a365a4f50017Ja821zL6hE659","sessionID":"ses_e5ca055c6ffeOZO66b0AKuGGW6","messageID":"msg_1a3659e660016i6l8yrMmFCqto","type":"step-start","snapshot":"79a4d55fd6bc95fdd899449a27a350d3ba4f6bb0"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1793742715379,"sessionID":"ses_e5ca055c6ffeOZO66b0AKuGGW6","part":{"partID":"prt_1a365b970001ZTbR0p5WBTWtCw","sessionID":"ses_e5ca055c6ffeOZO66b0AKuGGW6","messageID":"msg_1a3659e660016i6l8yrMmFCqto","type":"tool","id":"call_00_3ajxgr174hmy55x0pgnfziu0","tool":"shell","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"sed -n '92,118p' .skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-quality/references/quality-config.md; echo \"===RUNNER longest lines===\"; awk 'length>500 {print NR\": \"length}' .skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-quality/references/runner-contract.md | head -8; echo \"===RUNNER tail===\"; sed -n '260,278p' .skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-quality/references/runner-contract.md; echo \"=== RELATED grep ===\"; grep -n -i 'related resources\\|see also' .skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-quality/references/*.md"},"output":"  \"schemaVersion\": 1,\n  \"checklists\": [{\"file\": \".skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-quality/repository/checklists/house.md\", \"paths\": [\"src/**\"], \"why\": \"house style for the service code\"}],\n  \"commentHygiene\": {\"disableRules\": [\"task\", \"issue\"]}\n}\n```\n\n```markdown\n## Rule overrides\n\n| Rule | Action | Reason |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `floor.documentation` | none | Public functions carry no doc comments here |\n```\n\n- **The checklist.** Each `checklists[]` row names a repository-relative checklist file and the files it applies to, by `paths`, `extensions` or `contentMarkers`. The runner lists it under the `repo-config` seat for every file the row selects.\n- **The overrides.** That file may carry one `## Rule overrides` section, the shared contract in `../../shared/references/universal/rule-overrides.md`. In the contract's precedence a repository checklist ranks above every surface. A row that names a hard-floor rule, such as `floor.input-validation`, is refused, and so is a section with a row whose id no rule carries, such as a misspelled one. A refused section applies none of its rows: a gate run that loads the file ends `CONFIG_INVALID`, exit 3, and names every reason. Each accepted row is recorded in the envelope's `p0_p1_p2_decisions`. Check a file with `node .skilled/skills/sk-code/shared/scripts/check-rule-overrides.cjs <checklist.md>` before the config names it.\n- **The machine-checked hygiene rules.** A `## Rule overrides` row on a `hygiene.*` id changes what the agent and a review apply, not what the checker matches. Switch a checker rule off with `commentHygiene.disableRules`.\n- **The limit.** A row adds a checklist and never a surface label. `--explain` lists the checklist, and its `surfaces` and `surface_skills` never name a surface of the repository's own.\n\n`/doctor:sk-code quality` status shows the result: each checklist with its seat, each override row or the reason a section was refused, and the hygiene rules that apply.\n\n---\n\n## 4. MERGE RULE\n\nThe shipped base layer, `assets/quality-defaults.json`, always loads first, and the repository layer merges over it field by field.\n\n===RUNNER longest lines===\n53: 1296\n55: 780\n103: 971\n113: 1657\n118: 815\n139: 626\n154: 590\n156: 721\n===RUNNER tail===\n| `files[].findings[].line` | Decides the finding's origin |\n| `files[].findings[].rule` | Becomes `hygiene.<rule>` |\n| `files[].findings[].reason`, `excerpt` | Copied to the finding |\n| `disabled_rules` | Present only when a rule is off: the core rule names `--disable-rule` switched off |\n\nThe runner passes the repository config's `commentHygiene` values as the checker's own flags: `--ticket-prefix` per prefix, `--extension EXT=FAMILY` per mapped extension, `--extra-pattern` per pattern and `--disable-rule` per rule `commentHygiene.disableRules` names, before `--`. `quality-config.cjs` builds that list in `hygieneCheckerFlags`. The pre-commit hook and the CI hygiene job take only the `--disable-rule` part, `hygieneDisableFlags`, from `scripts/hygiene-config-flags.cjs`, because the config's additions tighten the author gate alone. The checker refuses a `--disable-rule` id that names no core rule or that `rule-overrides.json` fixes. The verdict's `config.hygiene.disabled` lists each disabled rule as `hygiene.<rule>`, and it is empty when the key is absent. A file `commentHygiene.exclude` matches is left out of the call. Output that does not parse as this document makes the check `ERROR`, so a broken checker never reads as clean. When the checker refuses its arguments, a value the repository config supplied ends the run with `CONFIG_INVALID`, exit 3 and one `config.errors` row naming the field, where a refused flag the runner supplies itself leaves the row's `repair` carrying the checker's message. The runner reads `languages.<name>.extensions`, `shebangs` and `family` from `assets/comment-syntax.json`, the same table the checker reads.\n\n---\n\n## 8. DIST ADAPTER\n\nThe base layer declares the build outputs this repository ships, one `paths` entry per generated path, so the check reads `NOT_APPLICABLE` only when none of them is in scope. A path that matches a declared row is read as a generated file even though the base exclude list holds `**/dist/**`. Only this adapter reads such a path: a configured check skips it, because a built file is output rather than source, and a path the repository's own `exclude` list matches stays excluded, so a repository can keep a build output out of the run. A `generated` row in a repository config carries its own command and runs as a configured check, never through this adapter.\n\n- An edited dist file maps back to the package that built it, so the session hook can warn about a stale build after a dist edit.\n- It runs `python3 -B check-dist-staleness.sh <file>` once per file, the per-file mode, which never rebuilds. The working directory is the root of the hub's repository, where the checker finds its freshness library, and `SPECKIT_DIST_AUTO_REBUILD=0` with `SPECKIT_DIST_READ_ONLY=1` are set. The second switch is the read-only dist path, so the checker returns the same verdict and writes no cache record beside the dist entry it read. The default stays writable, because the session-start self-heal and `--all` rely on that record. The switch also keeps the adapter's promise checkable, because the guard hashes no ignored path and a cache record written beside a dist entry would pass unobserved, as section 1 states.\n- The checker exits 0 on every path, so the adapter reads the banner. A `STALE DIST WARNING:` line is a P1 `dist.stale` finding. Empty output with empty stderr is `RAN_CLEAN`. A `DIST FRESHNESS CHECK ERROR:` line, any other text, any stderr or a non-zero exit is `ERROR`. In the read-only mode a dist check that cannot assess the package is an `ERROR` too, which covers a checker that throws, exits non-zero, prints nothing, prints a payload with no status, or reports the package as unprovisioned.\n- The adapter reads the checker's own switch the way the checker does: `SYSTEM_HOOKS_DISABLED` or `SYSTEM_DIST_FRESHNESS_DISABLED`, from the environment first and then from `HOOK_FLAGS_CONFIG` or the hub's `hooks/hook-flags.env`. A switch that is off reads `SKIPPED_BY_FLAG`, because the checker would print nothing and silence is not evidence.\n- The fixture snapshot covers the folders that hold the declared paths, so a run that creates or rewrites a file beside a dist entry fails a case.\n- One limit stays open. Outside the read-only mode the checker still stays silent when its library prints output it cannot parse, because a session hook has no gate waiting on the verdict.\n=== RELATED grep ===\n.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-quality/references/code-style-guide.md:189:## 7. RELATED RESOURCES\n","title":"shell","metadata":{"metadata":{"status":"completed","truncated":false,"exit":0},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"  \"schemaVersion\": 1,\n  \"checklists\": [{\"file\": \".skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-quality/repository/checklists/house.md\", \"paths\": [\"src/**\"], \"why\": \"house style for the service code\"}],\n  \"commentHygiene\": {\"disableRules\": [\"task\", \"issue\"]}\n}\n```\n\n```markdown\n## Rule overrides\n\n| Rule | Action | Reason |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `floor.documentation` | none | Public functions carry no doc comments here |\n```\n\n- **The checklist.** Each `checklists[]` row names a repository-relative checklist file and the files it applies to, by `paths`, `extensions` or `contentMarkers`. The runner lists it under the `repo-config` seat for every file the row selects.\n- **The overrides.** That file may carry one `## Rule overrides` section, the shared contract in `../../shared/references/universal/rule-overrides.md`. In the contract's precedence a repository checklist ranks above every surface. A row that names a hard-floor rule, such as `floor.input-validation`, is refused, and so is a section with a row whose id no rule carries, such as a misspelled one. A refused section applies none of its rows: a gate run that loads the file ends `CONFIG_INVALID`, exit 3, and names every reason. Each accepted row is recorded in the envelope's `p0_p1_p2_decisions`. Check a file with `node .skilled/skills/sk-code/shared/scripts/check-rule-overrides.cjs <checklist.md>` before the config names it.\n- **The machine-checked hygiene rules.** A `## Rule overrides` row on a `hygiene.*` id changes what the agent and a review apply, not what the checker matches. Switch a checker rule off with `commentHygiene.disableRules`.\n- **The limit.** A row adds a checklist and never a surface label. `--explain` lists the checklist, and its `surfaces` and `surface_skills` never name a surface of the repository's own.\n\n`/doctor:sk-code quality` status shows the result: each checklist with its seat, each override row or the reason a section was refused, and the hygiene rules that apply.\n\n---\n\n## 4. MERGE RULE\n\nThe shipped base layer, `assets/quality-defaults.json`, always loads first, and the repository layer merges over it field by field.\n\n===RUNNER longest lines===\n53: 1296\n55: 780\n103: 971\n113: 1657\n118: 815\n139: 626\n154: 590\n156: 721\n===RUNNER tail===\n| `files[].findings[].line` | Decides the finding's origin |\n| `files[].findings[].rule` | Becomes `hygiene.<rule>` |\n| `files[].findings[].reason`, `excerpt` | Copied to the finding |\n| `disabled_rules` | Present only when a rule is off: the core rule names `--disable-rule` switched off |\n\nThe runner passes the repository config's `commentHygiene` values as the checker's own flags: `--ticket-prefix` per prefix, `--extension EXT=FAMILY` per mapped extension, `--extra-pattern` per pattern and `--disable-rule` per rule `commentHygiene.disableRules` names, before `--`. `quality-config.cjs` builds that list in `hygieneCheckerFlags`. The pre-commit hook and the CI hygiene job take only the `--disable-rule` part, `hygieneDisableFlags`, from `scripts/hygiene-config-flags.cjs`, because the config's additions tighten the author gate alone. The checker refuses a `--disable-rule` id that names no core rule or that `rule-overrides.json` fixes. The verdict's `config.hygiene.disabled` lists each disabled rule as `hygiene.<rule>`, and it is empty when the key is absent. A file `commentHygiene.exclude` matches is left out of the call. Output that does not parse as this document makes the check `ERROR`, so a broken checker never reads as clean. When the checker refuses its arguments, a value the repository config supplied ends the run with `CONFIG_INVALID`, exit 3 and one `config.errors` row naming the field, where a refused flag the runner supplies itself leaves the row's `repair` carrying the checker's message. The runner reads `languages.<name>.extensions`, `shebangs` and `family` from `assets/comment-syntax.json`, the same table the checker reads.\n\n---\n\n## 8. DIST ADAPTER\n\nThe base layer declares the build outputs this repository ships, one `paths` entry per generated path, so the check reads `NOT_APPLICABLE` only when none of them is in scope. A path that matches a declared row is read as a generated file even though the base exclude list holds `**/dist/**`. Only this adapter reads such a path: a configured check skips it, because a built file is output rather than source, and a path the repository's own `exclude` list matches stays excluded, so a repository can keep a build output out of the run. A `generated` row in a repository config carries its own command and runs as a configured check, never through this adapter.\n\n- An edited dist file maps back to the package that built it, so the session hook can warn about a stale build after a dist edit.\n- It runs `python3 -B check-dist-staleness.sh <file>` once per file, the per-file mode, which never rebuilds. The working directory is the root of the hub's repository, where the checker finds its freshness library, and `SPECKIT_DIST_AUTO_REBUILD=0` with `SPECKIT_DIST_READ_ONLY=1` are set. The second switch is the read-only dist path, so the checker returns the same verdict and writes no cache record beside the dist entry it read. The default stays writable, because the session-start self-heal and `--all` rely on that record. The switch also keeps the adapter's promise checkable, because the guard hashes no ignored path and a cache record written beside a dist entry would pass unobserved, as section 1 states.\n- The checker exits 0 on every path, so the adapter reads the banner. A `STALE DIST WARNING:` line is a P1 `dist.stale` finding. Empty output with empty stderr is `RAN_CLEAN`. A `DIST FRESHNESS CHECK ERROR:` line, any other text, any stderr or a non-zero exit is `ERROR`. In the read-only mode a dist check that cannot assess the package is an `ERROR` too, which covers a checker that throws, exits non-zero, prints nothing, prints a payload with no status, or reports the package as unprovisioned.\n- The adapter reads the checker's own switch the way the checker does: `SYSTEM_HOOKS_DISABLED` or `SYSTEM_DIST_FRESHNESS_DISABLED`, from the environment first and then from `HOOK_FLAGS_CONFIG` or the hub's `hooks/hook-flags.env`. A switch that is off reads `SKIPPED_BY_FLAG`, because the checker would print nothing and silence is not evidence.\n- The fixture snapshot covers the folders that hold the declared paths, so a run that creates or rewrites a file beside a dist entry fails a case.\n- One limit stays open. Outside the read-only mode the checker still stays silent when its library prints output it cannot parse, because a session hook has no gate waiting on the verdict.\n=== RELATED grep ===\n.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-quality/references/code-style-guide.md:189:## 7. RELATED RESOURCES\n"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"command\": \"sed -n '92,118p' .skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-quality/references/quality-config.md; echo \\\"===RUNNER longest lines===\\\"; awk 'length>500 {print NR\\\": \\\"length}' .skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-quality/references/runner-contract.md | head -8; echo \\\"===RUNNER tail===\\\"; sed -n '260,278p' .skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-quality/references/runner-contract.md; echo \\\"=== RELATED grep ===\\\"; grep -n -i 'related resources\\\\|see also' .skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-quality/references/*.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1793742715248,"end":1793742715379}}}}
