{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1793742773535,"sessionID":"ses_e5ca055c6ffeOZO66b0AKuGGW6","part":{"id":"prt_1a3669d1f0011l0kjZZVcZZLn4","sessionID":"ses_e5ca055c6ffeOZO66b0AKuGGW6","messageID":"msg_1a366969f001qGPSUTgxPk64sy","type":"step-start","snapshot":"50d5a7dafcd96b9e2fd95a653ba504e9c53d9317"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1793742777347,"sessionID":"ses_e5ca055c6ffeOZO66b0AKuGGW6","part":{"partID":"prt_1a366ab46001tfnrxgWaLiSaJJ","sessionID":"ses_e5ca055c6ffeOZO66b0AKuGGW6","messageID":"msg_1a366969f001qGPSUTgxPk64sy","type":"tool","id":"call_00_y3p7o5o5vnavgjxvkao29yt8","tool":"shell","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"echo \"===SKILL 255-345===\"; sed -n '255,345p' .skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-review/SKILL.md; echo \"===REVIEW-CORE headings===\"; grep -n '^#\\{1,3\\} ' .skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-review/references/review-core.md; echo \"===REVIEW-CORE tail===\"; tail -20 .skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-review/references/review-core.md"},"output":"===SKILL 255-345===\n- P0 blocks merge, P1 must be fixed before merge, and P2 is advisory and never blocks.\n- A dispatcher adds the `CODE-REVIEW` marker line as the first line of the rendered review prompt.\n- Every review ends on exactly one of the three status lines in section 3.\n- The mode writes only its PR-state cache file in the reviewing user's cache directory, never into the reviewed repository.\n\n---\n\n## 5. REFERENCES\n\n### Core References\n\n- [review-core.md](./references/review-core.md) - Shared review doctrine: severity model, evidence rules, precedence, finding schema, and the floor, default lens and `Rule sources:` line.\n- [rule-overrides.md](../shared/references/universal/rule-overrides.md) - The shared `## Rule overrides` format, rule ids, hard floor and precedence that review and quality both read.\n- [review-ux-single-pass.md](./references/review-ux-single-pass.md) - Interactive single-pass review flow, presentation modes, and PR/pre-commit behavior.\n- [quick-reference.md](./references/quick-reference.md) - Lightweight index for routing between shared doctrine and single-pass UX guidance.\n- [pr-state-dedup.md](./references/pr-state-dedup.md) - Content-hash deduplication for unchanged pull-request reviews.\n- [security-checklist.md](./assets/security-checklist.md) - Mandatory security and reliability checks.\n- [code-quality-checklist.md](./assets/code-quality-checklist.md) - Correctness, performance, KISS, and DRY checks.\n- [solid-checklist.md](./assets/solid-checklist.md) - SOLID (SRP/OCP/LSP/ISP/DIP) and architecture assessment prompts.\n- [removal-plan.md](./assets/removal-plan.md) - Safe-now vs deferred removal planning template.\n- [test-quality-checklist.md](./assets/test-quality-checklist.md) - Test quality, coverage, and anti-pattern detection.\n\n### Reference Loading Notes\n\n- Load only the references needed for the selected intents.\n- Routing authority: the hub's `SKILL.md` section 2 names `hub-router.json` as the mode vocabulary and `ROUTER.md` as the leaf-set router. This packet's `INTENT_SIGNALS`, `RESOURCE_MAP` and `DEFAULT_RESOURCES` live in its [`ROUTER.md`](./ROUTER.md) section 3.\n\n---\n\n## 6. SUCCESS CRITERIA\n\n- Review output is findings-first and severity-ordered.\n- `sk-code-review` mode baseline + `sk-code` surface evidence contract is explicit in report context.\n- Security/correctness minimums are always covered.\n- Recommended fixes are actionable and scope-proportional.\n\n---\n\n## 7. INTEGRATION POINTS\n\n- Primary review baseline for the runtime's review agent (`@review`), wherever that runtime keeps its agent definitions.\n- Dispatched as `@review` by `@orchestrate`, which adds the `CODE-REVIEW` marker. The `/speckit:implement` and `/speckit:complete` workflows name `@review` as the agent for their review step and state the marker for a step that dispatches it. The `/create:*` workflows do not dispatch a review.\n- Complements, but does not replace, sibling ownership: the surface packets (`sk-code-skilled`) carry the implement → debug → verify workflow doctrine the acting agent applies to fix accepted findings, and `sk-code-quality` owns author-side gates.\n\n### User-Authored Modes\n\nA repository can add its own mode to the sk-code hub, such as a security surface for its auth code. This is the review side of that mode.\n\n**What a security surface supplies.** It registers as a surface packet (`packetKind: \"surface\"`, `backendKind: \"evidence-base\"`) with its own `surfaceDetection` block, router vocabulary and a tie-break slot ahead of the fallback surface.\nIt supplies read-only evidence: the standards and checklists to load, the markers that place a path inside it, and the verification commands for the code it owns.\nIt never supplies a severity table, a status value or findings of its own.\n\n**What a consuming workflow may rely on.** When a request scores both this mode and the surface, the hub returns a surface bundle with this mode first and the surface after it.\nThat bundled surface is a candidate: it applies to the files the quality runner's resolver places in it.\nThe review then:\n\n- Names the surface's label in `Surface evidence used`.\n- Applies the surface's style, process and verification guidance under the precedence matrix in section 2.\n- Honors the `## Rule overrides` rows of the checklists its `quality` block declares at the specific-surface level.\n- Keeps its own `P0`/`P1`/`P2` findings, report shape and final status line.\n\nA caller parses the report the same way whichever surface was loaded.\n\n**What it may not change.** The Extension Floors in section 4 bind every user mode. A surface may add checks beside a floor and may raise one, and it may never lower, skip or reword one.\n\nThe playbook scenario `CR-028` replays a user security surface through both routing stages.\n\n---\n\n## 8. RELATED RESOURCES\n\nStart with `references/quick-reference.md`, then load task-specific doctrine, assets, or scripts.\n\n### Manual Testing Playbook\n\nManual testing scenarios for the `sk-code-review` mode (of the sk-code family) live in `manual-testing-playbook/manual-testing-playbook.md`, the root index, plus per-feature sub-files under `manual-testing-playbook/<topic>/<scenario>.md`.\nBoth the category folder and the scenario file use bare descriptive slugs, with no numeric prefix.\n\nValidate the root index with the sk-doc document validator in playbook mode:\n`validate_document.py --type playbook manual-testing-playbook/manual-testing-playbook.md`.\nThe playbook path is relative to this packet, and the validator path is wherever sk-doc is installed.\nPlaybook mode applies the playbook rule set.\n\nExecute scenarios in Claude Code or OpenCode sessions for behavioral verification.\n\n---\n\n## 9. PR-STATE EFFICIENCY GATES\n\n### 9.1 M-1: PR-State Content-Hash Dedup\n\n===REVIEW-CORE headings===\n16:# Review Core Doctrine\n24:## 1. OVERVIEW\n32:## 2. SEVERITY DEFINITIONS\n40:### Reach Calibration\n51:### Numeric Severity Calibration\n57:## 3. EVIDENCE REQUIREMENTS\n66:## 4. FINDINGS OUTPUT ORDERING\n75:## 5. BASELINE + SURFACE PRECEDENCE\n91:## 6. BASELINE CHECK FAMILIES\n102:## 7. FINDING SCHEMA\n124:### 2 [P1] Missing authorization check\n138:## 8. FLOOR, DEFAULT LENS AND RULE OVERRIDES\n142:### The Floor\n159:### The Default Lens\n193:### Where the Rules for a File Come From\n199:### Precedence\n213:### Outcomes\n223:### Hygiene Ids and the TODO Rule\n231:### The Rule Sources Line\n246:## 9. THE RESOLVER ALGORITHM\n254:# The quality runner's explain entry is the one resolver for which surfaces, skill\n255:# folders, checklists and rule overrides apply to a file. Review runs it and keeps no\n256:# surface markers of its own. HUB_ROOT is the sk-code folder this mode was loaded from.\n260:# bundled_surfaces holds the router's surface bundle, each with its label and packet.\n261:# known_rule_ids() reads the lens table of review-core.md section 8, the Id column of\n262:# the universal standards and the hygiene checker's rule names. redact_private()\n263:# prints a local-* path segment as its surface label, the envelope's redaction.\n381:# The caller composes the steps in this order, each one feeding the next.\n387:# The precedence the route reports, the mapping of the precedence table in section 8.\n399:## 10. RELATED RESOURCES\n===REVIEW-CORE tail===\n\n# The precedence the route reports, the mapping of the precedence table in section 8.\nprecedence = {\n    \"baseline_minimums\": [\"security\", \"correctness\"],\n    \"fixed\": \"the floor of review-core.md section 8\",\n    \"lens_overrides\": [\"repository\", \"specific_surface\", \"skilled_fallback\", \"default_lens\"],\n    \"surface_overrides\": [\"style\", \"build\", \"test_commands\", \"surface_process\"],\n    \"on_conflict\": \"escalate\",\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## 10. RELATED RESOURCES\n\n- [review-ux-single-pass.md](./review-ux-single-pass.md) - Interactive single-pass report flow and next-step prompts.\n- [quick-reference.md](./quick-reference.md) - Lightweight index across baseline review references.\n- [security-checklist.md](../assets/security-checklist.md) - Security and reliability checks.\n- [code-quality-checklist.md](../assets/code-quality-checklist.md) - Correctness, KISS, DRY, and maintainability checks.\n- [rule-overrides.md](../../shared/references/universal/rule-overrides.md) - The shared override format, ids, hard floor and precedence.\n","title":"shell","metadata":{"metadata":{"status":"completed","truncated":false,"exit":0},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"===SKILL 255-345===\n- P0 blocks merge, P1 must be fixed before merge, and P2 is advisory and never blocks.\n- A dispatcher adds the `CODE-REVIEW` marker line as the first line of the rendered review prompt.\n- Every review ends on exactly one of the three status lines in section 3.\n- The mode writes only its PR-state cache file in the reviewing user's cache directory, never into the reviewed repository.\n\n---\n\n## 5. REFERENCES\n\n### Core References\n\n- [review-core.md](./references/review-core.md) - Shared review doctrine: severity model, evidence rules, precedence, finding schema, and the floor, default lens and `Rule sources:` line.\n- [rule-overrides.md](../shared/references/universal/rule-overrides.md) - The shared `## Rule overrides` format, rule ids, hard floor and precedence that review and quality both read.\n- [review-ux-single-pass.md](./references/review-ux-single-pass.md) - Interactive single-pass review flow, presentation modes, and PR/pre-commit behavior.\n- [quick-reference.md](./references/quick-reference.md) - Lightweight index for routing between shared doctrine and single-pass UX guidance.\n- [pr-state-dedup.md](./references/pr-state-dedup.md) - Content-hash deduplication for unchanged pull-request reviews.\n- [security-checklist.md](./assets/security-checklist.md) - Mandatory security and reliability checks.\n- [code-quality-checklist.md](./assets/code-quality-checklist.md) - Correctness, performance, KISS, and DRY checks.\n- [solid-checklist.md](./assets/solid-checklist.md) - SOLID (SRP/OCP/LSP/ISP/DIP) and architecture assessment prompts.\n- [removal-plan.md](./assets/removal-plan.md) - Safe-now vs deferred removal planning template.\n- [test-quality-checklist.md](./assets/test-quality-checklist.md) - Test quality, coverage, and anti-pattern detection.\n\n### Reference Loading Notes\n\n- Load only the references needed for the selected intents.\n- Routing authority: the hub's `SKILL.md` section 2 names `hub-router.json` as the mode vocabulary and `ROUTER.md` as the leaf-set router. This packet's `INTENT_SIGNALS`, `RESOURCE_MAP` and `DEFAULT_RESOURCES` live in its [`ROUTER.md`](./ROUTER.md) section 3.\n\n---\n\n## 6. SUCCESS CRITERIA\n\n- Review output is findings-first and severity-ordered.\n- `sk-code-review` mode baseline + `sk-code` surface evidence contract is explicit in report context.\n- Security/correctness minimums are always covered.\n- Recommended fixes are actionable and scope-proportional.\n\n---\n\n## 7. INTEGRATION POINTS\n\n- Primary review baseline for the runtime's review agent (`@review`), wherever that runtime keeps its agent definitions.\n- Dispatched as `@review` by `@orchestrate`, which adds the `CODE-REVIEW` marker. The `/speckit:implement` and `/speckit:complete` workflows name `@review` as the agent for their review step and state the marker for a step that dispatches it. The `/create:*` workflows do not dispatch a review.\n- Complements, but does not replace, sibling ownership: the surface packets (`sk-code-skilled`) carry the implement → debug → verify workflow doctrine the acting agent applies to fix accepted findings, and `sk-code-quality` owns author-side gates.\n\n### User-Authored Modes\n\nA repository can add its own mode to the sk-code hub, such as a security surface for its auth code. This is the review side of that mode.\n\n**What a security surface supplies.** It registers as a surface packet (`packetKind: \"surface\"`, `backendKind: \"evidence-base\"`) with its own `surfaceDetection` block, router vocabulary and a tie-break slot ahead of the fallback surface.\nIt supplies read-only evidence: the standards and checklists to load, the markers that place a path inside it, and the verification commands for the code it owns.\nIt never supplies a severity table, a status value or findings of its own.\n\n**What a consuming workflow may rely on.** When a request scores both this mode and the surface, the hub returns a surface bundle with this mode first and the surface after it.\nThat bundled surface is a candidate: it applies to the files the quality runner's resolver places in it.\nThe review then:\n\n- Names the surface's label in `Surface evidence used`.\n- Applies the surface's style, process and verification guidance under the precedence matrix in section 2.\n- Honors the `## Rule overrides` rows of the checklists its `quality` block declares at the specific-surface level.\n- Keeps its own `P0`/`P1`/`P2` findings, report shape and final status line.\n\nA caller parses the report the same way whichever surface was loaded.\n\n**What it may not change.** The Extension Floors in section 4 bind every user mode. A surface may add checks beside a floor and may raise one, and it may never lower, skip or reword one.\n\nThe playbook scenario `CR-028` replays a user security surface through both routing stages.\n\n---\n\n## 8. RELATED RESOURCES\n\nStart with `references/quick-reference.md`, then load task-specific doctrine, assets, or scripts.\n\n### Manual Testing Playbook\n\nManual testing scenarios for the `sk-code-review` mode (of the sk-code family) live in `manual-testing-playbook/manual-testing-playbook.md`, the root index, plus per-feature sub-files under `manual-testing-playbook/<topic>/<scenario>.md`.\nBoth the category folder and the scenario file use bare descriptive slugs, with no numeric prefix.\n\nValidate the root index with the sk-doc document validator in playbook mode:\n`validate_document.py --type playbook manual-testing-playbook/manual-testing-playbook.md`.\nThe playbook path is relative to this packet, and the validator path is wherever sk-doc is installed.\nPlaybook mode applies the playbook rule set.\n\nExecute scenarios in Claude Code or OpenCode sessions for behavioral verification.\n\n---\n\n## 9. PR-STATE EFFICIENCY GATES\n\n### 9.1 M-1: PR-State Content-Hash Dedup\n\n===REVIEW-CORE headings===\n16:# Review Core Doctrine\n24:## 1. OVERVIEW\n32:## 2. SEVERITY DEFINITIONS\n40:### Reach Calibration\n51:### Numeric Severity Calibration\n57:## 3. EVIDENCE REQUIREMENTS\n66:## 4. FINDINGS OUTPUT ORDERING\n75:## 5. BASELINE + SURFACE PRECEDENCE\n91:## 6. BASELINE CHECK FAMILIES\n102:## 7. FINDING SCHEMA\n124:### 2 [P1] Missing authorization check\n138:## 8. FLOOR, DEFAULT LENS AND RULE OVERRIDES\n142:### The Floor\n159:### The Default Lens\n193:### Where the Rules for a File Come From\n199:### Precedence\n213:### Outcomes\n223:### Hygiene Ids and the TODO Rule\n231:### The Rule Sources Line\n246:## 9. THE RESOLVER ALGORITHM\n254:# The quality runner's explain entry is the one resolver for which surfaces, skill\n255:# folders, checklists and rule overrides apply to a file. Review runs it and keeps no\n256:# surface markers of its own. HUB_ROOT is the sk-code folder this mode was loaded from.\n260:# bundled_surfaces holds the router's surface bundle, each with its label and packet.\n261:# known_rule_ids() reads the lens table of review-core.md section 8, the Id column of\n262:# the universal standards and the hygiene checker's rule names. redact_private()\n263:# prints a local-* path segment as its surface label, the envelope's redaction.\n381:# The caller composes the steps in this order, each one feeding the next.\n387:# The precedence the route reports, the mapping of the precedence table in section 8.\n399:## 10. RELATED RESOURCES\n===REVIEW-CORE tail===\n\n# The precedence the route reports, the mapping of the precedence table in section 8.\nprecedence = {\n    \"baseline_minimums\": [\"security\", \"correctness\"],\n    \"fixed\": \"the floor of review-core.md section 8\",\n    \"lens_overrides\": [\"repository\", \"specific_surface\", \"skilled_fallback\", \"default_lens\"],\n    \"surface_overrides\": [\"style\", \"build\", \"test_commands\", \"surface_process\"],\n    \"on_conflict\": \"escalate\",\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## 10. RELATED RESOURCES\n\n- [review-ux-single-pass.md](./review-ux-single-pass.md) - Interactive single-pass report flow and next-step prompts.\n- [quick-reference.md](./quick-reference.md) - Lightweight index across baseline review references.\n- [security-checklist.md](../assets/security-checklist.md) - Security and reliability checks.\n- [code-quality-checklist.md](../assets/code-quality-checklist.md) - Correctness, KISS, DRY, and maintainability checks.\n- [rule-overrides.md](../../shared/references/universal/rule-overrides.md) - The shared override format, ids, hard floor and precedence.\n"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"command\": \"echo \\\"===SKILL 255-345===\\\"; sed -n '255,345p' .skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-review/SKILL.md; echo \\\"===REVIEW-CORE headings===\\\"; grep -n '^#\\\\{1,3\\\\} ' .skilled/skills