{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1793742826000,"sessionID":"ses_e5ca055c6ffeOZO66b0AKuGGW6","part":{"partID":"prt_1a367699c001NvSZ5wAJ80667Y","sessionID":"ses_e5ca055c6ffeOZO66b0AKuGGW6","messageID":"msg_1a3675511001rhDxgFTq8DyGCP","type":"tool","id":"call_00_roao2vd0rg33hgghpssbq10q","tool":"shell","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"for f in .skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/references/rust/style-guide/*.md; do echo \"=== $f\"; sed -n '/## 1. OVERVIEW/,/## 2\\./p' \"$f\" | head -18; done"},"output":"=== .skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/references/rust/style-guide/commenting-and-rustdoc.md\n## 1. OVERVIEW\n\n### Purpose\n\nProvides focused Rust guidance for commenting & rustdoc rules in the OpenCode development environment.\n\n### When to Use\n\n- Implementing or reviewing Rust code covered by this topic\n- Preserving TypeScript interoperability and deterministic behavior\n- Applying the corresponding Rust standards at an implementation boundary\n\n---\n\n## 2. COMMENTING AND RUSTDOC RULES\n=== .skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/references/rust/style-guide/formatting-and-imports.md\n## 1. OVERVIEW\n\n### Purpose\n\nProvides focused Rust guidance for formatting rules & import/module ordering in the OpenCode development environment.\n\n### When to Use\n\n- Implementing or reviewing Rust code covered by this topic\n- Preserving TypeScript interoperability and deterministic behavior\n- Applying the corresponding Rust standards at an implementation boundary\n\n---\n\n## 2. FORMATTING RULES\n=== .skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/references/rust/style-guide/interop-errors-and-parity.md\n## 1. OVERVIEW\n\n### Purpose\n\nProvides focused Rust guidance for rust/typescript interop — errors, parity & related resources in the OpenCode development environment.\n\n### When to Use\n\n- Implementing or reviewing Rust code covered by this topic\n- Preserving TypeScript interoperability and deterministic behavior\n- Applying the corresponding Rust standards at an implementation boundary\n\n---\n\n## 2. ERROR STYLE\n=== .skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/references/rust/style-guide/interop-model.md\n## 1. OVERVIEW\n\n### Purpose\n\nProvides focused Rust guidance for rust/typescript interop — model & boundary types in the OpenCode development environment.\n\n### When to Use\n\n- Implementing or reviewing Rust code covered by this topic\n- Preserving TypeScript interoperability and deterministic behavior\n- Applying the corresponding Rust standards at an implementation boundary\n\n---\n\n## 2. RUST/TYPESCRIPT INTEROP AND COEXISTENCE\n=== .skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/references/rust/style-guide/naming-conventions.md\n## 1. OVERVIEW\n\n### Purpose\n\nProvides focused Rust guidance for naming conventions in the OpenCode development environment.\n\n### When to Use\n\n- Implementing or reviewing Rust code covered by this topic\n- Preserving TypeScript interoperability and deterministic behavior\n- Applying the corresponding Rust standards at an implementation boundary\n\n---\n\n## 2. NAMING CONVENTIONS\n=== .skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/references/rust/style-guide/overview-and-file-header.md\n## 1. OVERVIEW\n\n### Purpose\n\nDefines consistent styling rules for Rust used as napi-rs, WASM/WASI, or sidecar compute modules alongside the existing TypeScript/Node MCP backend.\n\nThe primary requirement is not merely idiomatic Rust. Rust implementations MUST preserve the observable TypeScript contract byte for byte, including six-decimal numeric behavior, stable comparator chains, deterministic identifiers, key order, serialized number spelling, DTO shapes, and JavaScript error behavior.\n\n### When to Use\n\n- Writing new Rust files under an OpenCode workspace\n- Extracting measured compute kernels from TypeScript\n- Implementing napi-rs, WASM/WASI, or sidecar adapters\n- Reviewing Rust naming, formatting, ownership, or module organization\n- Maintaining byte-for-byte parity with a TypeScript oracle\n- Defining Rust DTOs consumed by JavaScript or TypeScript\n\n### Repository Non-Negotiables\n=== .skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/references/rust/style-guide/toolchain-and-project-structure.md\n## 1. OVERVIEW\n\n### Purpose\n\nProvides focused Rust guidance for toolchain, compilation baseline & project structure in the OpenCode development environment.\n\n### When to Use\n\n- Implementing or reviewing Rust code covered by this topic\n- Preserving TypeScript interoperability and deterministic behavior\n- Applying the corresponding Rust standards at an implementation boundary\n\n---\n\n## 2. EDITION, TOOLCHAIN, AND COMPILATION BASELINE\n","title":"shell","metadata":{"metadata":{"status":"completed","truncated":false,"exit":0},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"=== .skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/references/rust/style-guide/commenting-and-rustdoc.md\n## 1. OVERVIEW\n\n### Purpose\n\nProvides focused Rust guidance for commenting & rustdoc rules in the OpenCode development environment.\n\n### When to Use\n\n- Implementing or reviewing Rust code covered by this topic\n- Preserving TypeScript interoperability and deterministic behavior\n- Applying the corresponding Rust standards at an implementation boundary\n\n---\n\n## 2. COMMENTING AND RUSTDOC RULES\n=== .skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/references/rust/style-guide/formatting-and-imports.md\n## 1. OVERVIEW\n\n### Purpose\n\nProvides focused Rust guidance for formatting rules & import/module ordering in the OpenCode development environment.\n\n### When to Use\n\n- Implementing or reviewing Rust code covered by this topic\n- Preserving TypeScript interoperability and deterministic behavior\n- Applying the corresponding Rust standards at an implementation boundary\n\n---\n\n## 2. FORMATTING RULES\n=== .skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/references/rust/style-guide/interop-errors-and-parity.md\n## 1. OVERVIEW\n\n### Purpose\n\nProvides focused Rust guidance for rust/typescript interop — errors, parity & related resources in the OpenCode development environment.\n\n### When to Use\n\n- Implementing or reviewing Rust code covered by this topic\n- Preserving TypeScript interoperability and deterministic behavior\n- Applying the corresponding Rust standards at an implementation boundary\n\n---\n\n## 2. ERROR STYLE\n=== .skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/references/rust/style-guide/interop-model.md\n## 1. OVERVIEW\n\n### Purpose\n\nProvides focused Rust guidance for rust/typescript interop — model & boundary types in the OpenCode development environment.\n\n### When to Use\n\n- Implementing or reviewing Rust code covered by this topic\n- Preserving TypeScript interoperability and deterministic behavior\n- Applying the corresponding Rust standards at an implementation boundary\n\n---\n\n## 2. RUST/TYPESCRIPT INTEROP AND COEXISTENCE\n=== .skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/references/rust/style-guide/naming-conventions.md\n## 1. OVERVIEW\n\n### Purpose\n\nProvides focused Rust guidance for naming conventions in the OpenCode development environment.\n\n### When to Use\n\n- Implementing or reviewing Rust code covered by this topic\n- Preserving TypeScript interoperability and deterministic behavior\n- Applying the corresponding Rust standards at an implementation boundary\n\n---\n\n## 2. NAMING CONVENTIONS\n=== .skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/references/rust/style-guide/overview-and-file-header.md\n## 1. OVERVIEW\n\n### Purpose\n\nDefines consistent styling rules for Rust used as napi-rs, WASM/WASI, or sidecar compute modules alongside the existing TypeScript/Node MCP backend.\n\nThe primary requirement is not merely idiomatic Rust. Rust implementations MUST preserve the observable TypeScript contract byte for byte, including six-decimal numeric behavior, stable comparator chains, deterministic identifiers, key order, serialized number spelling, DTO shapes, and JavaScript error behavior.\n\n### When to Use\n\n- Writing new Rust files under an OpenCode workspace\n- Extracting measured compute kernels from TypeScript\n- Implementing napi-rs, WASM/WASI, or sidecar adapters\n- Reviewing Rust naming, formatting, ownership, or module organization\n- Maintaining byte-for-byte parity with a TypeScript oracle\n- Defining Rust DTOs consumed by JavaScript or TypeScript\n\n### Repository Non-Negotiables\n=== .skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/references/rust/style-guide/toolchain-and-project-structure.md\n## 1. OVERVIEW\n\n### Purpose\n\nProvides focused Rust guidance for toolchain, compilation baseline & project structure in the OpenCode development environment.\n\n### When to Use\n\n- Implementing or reviewing Rust code covered by this topic\n- Preserving TypeScript interoperability and deterministic behavior\n- Applying the corresponding Rust standards at an implementation boundary\n\n---\n\n## 2. EDITION, TOOLCHAIN, AND COMPILATION BASELINE\n"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"command\": \"for f in .skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/references/rust/style-guide/*.md; do echo \\\"=== $f\\\"; sed -n '/## 1. OVERVIEW/,/## 2\\\\./p' \\\"$f\\\" | head -18; done\"}"},"time":{"start":1793742825884,"end":1793742826000}}}}
