{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1793742906863,"sessionID":"ses_e5ca055c6ffeOZO66b0AKuGGW6","part":{"id":"prt_1a368a5ef001myCIig5mVsanRD","sessionID":"ses_e5ca055c6ffeOZO66b0AKuGGW6","messageID":"msg_1a3689f73001XKpkpxt7cRnZnL","type":"step-start","snapshot":"65355fab8e2ed029a449dbdb5699d36e9f346b13"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1793742920900,"sessionID":"ses_e5ca055c6ffeOZO66b0AKuGGW6","part":{"partID":"prt_1a368da58001EIlUYdNJZToqeT","sessionID":"ses_e5ca055c6ffeOZO66b0AKuGGW6","messageID":"msg_1a3689f73001XKpkpxt7cRnZnL","type":"tool","id":"call_00_usa1vt5mi6dflzajhl1b9m4y","tool":"write","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"content":"# Iteration 7: Why the Validator and Guidance Let the Defects Pass\n\n## Focus\n\nTrace, check by check, why `.skilled/skills/sk-doc/scripts/validate_document.py`, its `template-rules.json`, and the sk-create-skill templates let every confirmed defect class pass. Verify the claim about the pre-fix `web-cascade-and-selectors.md` directly.\n\n## Actions Taken\n\n1. Read `validate_document.py` §4-§8: `validate_general_structure`, `validate_h2_headers`, `validate_required_sections`, the `validate_document` orchestration and the path-exclusion logic.\n2. Read the `reference` and `asset` rule blocks in `.skilled/skills/sk-doc/shared/assets/template-rules.json`.\n3. Ran the validator (`python3 -B`, `JEV_FEATURES=0`) on the pre-fix cascade copy and on four current files already confirmed defective.\n4. Re-read the template's intro, duplication and related-placement rules for the guidance half of the gap.\n\n## Findings\n\n1. The validator passes every confirmed defect. The pre-fix `web-cascade-and-selectors.md` returned `\"valid\": true`, `\"total_issues\": 0`, exit 0 under `--type reference`, and four current files with confirmed defects (`runner-contract.md`, `web-performance-and-loading.md`, `rust/style-guide/naming-conventions.md`, `pr-state-dedup.md`) also each returned `valid: true, total_issues: 0`. The claim that the pre-fix file validated VALID with 0 issues is confirmed by direct execution, not inference. [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/sk-doc/scripts/validate_document.py:2008] [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/sk-doc/scripts/validate_document.py:2045]\n\n2. Required sections are checked by name, not by content: `validate_required_sections` collects `## <n>.` headers, normalizes their names, and accepts a match when every required word appears as a substring of the raw heading. `requiredSections` for a reference is only `[\"overview\"]`, so `## 1. OVERVIEW AND CASCADE AND SPECIFICITY` satisfies it even when the section has no overview prose, no Purpose and no When to Use. [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/sk-doc/scripts/validate_document.py:837] [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/sk-doc/scripts/validate_document.py:909] [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/sk-doc/shared/assets/template-rules.json:321]\n\n3. The H2 checker only recognises `^## (\\d+)\\.\\s+`, so an unnumbered H2 and a letter-suffixed H2 are invisible to it: `## 2b. SMART ROUTING` never matches, which is why the ci and skilled skill bodies pass, and `pr-state-dedup.md`'s seven unnumbered H2s are never numbered at all. When a number does match, a non-sequential value is emitted as `severity: warning`, not blocking, so the P10/P8 inversion and the I8 gap cannot fail a run. [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/sk-doc/scripts/validate_document.py:736] [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/sk-doc/scripts/validate_document.py:761] [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/sk-doc/scripts/validate_document.py:2041]\n\n4. Related-section placement is not enforced: `recommendedSections` is read only inside the router branch, so a reference's `related_resources` is never required, never ordered and never checked for position. That is why `pr-state-dedup.md` can end without one, `quality-config.md` can end without one, and `ROUTER.md` can place one before sections 9-11. The rule data even downgrades the asset template's \"must be last\" to `recommendedSections`, so the two normative sources disagree. [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/sk-doc/scripts/validate_document.py:928] [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/sk-doc/shared/assets/template-rules.json:323] [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-skill/assets/skill/skill-asset-template.md:166]\n\n5. No check exists for any content-level defect class. The orchestration runs TOC, H2 headers, general structure, required sections and frontmatter, and nothing else: there is no adjacent-bold-label check, no setext-heading check, no bare-URL or link check, no duplicate-heading or duplicate-paragraph check, no intro-length check and no item-order check. The mechanical scan in this lineage found all of those classes, yet every one is outside the validator's vocabulary. [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/sk-doc/scripts/validate_document.py:2009] [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/sk-doc/scripts/validate_document.py:2016] [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/sk-doc/scripts/validate_document.py:2028]\n\n6. The divider rule can reward the setext layout: `validate_general_structure` requires a `---` line immediately above every numbered ALL-CAPS H2, and it looks only for that line, never for a blank line between the preceding prose and the rule. A text line directly above `---` therefore satisfies the structure rule while rendering as a setext heading. The guidance compounds this: the reference template forbids duplication and fixes the intro to 1-2 sentences, but its own Section 1 duplicates Section 3's when-to-create content, and neither template says a bold-label line needs a blank line after it. [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/sk-doc/scripts/validate_document.py:676] [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/sk-doc/scripts/validate_document.py:690] [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-skill/assets/skill/skill-reference-template.md:86] [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-skill/assets/skill/skill-reference-template.md:182]\n\n## Ruled Out\n\n- \"The validator is disabled for these paths\": the explicit `--type reference` run returned a real verdict, not a skip, and `valid: true` came from `blocking_errors == []`.\n- \"Frontmatter validation catches these files\": all ten frontmatter fields are present in the tested files, so the frontmatter path has nothing to report.\n- \"The citation-drift advisory would catch it\": that advisory checks file-and-line citations and never changes the exit code; it has no URL, adjacency or ordering check.\n\n## Dead Ends\n\n- Looking for a hidden link checker under a different rule id: the `reference` rule block has no link or URL key, and the orchestration calls no link function.\n\n## Edge Cases\n\n- Ambiguous input: \"authoring guidance\" could mean the templates or the sk-create-skill scripts; this iteration traces the templates and the rule data, and leaves the scripts' generation path to iteration 8.\n- Contradictory evidence: the asset template says related resources \"must be last\" while `template-rules.json` lists it as recommended only; the template is the stated baseline, so the rule data is the half that needs correcting.\n- Missing dependencies: none.\n- Partial success: the validator was run with `JEV_FEATURES=0` to keep the citation advisory from making model calls; that switch does not affect the structural checks traced here.\n\n## Sources Consulted\n\n- `.skilled/skills/sk-doc/scripts/validate_document.py:603-938`\n- `.skilled/skills/sk-doc/scripts/validate_document.py:1937-2058`\n- `.skilled/skills/sk-doc/shared/assets/template-rules.json:319-345`\n- `.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-skill/assets/skill/skill-reference-template.md:36-213`\n- `.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-skill/assets/skill/skill-asset-template.md:129-174`\n- pre-fix `web-cascade-and-selectors.md` validator run (valid true, 0 issues)\n- current `runner-contract.md`, `web-performance-and-loading.md`, `rust/style-guide/naming-conventions.md`, `pr-state-dedup.md` validator runs (each valid true, 0 issues)\n\n## Assessment\n\n- New information ratio: 0.90\n- Questions addressed: validator coverage, rule-data enforcement, template guidance, direct pre-fix verification.\n- Questions answered: \"Why do the sk-doc templates, authoring guidance and validate_document.py fail to detect each defect class, traced check by check?\"\n\n## Reflection\n\n- What worked and why: running the validator on the pre-fix copy turned a brief assertion into observed evidence, and running it on four current defective files showed the gap is not historical.\n- What did not work and why: reading the rule names alone would have suggested `recommendedSections` were enforced; only reading the router-only branch showed they are not.\n- What I would do differently: run the validator on a known-bad control before reading its source, so the source read is aimed at the checks that could have caught it.\n\n## Recommended Next Focus\n\nIteration 8: convert the traced gaps into concrete template, guidance and validator changes, with a mechanical-checkability verdict and precision estimate for each defect class.\n","path":"specs/sk-code/018-frontend-upstream-proposals/010-reference-readability-research/research/lineages/deepseek/iterations/iteration-007.md"},"output":"Created file successfully: specs/sk-code/018-frontend-upstream-proposals/010-reference-readability-research/research/lineages/deepseek/iterations/iteration-007.md","title":"write","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Created file successfully: specs/sk-code/018-frontend-upstream-proposals/010-reference-readability-research/research/lineages/deepseek/iterations/iteration-007.md"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"content\": \"# Iteration 7: Why the Validator and Guidance Let the Defects Pass\\n\\n## Focus\\n\\nTrace, check by check, why `.skilled/skills/sk-doc/scripts/validate_document.py`, its `template-rules.json`, and the sk-create-skill templates let every confirmed defect class pass. Verify the claim about the pre-fix `web-cascade-and-selectors.md` directly.\\n\\n## Actions Taken\\n\\n1. Read `validate_document.py` §4-§8: `validate_general_structure`, `validate_h2_headers`, `validate_required_sections`, the `validate_document` orchestration and the path-exclusion logic.\\n2. Read the `reference` and `asset` rule blocks in `.skilled/skills/sk-doc/shared/assets/template-rules.json`.\\n3. Ran the validator (`python3 -B`, `JEV_FEATURES=0`) on the pre-fix cascade copy and on four current files already confirmed defective.\\n4. Re-read the template's intro, duplication and related-placement rules for the guidance half of the gap.\\n\\n## Findings\\n\\n1. The validator passes every confirmed defect. The pre-fix `web-cascade-and-selectors.md` returned `\\\"valid\\\": true`, `\\\"total_issues\\\": 0`, exit 0 under `--type reference`, and four current files with confirmed defects (`runner-contract.md`, `web-performance-and-loading.md`, `rust/style-guide/naming-conventions.md`, `pr-state-dedup.md`) also each returned `valid: true, total_issues: 0`. The claim that the pre-fix file validated VALID with 0 issues is confirmed by direct execution, not inference. [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/sk-doc/scripts/validate_document.py:2008] [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/sk-doc/scripts/validate_document.py:2045]\\n\\n2. Required sections are checked by name, not by content: `validate_required_sections` collects `## <n>.` headers, normalizes their names, and accepts a match when every required word appears as a substring of the raw heading. `requiredSections` for a reference is only `[\\\"overview\\\"]`, so `## 1. OVERVIEW AND CASCADE AND SPECIFICITY` satisfies it even when the section has no overview prose, no Purpose and no When to Use. [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/sk-doc/scripts/validate_document.py:837] [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/sk-doc/scripts/validate_document.py:909] [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/sk-doc/shared/assets/template-rules.json:321]\\n\\n3. The H2 checker only recognises `^## (\\\\d+)\\\\.\\\\s+`, so an unnumbered H2 and a letter-suffixed H2 are invisible to it: `## 2b. SMART ROUTING` never matches, which is why the ci and skilled skill bodies pass, and `pr-state-dedup.md`'s seven unnumbered H2s are never numbered at all. When a number does match, a non-sequential value is emitted as `severity: warning`, not blocking, so the P10/P8 inversion and the I8 gap cannot fail a run. [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/sk-doc/scripts/validate_document.py:736] [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/sk-doc/scripts/validate_document.py:761] [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/sk-doc/scripts/validate_document.py:2041]\\n\\n4. Related-section placement is not enforced: `recommendedSections` is read only inside the router branch, so a reference's `related_resources` is never required, never ordered and never checked for position. That is why `pr-state-dedup.md` can end without one, `quality-config.md` can end without one, and `ROUTER.md` can place one before sections 9-11. The rule data even downgrades the asset template's \\\"must be last\\\" to `recommendedSections`, so the two normative sources disagree. [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/sk-doc/scripts/validate_document.py:928] [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/sk-doc/shared/assets/template-rules.json:323] [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-skill/assets/skill/skill-asset-template.md:166]\\n\\n5. No check exists for any content-level defect class. The orchestration runs TOC, H2 headers, general structure, required sections and frontmatter, and nothing else: there is no adjacent-bold-label check, no setext-heading check, no bare-URL or link check, no duplicate-heading or duplicate-paragraph check, no intro-length check and no item-order check. The mechanical scan in this lineage found all of those classes, yet every one is outside the validator's vocabulary. [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/sk-doc/scripts/validate_document.py:2009] [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/sk-doc/scripts/validate_document.py:2016] [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/sk-doc/scripts/validate_document.py:2028]\\n\\n6. The divider rule can reward the setext layout: `validate_general_structure` requires a `---` line immediately above every numbered ALL-CAPS H2, and it looks only for that line, never for a blank line between the preceding prose and the rule. A text line directly above `---` therefore satisfies the structure rule while rendering as a setext heading. The guidance compounds this: the reference template forbids duplication and fixes the intro to 1-2 sentences, but its own Section 1 duplicates Section 3's when-to-create content, and neither template says a bold-label line needs a blank line after it. [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/sk-doc/scripts/validate_document.py:676] [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/sk-doc/scripts/validate_document.py:690] [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-skill/assets/skill/skill-reference-template.md:86] [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-skill/assets/skill/skill-reference-template.md:182]\\n\\n## Ruled Out\\n\\n- \\\"The validator is disabled for these paths\\\": the explicit `--type reference` run returned a real verdict, not a skip, and `valid: true` came from `blocking_errors == []`.\\n- \\\"Frontmatter validation catches these files\\\": all ten frontmatter fields are present in the tested files, so the frontmatter path has nothing to report.\\n- \\\"The citation-drift advisory would catch it\\\": that advisory checks file-and-line citations and never changes the exit code; it has no URL, adjacency or ordering check.\\n\\n## Dead Ends\\n\\n- Looking for a hidden link checker under a different rule id: the `reference` rule block has no link or URL key, and the orchestration calls no link function.\\n\\n## Edge Cases\\n\\n- Ambiguous input: \\\"authoring guidance\\\" could mean the templates or the sk-create-skill scripts; this iteration traces the templates and the rule data, and leaves the scripts' generation path to iteration 8.\\n- Contradictory evidence: the asset template says related resources \\\"must be last\\\" while `template-rules.json` lists it as recommended only; the template is the stated baseline, so the rule data is the half that needs correcting.\\n- Missing dependencies: none.\\n- Partial success: the validator was run with `JEV_FEATURES=0` to keep the citation advisory from making model calls; that switch does not affect the structural checks traced here.\\n\\n## Sources Consulted\\n\\n- `.skilled/skills/sk-doc/scripts/validate_document.py:603-938`\\n- `.skilled/skills/sk-doc/scripts/validate_document.py:1937-2058`\\n- `.skilled/skills/sk-doc/shared/assets/template-rules.json:319-345`\\n- `.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-skill/assets/skill/skill-reference-template.md:36-213`\\n- `.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-skill/assets/skill/skill-asset-template.md:129-174`\\n- pre-fix `web-cascade-and-selectors.md` validator run (valid true, 0 issues)\\n- current `runner-contract.md`, `web-performance-and-loading.md`, `rust/style-guide/naming-conventions.md`, `pr-state-dedup.md` validator runs (each valid true, 0 issues)\\n\\n## Assessment\\n\\n- New information ratio: 0.90\\n- Questions addressed: validator coverage, rule-data enforcement, template guidance, direct pre-fix verification.\\n- Questions answered: \\\"Why do the sk-doc templates, authoring guidance and validate_document.py fail to detect each defect class, traced check by check?\\\"\\n\\n## Reflection\\n\\n- What worked and why: running the validator on the pre-fix copy turned a brief assertion into observed evidence, and running it on four current defective files showed the gap is not historical.\\n- What did not work and why: reading the rule names alone would have suggested `recommendedSections` were enforced; only reading the router-only branch showed they are not.\\n- What I would do differently: run the validator on a known-bad control before reading its source, so the source read is aimed at the checks that could have caught it.\\n\\n## Recommended Next Focus\\n\\nIteration 8: convert the traced gaps into concrete template, guidance and validator changes, with a mechanical-checkability verdict and precision estimate for each defect class.\\n\", \"path\": \"specs/sk-code/018-frontend-upstream-proposals/010-reference-readability-research/research/lineages/deepseek/iterations/iteration-007.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1793742920280,"end":1793742920900}}}}
